      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0001" n="art0001">
        <p>NO 903 - REGISTERED FOR . TRANSMISSION ABROAD,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0002" n="art0002">
        <p>WIDOW WELCH'S PILLS FOR FEMALE COMPLAINTS AIRS. SMITHERS, Grand-daughter to the Widow AI Welch, continues to prepare this celehrated Me, Hein from the only original and genuine Family Recipe. Its just celebrity for all Female Complaints has obtained for it a most extensive and inc, easing demand. Purchasers should therefore attend to the following CAUTION.—To be careful that what they purchtse is prepared by Mrs. Smithers who is the only person in possession of the ORIGINAL RECIPE. The genuine are wrapped in Blue Paper, and signed on the Outside label by Mrs. Smithers, and the names of the Sole Wholesale Agents, " W. EDWARDS 86 Sox, 157, Queen Victoria Street," formerly of 67. St. Paul's, London, are en- graved on the Government Stamp. Sold in boxes at 2s. 9d each by most Chemista.</p>
        <p>T " PATERSON, FLUSHING, 110 GLAND, THE TURF REGISTER, Published twice daily. Post free. Telegraphic Addresses: PATBBSON. Flushing; BUMPTIOUS. London. x</p>
        <p>The SUBSCRIPTION LIST opened on THURSDAY, the 36th day of July, 1896, and will close on or before THURSDAY. the 6th day of August, 1896. rr H E MERSEY BEDSTEA.D COMPANY, LIMITED. INCORPORATED UNDER IHE COMPANIES' ACTS, 1862 TO 1890.</p>
        <p>Share Capital 40 .14 per cent. Debentures of £lOO each -- 2,9.000 0 0</p>
        <p>The Share Capital is divided into 500 51 rer cent. Preference Shares of £lO each (cumulative and with priority in respect of Capital as well as Dividend) 5,000 0 0 2,000 Ordinary Shares of £lO each</p>
        <p>£25,,, 00 0 0</p>
        <p>(Of the Preference Shares 400 will only bP the first issue, and of the Ordinary Stares 1,200 only will be the first issue). In addition to which 49 Debentures &amp; £1,:0 e,ch, bearing interest at the rate of 4. per centum per annum, will be issued ............ £4,000 0 0</p>
        <p>The debentures will be secured by a first Mortgage of the freehold property of the Company, situate at Latchtor, Warrington, and will be a first floating charge on all the undertakargs and assets of the Company, including its uncalled capital for the lime being, and will be repayable at par on the let day of July, 1906. or at the optton of the Company at an earlier nate, subject to six months' notice, upon payment of a premium of 41 per c•nt. Interest will be payable halt-yearly, on the 2nd day of January and the let day ot July in each year. The Vendors will take 800 Ordinary Shares in part pay- ment of the purchase money. The remainder of the Shares and Debentures are now offered for Puolic Subscription, viz. : £ s. d. 400 51 per cent. Preference Shares of £lO each 4OOO 0 0 400 Ordinary Shares of £lO each, £7 13. paid 3000 0 0 And 40 41 per cent. Debentures of £lOO each 4OOO 0 0</p>
        <p>£ll,O 0 0 0</p>
        <p>Which will be payable as follows, viz.:— Preference Ordinary Deben- Shares. Shares. tures. £ s. d, £ 8. d. £ • per cent. On Application On Allotment 3 0 0 ... 3 0 0 ... 40 Two months after Allotment 2 10 Q ... 2 10 0 ... 25 Three months after Allotment 210 0 ... .., 25</p>
        <p>Total £lO 0 0 _...£7 10 0 ~2103</p>
        <p>The remaining £2 Ns. uncalled upon the Ordinary Shares shall not be called within three it onths after date of the last named pat ment, nor upon less than fourteen days' notice. The Debentures and Preference Shares may be paid up In full on allotment or at the due date of any other instalment, and interest will be allowed from tin date of actual payment.</p>
        <p>DIRECTORS. Joseph Titchett, Estate Agent, Warrington, (Chairman). David Fanshaw, Forge Manager, Warrington. George Clay. Wholes&amp; e Ironmonger. Warrington. James Procter Jones, Bedstead Manufacturer. Warrington. BANRERS.—The Manche•ter and Liverpool District Banking Company Limited, Warrington and Branches (London Office— 75, Cornhill, E C.) SOLICITORS.—rercy Davies and Forshaw. King-street, Warrington. AUDITOR.</p>
        <p>James Bolton, Public Ace untatit.," Sankey-street, Warring- ton. _</p>
        <p>SECRETARY (pro tax.). George Titchett, Accountant, Arplev-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>REGISTERED- OFEICS.</p>
        <p>Hadfie d Works, St. James'-stres-t, -L—atc—hford, Warrington.</p>
        <p>PROS PRCT US . This Company is formed for the purpose of acquiring and working as a going concern,and to extend and further develop the business now carried on at the Hadfield Works .St. James'- street, Latchford, Warrington, by the Mersey Bedstead Com- pany as Metallic Bedstead Manufacturers. The manufacture of Bedsteads was, commenced abrut two years ago with a plant capable of making 16 different patterns and the plant now possessed by the Otimpany has been so en- larged and extended that they are able to make more than 200 different patterns of the newest and best design, of perfect fit and finish, including French Bedsteads of every descrip- tion, Parisian, Italian, Half 1 ester and Fourpist bedsteads suitable for all markets. Cots. Folding Chair Beds, Institu- tion beds, ac., ttc. The demand an! output have increased so largely that the cpresent Foundry accommodation Is very inadequate to the pall upon it and one of the first operations of the new Com- qany will be to erect a new Foundry of the dimensions re- bu.red to meet an output of LOU Bedsteads per week. The usiness is a sound. flourishing and increasing one. The Works are situated in St. James's-st . Latchford, near to Warrington Bridge, and extend back to the riv,r Mersey. where the Company have a wharf frontage of 06 feet 6inches The possession of a wharf for the purposes of unloading pig iron and other materials coming by water, the cost freight and cartage being considerably diminished thereby,will place the Company in a m-ch better position in this respect than the majority of bedstead Manufacturers. The advantageous position of the works, situated as they are within 18 miles of oth Manchestcr and Liverpool, gives them a commanding influence in the3e commercial centres. The cost of the care rings of Bedstc -ds from Warrington to either of the towns named, and t 3 the greater part of Lancashire and Yorkshire, is very mu ,11 less than the carriage from the chief centre of this industry, a point largely appreciated by the buyers in those districts. This hui been, and will continue to be, an import Int factor in the increase if the Company's trade. The facilities offered by the Mancheiter Ship Canal, and the vicinity ol ihe port of Liver. 00l give the Comp iny a gre:t advantage in the export trade to Ireland and other places. The Company have also obtained special Railway Rates for London, and are able to compete with other B: istead Manu- facturers for the trade of the Metropolis. The Company's goods are now well known and in constant demand throughout Lancashire, Yorkshire, the North of Eng- land, Ireland and N. and S. Wales The Company are members of the Bedstead Manufacturers' 'Association. The ch ef reason for the conversion of the business hat a a I Limited Liability Company is to provide :urther capital in or..ter to cope with the increased demands for their goods. The stoves erected by the Comp fly arc constructed on an improved principle, and are probably the best in the trade, the cost ot japanning being reduced ,to a minimum. Mr. S. Percy Silcock. Surveyor and Valuer, of Warrington, has valued the works, and the following is a copy of his Certificate:—</p>
        <p>VALUATION BY PERCY SILCOCK, A.R 1.8 A., Architect and Surveyor, Egypt-street, Warrington. July 10th, 1896. HADFIELD WORKS. ST. JAMES'-STRIGET, WARRING- TON, THE PROPERTY OF THE MERSEY BEDSTEAD CO. Sirs,</p>
        <p>In accordance with your instructions I have made a careful survey of the above Premises in order to report as to their vain-, and am of opinion that the value of the same is as under:— £ _ s._ d,</p>
        <p>iii:es-hOld Land, 2,500 square yards 765 0 0 Buildings and Chimney</p>
        <p>Total value , £3,086 0 0</p>
        <p>Three Thousand and Highty-Six Pounds. I am further of opinion that the value of this Property might be cons.derablv enhanced by the building of a wlearf, there being ample river frontage to accommodat vessels of good tonnage. I am, Gentlemen, yours faithfully, • S. PERCY SILCOCK.</p>
        <p>To the Mersey Bedstead Co., Waariugton. Mr. 'Thomas Sutton, Valuer, of Warrington, has valued the Stock-in-Trade and other assets of the Company, and the following is a copy of his Certificate :</p>
        <p>BALANCE SHEET, LIABILITIES. e. d. £ s. d. To Shareholder's Capital Paid-up 25,000 0 0 „ Assuraace Fund</p>
        <p>„ Total Funds as par first Schedule „ Claims admitted but not paid ~ Sundry Creditors</p>
        <p>£249,521 4 9</p>
        <p>Distriel Superintendent, E. B. JOHNSON. Office: Union Bank Chambers, Market Gate. Warrington.</p>
