      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>ALWAYS USN WIC DOUG-ALLS PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR IT IS 'IRE MST</head>
        <p>ALWAYS USN WIC DOUG-ALLS PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR IT IS 'IRE MST</p>
        <p>PLEASA_NT R.UAGRATTIS.</p>
        <p>SOME mortals talk so much that nobody hears what they say. WE are glad the war is over in China; also glad that it was over in China.</p>
        <p>IN THE SAME WAY.—He "They married in haste." She: "Arid, of course; repented at leisure?" He: "No." She: "No ?" He: "No. They repented the same way. As OTHERS SEE US.—Cobwigger : "You posse.,s that rare gift of being able to tell the moment your clothes begin to look shabby." Merritt: "That's easily acquired. As soon as the bootblacks stop calling Shine, sir?' after me, I know it is time to order a new suit."</p>
        <p>Nor MUCH RlSK.—Examining Physician (for insurance company): "I'm afraid we can't take YOU, sir. You are too great a risk." Applicant (resignedly): "Well, perhaps I am. The fact is that when I get sick I never send for a doctor. I just keep quiet until I get well." Examining Physician: "Eh? Um—we'll take you." Sag: "The man I marry Must be only a little lower than the angels.'l' He (suddenly flop- ping): "Here I am on my knees a little lower than one of them." "HENRY, von look very pale. What's the trouble?" "I was stung to the quick by an adder this afternoon." "How did it happen?' "Why. I dropped in at the bank, and the 'bookkeeper tol. inc my account was overdrawn." Too Hau.su.—Fair Devotee: "I don't see any way to raise our church debt, except to have. a lottery." Minister (shocked) : "That will nevel• have my sanction, madame—never, unless you cali it by some other name." CHAINING A BEATITY.—Jinks : "Everybody pre- dicted that Hardhead would have trouble after Ity married that vain beauty; but she never leaves her home unless he is with her. How does he manage ?” Winks: "He filled the house with mirrors." IT WAS A CAMOMILE.—" I cotdd have laughed outright," said Mrs. Brown, "when we were goin..; round Barnum and Bailey's menagerie on Friday." "Why, what happened?" asked Ml's. - Jones. " Welh, Mrs, Smith called an animal a seraph-0i: course, she meant a giraffe; but.the fun of it was, it wasn't a giraffe at ail—it was a camomile !" .STRANGE that a man cannot see as far forward as he can backward. But it is also lucky for him that he cannot, for if he could see far ahead he would probably be afraid to go on. WHERE SILENCE IS BEST.—" What should. fellow say," asked the young man, anxiously, " when his wife asks him if he would marry a,gaili if she were to die?" "Say nothing, of course," answered the elderly adviser. "If he says he would she'll think he doesn't like her. If he announces his intention of staying single she will have the idea that he is tired of matrimony." A BARE Hors.—" Didn't you 'tell that young man I was out ? " said the young woman. "Oi did, miss," replied Bridget. "But he towld me to come back an' ax yea again, so's- to be sure Oi liiidn't.Misunderstood his name."</p>
        <p>Hi 11.4. E. To.—The Man of the House: "You are not a cousin, or anything like that, of our fair hostess, are you 2" No; nothing of that sort.' .4' Well, did you ever attend such a stupid affair iu ,your life ? ' "Ye-es, a good many. I—l'm he, h : usband, you see, and I have to." As George folded the fair young creature to his heart, a dull, crackling -sound smote his ears. Ah, it's good-bye to those sixpenny cigars in my vest pocket," he said, grimly: " but never mind the expense at a moment like this !" IT is generally the gun that wasn't loaded that does the mischief, and it is oftentimes the man who hasn't anything in him.that hits the mark.</p>
        <p>A HOPELESS CASE.—Visitor: "What is his peeu • liar form of insanity ? " Warder of Insane Asylum: "He thinks he can find the man who stole his umbrella." FILLED THE REQUIREMENT.—Seedy Individual: "How much will you loan me on this suit of clothes ? " Pawnbroker: "Nothing. I advance inoney simply on entire wardrobes." Seedy Individual: "-Well, this is my entire wardrobe." IT is doubtful if there would be any enjoyment for good people in this world if there were no bad people with whose bad actions they could contrast their own goodness. GETTING Evks.—Austere lady: "Madam, your child is annoying me with his attention! I wish you would keep it to yourself." Mother (suppres- sing her anger) : "Oh ! you must excuse him. thinks you are his grandmother, and he is very fond of her." A NovEL FEATURE.—Parke: "Saw a remark- able thing at a séance recently. I was on the committee, and we fastened the medium securely in the cabinet—bound him hand and foot—there was, no collusion of any kind." Clarks : "How can you tell there wasn't? Parke: "Because he couldn't get out."</p>
        <p>Jr was night. The wind swept down the deserted street in fitful gusts, and inky black- ness covered the sky. Crouching behind a tree and concealed within its deep shadow was the form of a man. His eyes gleamed with a baleful light, and he held a gleaming knife, six inches long, that being the accepted length of his breath. For a long time he waited thus, until at last a step was heard, and as the unwary, belated pedestrian approached, the would-be murderer was about to plunge the deadly weapon into his victim's breast, when he suddenly paused, and as the knife dropped from his nerveless hand, he hissed through his clenched teeth: " No ! I'll be hanged if I do!" SOUND AND FURY. An English gentleman travelling in France was very much annoyed at the slowness of the pace, and wishing to get the coachman to increase the speed, he ried to speak to him in French; but he spoke the language very badly, and, as the coachman did not understand, the gentleman lost patience. At last he thought that if he could not make him undestand, he might at least frighten him by using some high-sounding words, and he roared into the ear of the coachman, "Westmoreland, Cumberland, Northumberland, Durham I" This had the desired effect. Cabby immediately whipped up his horses and drove full speed to a place where he could deposit a man whom he believed to be crazy.</p>
        <p>THEN E FIO TIT Bic.—An old. Kentuc 'woman WaS il.rtitil‘ ma witness to tell what sh, knew about a lig,: at her house several night.: before, in which taree or four people were kifi She mounted the stand with evident reluctance au, many misgivings; and when questioned by t-1-• court as to what she knew about thu nutter, sail " jedge, the fnst I knowed about it was who] Bill Sanders called Tom Smith a liar, en' T,)! knocked him down with a stick of wood. One ( Efill's friends then hit Tom with a krtifc. Jones, who was a friend of Tom's, then shot other fellow, en' two more shot him, en' three four others got cut right.smart by somebody. T- naturally caused s:), they ccinnw-acc.:d fitin</p>
        <p>• 1 ne excitement j ct-get t;,,</p>
        <p>--A few weeks ago a pasenger somewhat astoonding- proportions stepped from the train at a small station in the north. He wa; stranger to the village, and the only passengcr t„ alight there. After promenading the whole iang;.il of the poorly-lighted platform, he failed to discove: the way out. Not even a porter could be fowl 1, and the gentleman was almost in despair when ie. noticed a ragged little urchin peeping through t railings, evidently vastly interested in the stranger,: appearance. "I can't get out of this conforninie!l station of yours," said the traveller. "Can you show me the proper way?" The lad critically surveyed his questioner, moved away a yard or tv,-,•; and asked, with a grin—" 'Ave yer tried sideways ? PROVED IL —A journeyman obtained a situatio:i as a pattern-maker in an engineering works. Thy first job given him by the foreman, although only a small piece of work, was difficult, awl not of tl; , same sort which he had been accustomed to durin,; his apprenticeship. After trying hard to de iz right, he at last came to the conclusion that ther;_! were several mistakes, and so decided to hide his -work under the bench and start afresh en another piece of wood. He had got half through with his new piece and already made d mistake, when the foreman came round to inspect, and on seeing the job, .said—" Well, I never saw worse work in in \- life !" T.iie young fellow contended his work i;-;;; not so bad, and went so far as to say that he I Eat seen miorBe done on those very premises. "Wore work done on these premises !' exclaimed the irate foreman. "I will give you a sovereign to it to me." Upon this the young fellow palled from its hiding-place his first pattern, and explained. _</p>
