        <p>S K,IYDOR invaluable forth - F:kin fll-70i ; cools and refreshes it; removes Freckles, Tent, iteoness, lloughness ; cure and soothes irritation. Insect t-nsts, Cutaneous Hru;..tions ; pro.-itmes soft deli ate skin ..nd beAutifully fair complexion. BAties, 2s• 3d• sod 4s. ed. lot \vL MACASSAI; OIL prevent; hair fallin:f off or bee,rniug dry In summer time ; eraqicAtes 6ouri ; also in. Gol en .;olonr, bottles 33. 6.1., 75.. 10s. 61. 01:ONTO whitens the teeth, prevents decay. sweetens the breA h. 23. 9.1. Ask Chemists Druggi,t. Sto-es for liowLetcns' articles, sent post free toy A. ItutiLaNn .Soxs. 20. Hatton Garden, London, on receip of advertised prir'n inpostal ordent</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Few ladies are as conservative in their habits as the Queen, and perhaps no better illustration of this fact exists than the route whereny Her Majesty always travels from Windsor to Gosport, and vice versa. Al- though a great part of the journey is made over the system of the South Western Railway Company, Her Majesty invariably departs from the Great Western ter- minus, knowing that the Royal train will have to be shunted on to the South Western rails at Basingstoke. There is no saving of time,the,officials of two companies are kept on the gui vive and the general traffic materi- ally interfered with, simply on account of this dual con- trol. But the Queen always has come and gone in this fashion, and she will not have the route changed. For several days following the Royal Garden Party, Sir Henry Posonby Fane was overwhelmed with letters from people who had failed to secure an invitation to that particular function. No one has ever exactly as- certained the set orprinciples upon which the invitations are sent out to the " casuals "as opposed to the " re- gular list," but it is still more difficult to conjecture the exact grounds on which an indivival rn y consider that he, or she, has a right to reneive an invitation. In many instances the disappointed ones, p-nned positively abu- sive letters to Sir Henry Ponsonby Fane, mentioning as their reason for so doing, the fact that at one time or another, some member of the family had been in the army, and served in one of our smaller or greater wars.</p>
        <p>The Queen of Denmark, like her daughters, appears to have the gift of perpetual youth, for although she is seventy-eight years of age she is still pretty, and retains her vivacity and charm. There is no'hing shy:. likes better than to be surrounded by her grandchildren and their friends, and she nearly always has a houseful of them. The Queen is devoted to her hushand,and it is a charming sight to watch the illustx ions couple together. He, all attention and gallantry 1 and she, accepting his allegiarce with so pretty and queenly a grace. Considerable comment has been excited ty the very pointed address which the Archbishop gave at the Royal wedding. The source of his text, " Y‘-at see your cal- ling,' reads, according to the revised versicn, "For be- hold your calling, brethren, how that not many wise 'after the flesh, tiot many mighty, not many noble, are called." It was this verse which Lady Helen Colquhoun of LUS3, had in her mind when she thanked God that there was an 'M " in many, the verse N.:TWA read so differently without it. Speaking of the Royal bride and bridegroom reminds me of the fact that the appoint- ment of Comptroller of the Household has been given to Colonel Henry %nays. Be joined the army in 1860 He has had no war service, but has done useful staff work.</p>
        <p>The young Duke of Albany who recently celebrated his birthday is a sturdy, merry little lad of twelve, and a great favourite with the Queen and the rest of the Royal Family. At present ha is at a private 801(3,1, but it is expected he will join his cousin Prince Arthur of Connaught at Eton before long. Prince Arthur is do- ing very well at Eton and is very popular, though his lameness prevents his joining to any extent in the ,ames. When he gets higher in grade, he will, as at present ar- ranged, join the Army Class, a military career w,.,11 be- fitting the son of his father and the grandson of the •• Red Prince."</p>
        <p>_ There is now very little doubt that moire will be fashionable during the autumn and early winter, not only Lyons moire hut all sorts of moire pop!in,and plain satin is likely to continue in favour for some months to come, albeit that a taste for 'Patin damasks in single colours and a combination of colours shaws signs of be. log revived. Velvet, however, is to be placed in the front ranks of dress materials for the coming season.and the fact is mainly owing to so many of the brilliant toilettes prepared by Worth, Doucet, Rauff, and other contl..lors for the Russian Corporation festivities being composed ol this beautiful, and always becoming fabric. Mohair seems destined to have as long a run as that enjoyed by its predecessor erepon, which held its own for several consecutive seasons.</p>
        <p>The very newest shirts are made with removable fronts and detachable collars. This is a delightful an! econmeical mode. As a ground work, grass lawn is admirable because of its neutrality of texture and tone, and to one grass lawn shirt there may be many fronts. Pink, blue, and green muslin are pretty with it, whilst fronts of grass lawn with rows of white embroidery worn with frilled collar and cuffs of soft book muslin, and waistband and neckhow of heliotrope satin rihben, are very quakerish and demure. White book-muslin shirts with wide bishop sleeves and large turn-down collars are also in great favor just now. They are pretty, and cool for seaside and country wear, and possess more- over the admirable quality of never going wrong in the laundry. Authorities on the subject have decided that during the present month and September we are to experience great heat, and taking this item of news into considera- tilts we should do well to discard sombre colors, and sally forth clad in garments of lighter tint. It has been experimentally found that white material absorbs heat to the extent, of say 100 degrees Fahrenheit, and pale straw color 102 degrees, dark yellow abserbs 14e, light ripen 155, Tuikest red 165, dark green 168, light blue 195, and black 208 degrees. Therefore, it is clear that while color does not make much difference as regards the amount of heat given out by the skin, it has a powerful influence in absorbing heat from without. It is welcome news to learn that the short full bit•qued coat has departed, ..for a more trying, and unbecoming I mode, never came into vouge. Ou figures never seemed I really capable of adanting themselves to the perky lit' le exteneion,and we shall hail with joy the longer and more I gracefully defined shaping. Tight fitting coats, are corn- ing in again which i another item of news to be grate- ful,for as there is something so eminently neat,trim,and stylish about these shapely garments ; virtues altogether I,ckine in the loose fronted, short specimens we have lately indulged in. To have one's character told from one's particular style of walking is the very latest diversion of the moment. It appears obstinate people rests the foot flat on the ground, and walk beevily and slowly. The light-hearted volatile person trips lightly and easily, in sympathy with his, or her, rature, while Ely, crafty, people walk with a noiselese even tread, resembling the patter of a cat. The proud individual invariably takes measured ' steps, throwing the head and shoulders well hack, straightening the legs, and turning the toes well out. Turnekin toes generally belong to absent minded peoree. At present women cyclists very seldom come into con- flict with the police, hut those of us who do not cycle very often get tee worst of an encounter with the reck- less " scorebsr," thet flying terror of the road. All sorts of ingenious devices have been suggested for the suppression of these riders who disregard the law, and the common safety of others, hut none have enmemended themselves to the local authorities. Aahtnn-under-Lyne appears to he the first municipality to adopta suggestion on the principle of setting a thief to c4tch a thief. Two of the local police in plain ct thes are to be sent out daily on fast machines, in lisguise, to pursue and cap- ture the unwary offenders. For a time this would seem likely to make matters worse in the neighbourhood, but it will, in the end perhaps, have the desired result. VANILLA CUTS. —Take two strips of good pastry, alike in size and rather thin. Make a custard with the yolks of two eggs, and the white of one, one ounce of Leif, one gill of milk, and flavour with vanilla. When conl spread half the pastry with the custard lay the other half on the top and ice with soft icing and dry in a cool oven. Cu into narrow strips. The icing is made as follows : Strain the juice of a lemon into two tablespoon- fule of water, and boil in an enamelled sancepan; when boiling pour it over one pound of icing sugar. Mix with a wooden spoon till smooth and spread over the "Cuts" while warm.</p>
