      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DRAREST NEL.LIE,</p>
        <p>The prevailing fashion for the hair is to dress it) much higher at the back, a changed influenced, no doubt, by_ theiarge_ruffies and ruehes which. adorn the fashionable capes, or the high bands and bows, rosettes, and turnover collars which finish our gowns. The back arrangement is not .unduly large, but consists chiefly of puff curls, twists, and coils, and nowadays there are 'so many contrivances which materially assist the hairdressing, that to acquire some new and becoming style is by no means a difficult task.</p>
        <p>In describing that very been ;ng 1 arrange- in. ment known as the hand coii tback :of the head•with a 'cu4 fcinge in front, *I inadvertently mentioned the lioveiiden clip in.conricction with it. The .fnime and clip I was referring to is one invented by Mr. S. LiChtenteld (expe,rt ladies wig maker), of 300, 1-Leg:int-street, IAJW10:1. W.</p>
        <p>A really pretty bodice is nia,le of striped grass lawn, al'ul trimmed with white insertion and wide and rrow white lace. Tim elose-fittting sleeves are out into points at the wrist., +O. give fie it slid. egress to the bands, and finished with a dainty lace ruffle, and the swathed waist- band is quite the latest style ; in fact, these bands will be Much in evidence during tire next, few month. They are partictilvly effective in silk and satin, and should be cot on the Cross, lined with muslin, and simply stretc:ied iuLo shape, thus avoiding undue bulkiness.' •</p>
        <p>A becoming b10..4e, one that migit be worn of an evening, might be made of ivory net, and partly covered by an over-bodice of yellow velvet em- broidered in silver. This blouse, is very etiective, and particularly becomes a tall,person. Another blouse, with a low neck, might be made of striped glace silk, this time a dainty primrose tint, with narrow black line. It is the very thing for a travelling outfit, as when one is going for a short tour on the Continent, it is advisable to take one low-necked bodice, or it might be made of silk warp alpaca, with gaugings on the shoulders. This charming blouse can be made in all coloured shots and in black.</p>
        <p>There are some very pret,y, gowns made with lace jackets and ,basques. , might, be made in the form of an Eton Cr)? or cut long with a full Lasque. If you are thiiil,! of bffying-your- self a new gown to grace the AUgii rt garden parties, you can .scarcely do better than purchase a black and white stripe,i silk, or checied silk with a bolero of coarse ivory lace, the sleeves of the silk, the vest of w,hite ehiffon, and a collar band and high corselet belt of ::merald green glace silk. The lining is of soti: biue and green silk, and the trim- ming consists of a yoke of gold and emerald jewels and huge ruches of blue and green ..chiffon. The garment should have a Wattean ,ack, and a loose iront, huge sleeves and a-Me,dicis collar. How we do mix our fa.shies in -these dayF„ Medici, collars, Watteau backs, and Empire f,,,its all appearing on the .seine garment, but pi ovided the effect is pretty what does it matter?</p>
        <p>The mania for wearing hats of very vivid colour- ing, is spreading. Two yew% •girls Isa x -lately wore the brightest of turquoise hlue straw and tulle while a little to the left a Lrh woman attired in cherry-coloured straw trimmed with glae6 ribbon and geraniums, a lovely, hat, tha effect of which was quite spoilt by the collar, saih, and neck-hand which adorned her grey.dress, and which were just the wrong shade of vermilion red. I was much struck by a red-haired girl, who wore a most per- fect little frock of pale green silk under lace- trimmed grass-lawn, with the belt and collar-band of green velvet, which just matched in tone the moss-green of her coarse straw hat, with its high crown tied round . with.. soft green and glowing orange velvet, arid its posies of shaded nasturtiums set high at one side. I -also saw a particularly pretty gown worn by a rather stout lady past her first youth, who looked absolutely charming. The skirt and sleeves and the cleverly folded belt were of black moir6 silk, the sleeves quite tight-fitting with a pretty drapery on the shoulier. The remainder of the 'bodice Was of black net em- broidered in steel, jet, and the finest turquoise. , Your loving , JENNIE</p>
        <p>An Athens teleg,rara states that during a session of the, Cretan Assembly on Saturday a massacre of Christiana by Moslems was only averted by the prep:trations (.0 board 11-.M.S. Hood to land troops if necessary. A Cowes correspondent states that, aithough no cffi• cite, statement has been made of the fact. it is now posi- tively known that the German Emperor will not nuke his usual visit to Cowes this year. The Prince end Prin- cess of Wales and the Duke and Duchess of York will stay on the Royal yacht Osborne during the regatta week, but the proceedings at Osborne Palace;will be en- tirely 'devoid of any festive character,</p>
