      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>REMARKABLE TESTIMONY TO WARNER'S SAFE CURE.</head>
        <p>REMARKABLE TESTIMONY TO WARNER'S SAFE CURE.</p>
        <p>(The " Pet erboremgh Exnress.")</p>
        <p>Most people in Peterborough know Mr. Zachariah Read, of Eastgate. Ho was a fine acive man ; captain, indeed, of one of the City Quoit Clulas, until he was laid low by a long and painful itness. Than for two or three years he was a conspicuous figure in the streets, as, by the aid of tw• sticks, he slowly dragged his ghostly frame along, apparer thy tottering to hi. grave. His recovery seemed impossible. But last week one of our representatives met him walking with a buoyant step down the street. He was as surprised as if someone had returned from the tomb. On recovering from his surprise he congratulated him, and the followirir, conversation took place: - "Why, Headritaid our representative, " you seem to be quite well again !" "Yes, thank God, lam Well attain., and at work and feeling ever so strong," was thelgl4ad reply. "I was afraid at one time you were not going to pull through." "No, I did not think I should, and often I have lain in bed and wished I might go to sleep and never wake again."</p>
        <p>'" You had a lonz bout Af ; xvbat was the matter?" " Well, the doctor treated me first for disease of the kidneys, but it was ahscets on the bladder, caused by the kirk of s horse, that I was suffering from." " Well, lam really surprised to see you about again so well."</p>
        <p>" And what dm you think cured mi e p he asked, put. tine a question to me in turn. "I am sure I doter know. What was it P" " WARNER'S 81FR Cr," was the rePIY- " Indeed I" exclaimed our reoreientative, his interest thnr( ughly arnuFed.</p>
        <p>• "Yes, it. is to that I attribute my recovery. Ido not wish to say anything against Cho doctors, who eid all they could. for me, but 1' would have pawned my clothes to get it for it was the only t ing that gave rue relief. Several have told ene, to write to the proprietars, as they thought it would ho d thing for me. But I have not card to do that, though if I could do anybody eisie good they are welcome to use what I say." It is certainly one of the most r,markable Cases th,t has coma to our knowledge; and though we have hitherto regarded some medicine testimonials with sus- picion, we are able at any rate to. testify the bona fide character of this one, and we publish it in all good faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>,I'fil XAMTNER, %"•41% Pn y AITGTT&lt;II 1. 1P96 I THE HIDDEN HAND;</head>
        <p>,I'fil XAMTNER, %"•41% Pn y AITGTT&lt;II 1. 1P96 I THE HIDDEN HAND;</p>
        <p>CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. BY this. E. D. E. N. SOUTILWORTIL</p>
        <p>CHAPTER LVII. • THE MANIAC S STORY. 4JA. scbeming villain forged the tale That chains me in this dreary cell, My fate unknown, my friends bewail, 0. doctor, haste that fate to tell? 0, haste, my daughter's heart to cheer, Her heart, at once' 'twill grieve and glad To know, tho' chained and captive here, tam not mad ! I am not mad !" M. G. LEWIS.</p>
        <p>There is some advantage in having imagination, since that visionary faculty opens the mental eyes to facts that more practical and duller intelleets could never see. Traverse wss young and romantic, and deeply Interested in the doctor's beautiful eatieet He, therefore, did not yield his full credulity to the tale told by the "relative illustrious" to the old doctor, as to the history and cause of the lady's madness, or even take it for grunted that she was mad. He thought it quite possib'e that the d.stin- guished officer's story might be a wicked fabric'. tion, to conceal a crime, and that the lady's "crazy fancy" naight be the pure truth. And Traverse had beard to what heinous uses private mad-houses were sometimes put by so ae unscrupulous men, who wished to get certain women out of their way, yet who shrank from bloodshed. And he thought it not impossible that this "gentleman so noble, so compassionate, and tender," might be just such a man, and this "fallen angel" such a victim. And he determined to watch and obt=erve. And he further res Jived to treat the interestirg patient with all the studious eelicacy and respect due to a refined and accomplished woman in the fall possession of her faculties. If she were really mad, this demean- our would not hurt her; and if she were not mad, It was the only proper conduct to be observed towards her, as any other must be rqnally cruel and offensive. Her bodily health certainly required the attendance of a physician, and Traverse had, therefore, a fair excuse for his daily visits to her cell. His respectful manners, his grave brow, and his reverential tore in saying— "l hope I find you stronger to-day, Madam," seemed to gratify one who had few sources of pleasure. "I thank you," she would answer, with a softened tone and look, adding "yes," or "no," as the truth might be. One day, after looking at the young physician some time, she suddenly said: "You never forget.! You always address me by my proper title of Madam, and without the touch of irony which others indulge in when humouring' me as they call It! Now, pray exelain to me why In sober earnest, you give me this title ?" "Because, Madam, I have heard you lay claim to that title, and I think you, yourself, of all the world, have the best right to know how you should be addressed," said Traverse, respectfully. "But my next door neighbour asserts that she is a queen; she insists upon being called 'your majesty.' Has she, then, the best right to know how she should he addressed 7" " Ales ! no, Madam; and I am pained that you should do yourielf the great wrong to draw such comparisons." "Why ? Am not I and the ' queen ' inmates of the same ward of incurables, in the same lunatic asylum?" "Ys, but not with equal justice of canes,. The qw.eu ' Isa hopelessly deranged, but happy lunatic. You, Madam, are a lady who has retained the possession of your faculties amid circumstances and surroundings that must have overwhelmed the teason of a weaker mind," The lady looked at him in wonder and almost in</p>
        <p>Joy. " Ab, it was not the strength of my mind, it was the strength of the Almighty upon whom my mind was stayed for time and for eteroity, that has saved my reason in all tee many years! But how did you know that I was not mad? How do you knew that this is anything more than a lucid interval of longer duration that usual 9" she asked. "Madera, yon will forgive me for having looked at you so closely and watched you so constantly, but I am your physicia e you know-" "I have nothieg to torgive and much to thank you for, young man. You have an honcst, truthful, frank young face! the only one such that I have seen in eighteen years of sorrow? But why, then, did you not believe the doctor? Wny did you not take the fact of an insanity upon trust, 8 others did ?" she asked, fixing her glorious dark eyes inquiringly upon his face. "Madam, from the first moment in which I saw you, I disbelieved the story of your insaDity, and mentioned my doubt,* to Dactor St. Jean-- -" Who ridiculed your doubts, of course. I can readily .telieve that he did. Dector St. Jean is not a very bad man; but he is a charlatan and a dullard; he received the story of my reported insanity as he received me, as an advantage to his Institution; and he never g;:ve himself the un- profitable trounle to investigate the circumstances. I told him the truth about myself as calmly as I now speak to yeti; but somebody else had told him that this truth was the fiction of a deranged imagination, and he found it more convenient and profitable to believe somebody else I But, again, I ask you, why were not you also, so discreetly obtux ?" "Madam," said Traverse, blushing ingenuously, "I hope you will forgive me for saying that it is impossible any one could see you without becoming deeply interested in your fate. Your face, Madam, speaks equally of profound sorrows and of saintly resignation. I saw no sign of madness there I lii the calm depths of those sad eyes, lady, I know that the fires of imanity never could have burned. Pardon me that I looked at you so closely ; I was your physician, and was deeply anxious concerning my patient." "I thank you; may the Lord biers you; perhaps He has sent you here for my relief; for you are right, young friend; you are altogether right; I have been wild with grief, frantic with despair, bat never fer one hour in the whole course of my life have I been insane."</p>
        <p>"I believe you, Madam, on my sacred honour, I do " said Traverse, fervently. "And yet you could get no one about this place to believe you I They have taken any brother-in- law's false story, endorsed as it is by the doctor-pro- prietor, for granted. And just so long as I persist In telling any true story. they will consider me a monomanatc, aed so often 28 the thought of my many wrengs and sorrows, combines with the ner- vous irritability to which every woman is occa- sionally subject, and makes me rave with im- patiance and excitement, they• will report me a dangerous lunatic, subject to periodical attacks of violent frenzy; but, young man, even at my worst I am no more mad than any other woman, wild with grief and hysterical through nervous irritation, might at any tame become without having her sanity called in question." "I am !in e •tbat you are not, nor ever could have been, Madam. The nervous excitement of which you speak is entirely within the control of medicine, which mania prepar is not You will use the means that I prescrilata and your continued calmness will go far to convince even these dullards that they have been wrong." "I will do everything you recommend; indeed for some weeks before you came, I had put a constraint upon myself and forced myself to be very still; but the effect of that was, that acting upon their theory they said that I was sinking into the last or melaacholy-mad ' state of mania, and they put me in hero with the incurables." "Lady," said Traverse, respectfully taking her hand, "now that I am acquainted in some slight degree with the story of your heavy wrongs, do not suppose that I will ever leave you, until I see you restored to your friends." "Friends ! ah I young man, do you really sup- pose, that if I had friends, I should have been left thus long unsought? I have no friends, Doctor Rocks, except yourself' newly sent me by the Lord nor any relatives except a young dau later whom I have seen but twice in my life I—once upon the dreadful night when she was born and torn away from my sight, and once about two years ago, when she must have been sixteen years of age. My little daughter does not know that she has a poor mother living, and I have no friends upon earth but you, whom the Lord has sent." "And not in vain!" said Traverse, fervently; "though you have no other friends, yet you have the law to protect you. It will make your case known, and restore you to liberty! Then lady, listen I I have a good mother to whom Suffering has taught sympathy with the unfortunate; and I have a lovely betrothed bride, whom you will for- give her lover for thinking an angel in woman's form • and we have a beautiful ho.ne Among the hills a Virginia; and you shall add to oar eappiness by living with us." The lady looked at Traverse Racke with astonish- =ant and incredulity.</p>
        <p>___"ll.oy:' she said. do you know what YOU are</p>
        <p>prond.:;,, asAume thr wLiole burden el tne srlppor ot a ,t,,eless vimil‘in for her whole life! ti kt.D would your mother or promised wife say to such a propohition " 4. An you do not know my dear mother, nor my Clara, no, not even me. I tell you the truth when I say that your coming among us would make us happier. Oh, Madam, I myself owe so much to the Lord, and to his instruments, the benevolent of this world, for all that has been done for me, I seize with gratitude the chance to serve in my turn any of Ws suffering children! Pray believe me l'' "I do! I do, Doctor Rocke I I sae that life has not deprived you of a generous, youthful enthusiasm." said the lady, with the tears welling up into her glorious black eyes. After a little, with a smile, she held out her hand to him, saying: "Young friend, if you should succeed in freeing ma from this prison, and establish my sanity before a court of justice, I and my daughter will come Into immediate posession of one of the largest estates in your native Virginia! Sit down, Doctor Rocke, while I tell you my true story, and much, very much more of it than I have ever confided to any human being." "Lacy, I am very impatient to hear your history, but I am your physician, and must first consider your health. You have 1 een sufficiently excited for one day; it, is late; take your tea and retire early to bed. To-morrow morning, after I have visited the wards and you have taken your breakfast, I will come, and you shall tell me the story of your life."</p>
        <p>"I will do whatever you think best," said the lady. Traverse lifted her hand to his lips, bowed, and retreated from the cell.</p>
        <p>That same night Traverse wrote to his friend Herbert Greyson in Mexico, and to his mother and Clara, describing his interesting patient, though as yet he could tell but little of her, not even in fact her real name, but promising fuller particulars next time, and declaring his intention of bringing her home for the present to their housc</p>
        <p>CH AFTER END OF THE LADY'S STORY. ..)f the present naught is bright, But in the coming years I see A brilliant and a cheerful light, Which burns befoie thee constantly."</p>
        <p>-W. D. GALLAGHER.</p>
        <p>At the appointed hour the next morn Dug, Tra- verse Roche repaired to the cell of his mysterious patient. He was pleased to find her up and dressed with more than usual care and taste, and looking, upon the whole, much better in health and spirits than upon the preceding day. " Ah, my young hero, is it you? You see that I am ready for you," she said, holding out her hand. "You are looking very well, this morning," srAid Traverse, smiling. "Yes, hope is a fine tonic, Doctor Roche." She was seated by the same window at which Traverse had first seen her, and she now beckoned the youeg doctor to come and take a seat near her. "My story is almost as melo-dramatic as a modern romance, Doctor Roche," she said. Traverse bowed gravely and waited. "My father was a French patriot, who suffered death in the cause of liberty, when I, his only child, was but fourteen years of age. Alf mother, broken- hearted by his loss, followed him within a few months. I was left an orphan and penniless, for our estate was cor•fiscated."</p>
        <p>"Ala. your sorrows came early and heavily Indeed," said Traverse. "Yes; well I a former servant of my father, held In bumble situation of porter, on the ground floor of a house, the several floors of which were let out to different lodgers. This poor man and his wife gave me a temporary home with themselves. Among the lodgers of the house there was a young V:rginian gentleman of fortune, travelling for plea- sure and improvement, whose name was Mr. Eugene N</p>
        <p>LE Nom 1" cried Traverse, with a violent start. Yos 1 %wit is the matter 7" "In is a familiar Virginian name, Madam, that is all ;_ praY go on."</p>
        <p>coullitgivuaottede. every "'Nil.. N,Jir was as -good and kind as he was yHetiumse:ht: stoppedstop to at; the porter' R room to take or to leave his key. There he heard of the poor little orphan of the guillotine, who had no friend in the world but her father's old servant. He pitied me, and after many co:versa- wwiisteh and Cultivated. Mons_ with Fatber,and Madame Cliquot, he assumed the position of guardian to me, and placed me at one of the best E•chools in Paris. He lingered in the city and came to see me very often • but always saw me in the presence of Madam, the directress. I ciung to him with the affection for a father or an elder brother, and I knew he loved ma with the tenkler, protecting affection that ha would have wlven a younger i•ister, had he possessed one. Ah fl cior Roeke, tell me ly;sides yourself, are there many others in your State like km?" " I knew .hut one such; but go on, dear madam." "When 1 had been to school some months, he rattle . to we one day scarcely able to conceal his woe 1 He told me that his father was ill and that he should have to sail in the fiNt packet from Havre, end that in fact he had then come to take leave ot me. I was wild with grief, not only upon his rit bat upon my OVC4, at the prospect of losing Net, my only friend! I was but a child, and a F. eme ;mild to .boot. I knew nothing of the world; I i trded this noble gentleman, who was so much 6uperlor, in years as in everything else, as a cr elder brother, so in an agony C jf kt, I threw ' y ,elf into his arms, sobbing and weepiug Mtterly. act iruploring him not to break try heart by leavin me! It was in vain madame tbe DPtetiess exe:utineo and expostulated at these or!,ties. I on sure I did not hear a word unie he spoke, Yuctiag me out of his arms, he</p>
        <p>" • I must go my chili, duty calls me.' "'T iiwa take me with you—take your poor little one with you, and do not pall her out of your warm, good he Art. or she will wither and die like a poor fl,wer torn up by the roots!" I cried, between Ley sobs and tears. "He drew ins back to his bosom and whis- pered: e . riiere is but one way in which I can take you with me, my child. Will you be my wife, little 0, po ode ?" e aapito/a I' cried Traverse, with another great ate)'. i yas ; why? what is the matter now 7,' • ' "Why, it is such an odd name, that is all. Pray proceed, Madam." "We were married the same day, and sailed the third morning thereat tec from Hevre for the Unitei B:atesi, where we arriv,:d, abs I only to find the noble gentleman, my Eugene's father, laid in his grave. After Mr. L. Noir'e natural grief was over, we settled down peace.ably to our country life at the Hi:iden Ifotts,-- ' " The Hidden /Luse!" again ex laimecl Traverse 11,,c,Ze. " Yes ; that is another odd name, isn't it ? Well, I was very happy. At ti,st, when I understood my reel position, I had been afreid that my husband had married me only from compassion; but he soon proved to me that his love was ets high, as pure, and as noble as himself. I was very happy! But one day, in the midst of my eaculiant joy, a thunderbolt fell and shattered my pew° to dest.rection for-ever! On, Dr. Rock e, my husband was murdered by some unknown hand in his own woods, in open day. I ' cannot talk of this 1" cried the widow, breaking down, overwhelmed with the rush of terrible recol- lections. Traverse poured oat a glass of water, and handed it to her. She drank it, made an effort at self-control, and resumed: "Thus, scarcely sixteen years of ages I . was a widow, helpless, penniless, and entirely depen lent upon my brother-in-law, Colonel Gabriel Le or;N for by the terms of their father's will. if Eugene died without issue, the wriote property tle,,Ctuatit,l to his younger brother, Gabriel. To speak the truth, Colonel Le Noir was exceedingly kind to me after i my awful bereavement, until a circumstance was I discovered that changed all our relations. It was Itwo months after my husband's death, that I discovered with mingled emotions of joy and sor- Irow, that Heaven had certainly destined me to become a mother. I kept my cherished secret to myself as long as it was possible, but it could not Indeed be long 'concealed from the household. I believe that iny brother-in-law was the first to suspect it. He called me into his study one day. I obeyed like a child. . And there he rudely qua- tioned me upon the subjeet of my sacred mother- mystery. He learned the truth, more from my silence than from my replies, for I could not answer him." "The brute! the miserable hound 1" ejaculated Traverse. "Oh, Dr. Rocke, I could not tell you the avalancile of abuse, insult, and invective that he hui led upon my defenceless head. He accused me of more crimes than I had ever heard talk of. He told me that my condition was an. impossible one unless I had been false to the memory of his brother ; that I had dishonoured his name, disgraced his house, and brought myself to shame; that I should leeee the roo_f_e_ leave the neighbourhood.. and die as i</p>
        <p>f deserved to die, in a ditch 1 1 made no repty. I was crushed into silence under the weight of his reproaches." "The caitiff 1 the poltroon I Ah, poor stranger, why did you not leave the house at once and throw yourself upon the protection of the minister of your parish, or some other kind neighbour?" "Alas, I was a child, a widow, and a foreigner, all in one. I did not know your land, or your laws, or your people. I was not hopeful or confident, I had suffered so cruelly, and I was overwhelmed by his abuse.' "Bat did you not know, dear lady, that all his rage was aroused only by the fact that the birth of your child would disinherit him ? " " Ah. no. I was not aware, at that time, that Gabriel Le Noir was a villain. I thought his anger honest, though unjust ; and I was as ignorant as a child —I had no mother nor matronly friend to instruct me. I knew that I had broken no command of God or man—that I had been a faithful wife, but when Gabriel Le Noir accused me with such bitter earnest- nese. I feared that some strange departure from the usual course of nature had occurred for my destruc- tion. And I was overwhelmed by mortification, terror, and despair." "Ah, the villain 1" exclaimed Traverse,"between his teeth. "He told me at last, that, to save the memory of his dead brother he would hide my dishonour; and he ordered me to seclude myself from the sight of all persons. I obeyed -him like a slave, grateful even for the shelter of his roof." "A roof that was.your own as he very well knew. And he knew ala 6, the caitiff, that if the circum- stance became known, the whole State would have protected you in your rights, and ejected him like a cur." _ _</p>
        <p>"Nay, even in that case no harm should have reached him on my account. He was my husband's brother." "And worst enemy. But proceed, dear lady." "Well, I secluded myself as he commanded. For four months I never left the attic to which he had ordered me to retreat. At the end of that time I became the mother of twins—a boy and a girl. The boy only opened his eyes on this world to close them again directly. The girl was living and healthy. The old nurse who attended me had an honest and compassionate face; I persuaded her to secrete and save the living child, and to present the dead babe to Colonel La Noir as the only one; for the suspicions that had never been awakene for myself were alarmed for my child. I in- stinctively felt that he would have destroyed it." "The mother's instinct is like inspiration," said Traverse. "It may be so! Well, the old woman pitied me and did as I desired. She took the dead child to Colonel Le Noir, who carried it off, and afterwards buried it as the sole heir of his elder brother. The old woman carried off my living child and my wedding ring, concealed under her ample shawl. Anxiety for the fate of my child caused me to do what nothing else on earth would have tempted me to do—to creep about the halls and passages on tiptoe and under cover of the night, and listen at keyholes," said the lady, blushing deeply at the recollection. "You—you were perfectly right, Mrs. Le Noir! In a den of robbers, where your life and honour were always at stake, you could have done no other- wise 1" exclaimed Traverse, warmly. "I learned by this means that my poor old nurse had paid with her liberty for her kindness to me. She had been abducted and forced from her native country together with a child found in her possession, which they evidently suspected and I knew to be mine. Oh heaven! the agony then of thinking of what might be her unfortunate fate,—worse than death, perhaps! I felt I had only succeeded in saving her life ;—doubtfu, good 1" Here Mrs. Le Noir passed in thought for a few moments and then resumed. " In the memory of a long, dreary and hopeless Imprisonment, my recollection of my residence in that house In the same manner in which I gained all my information, I learned that it was reported In the neighbourhood that I had gone mad with grief for the loss of my husband and that I was an inmate of a mad-house in the north! It was altogther false! I never left the Hidden House in all those years until about two years ago. My life there was dreary beyond all conception. I was forbidden to go out or to appear at a window! I had the whole attic, containing some eight or ten rooms, to rove over, but I was forbidden to descend. An ill-looking woman, called Dorcas Knight, between whom and the elder Le Noir there seemed to have been some sinful bond, was engaged ostensibly as my attendant; but really as my gaoler. Nevertheless, when the sense of confine- menu grew- intolerable I sometimes eluded her vig- ilanee and wandered about the house at night." **Thence no doubt," said Traverse, "giving rise to the report that the house was haunted 1" Mrs. Le Noir smiled, saying: "I believe the Le Noire secretly encouraged that report! tell you why. They gave me a chamber lamp enclosed in an intense blue shade, that cast a strange unearthly light around. Their ostensible reason was to ensure my safety from fire. Their real reason was that this light might be seen from without in what was reputed to be an uninhabited portion of the house, and give colour to its bad re- putation among the ignorant of being haunted!" "So much for the origin of one authenticated ghost story," said Traverse. "Yes! And there was still more circumstantial evidence to support this ghostly reputation of the house. As the years passed I had, even in my con- fined state, gathered knowledge in one way and another—picking up stray books and hearing stray conversation; and so, in the end I learned how gross a deception and how great a wrong had been practised upon me. I was not wise or canning. I betrayed constantly to my attendant my knowledge hese tW.ess. In consequence of which my con- finement. 484' still more restricted." "Yes, they were afraid of you, and fear is always the mother of cruelty," said Traverse. "Well, from the time that I became enlightened as to my rot' position, all ray faculties were upon the alert to find means of escaping and making my condition known to the authorities. One night they had a guest, Colonel Eglen, of the army. Old Dorcas had bar hands full, and her prisoner. My door was left unlocked. So, long after Colonel Bglen had retired to rest, and when all the household were buried in repose, I left my attic and crept down to the chamber of the guest, with no other purpose than to make known my wrongs and appeal to his compassion. I entered his chamber, approached his bed to speak to him, when this hero of a hunored fields started up in a panic, and at the sight of the pale woman who drew his curtains in the dead of night, he shrieked, violently rang his hell, and fainted prone away 1" "Ha I ha 1 ha! he could brave an army, or march into a cannon's mouth, easier than meet a supposed denizen of another world! Well, Doctor Johnson believed in ghosts," laughed Traverse. , "It remained for me to retreat as fast as possible to my room, to avoid the Le Noits, who were hurry- ing with headlong speed to the guest-chamber. They knew, of course, that I was the ghost, although they elf -wed to treat their visitor's story as a dream. A. ter that my confinement was so strict that for ye-trs I had no opportunity of leaving my attic. At last the strict espionage was relexe,l. Sometimes my door would be left unlocked. Upon One such occasion, in creeping about in the dark, I learned, by overbearing a conversation between Le Noir and his housekeeper, that my long lost daughter, Capitols, had been found, and was living at Hurricane Hall! This was enough to comfort me for years. About three years ago, the surveillance ever me was so modified that I was left again to roam about the upper rooms of the house at will, until I learned that they had a new inmate, young Clara Day, a ward of Le Noir! Oh, how I longed to warn that child to fly! But I could not! Alas, again I was restricted to myown room, lest I should be seen by her! But again, upon one occasion, old Dorcas forgot to lock my door at niAlt. I stole forth from my room and learned that a young girl, caught out in the storm, was to stay all night at the Hidden House. Young girls were not plentiful in that neighbourhood I knew! Besides, some secret instinct told me that this was my daughter. I knew that she would sleep in the chamber under mine, because that was the only habitable guest-room in the whole house. In the dead of nieht I left my room and went below and entered the chamber of the young girl. I went first to the toilet table to see if among her little girlish ornonents, I could find any clue to her identity. I found it in a plain, gold rin7 --the same that I entrusted to the old nurse. Some\-nger. strange nkfi impulse caused me to slip the ringpon in Then I went to the bed and threw aside the curtains to anze noon the sleeper. My girl my own girl! With what strange sensations I first looked upon her face I Her eyes were open and furad upon mine in a panic of terror. I stooped to press my ups to hers and she closed her eyes in mortal fear. I carried nothing but terror with me I I withdrew from the room and went back, sobbing, to my ch:•mber. MY poor girl, next morning, uncon- seiouslv, betrayed her mother.. It had nearly cost nif• Ivy life. 4' When.the Le Noirs came home, the first night of thew arrival they entered my room, seized Lae in my bedaled_dragged me shrieking from it 1"</p>
