        <p>up and pacing the rarrow Unfit, of " Listen ! They scon stopped ho'' my breath at orce ! I lost conscion , or a and when I awoke I found myself Liz 4 • clrr,il4:3 rattling over a mountain rod. throur,'l. Late the next morning we reached an eitionabite! country-house, where I was again irra.rned, charge of an old dumb woman whom la. Noir calle“ Mrs. Raven. This I afterwards undettood to be Willow Heights, the property of the orohan heicess, Clara Day. And here, also, for the term. of my Ft4,l' thepresence of the unknown in mate got the housetue reputation of being haunted. The old dumb woiefiral VMS a shade kinder to me than Domes li/iight been; but I did not stay in her charge very loeP One night the Le Noire came in hot haste. The young heiress bad been delivered from their chsr4de by a decree of the Orphans' Court, and they b 6,1 to give up her house. I was drugged and hurrie" away. Some narcotic snadative must have hal Insinuated into my food, for I was in a state fe semi-insensibility and mild delirium during t' whole course of a long journey by land and sl which passed to me like a 'earn, and at the which I found myself here. No doubt, from 036 excessive use of narcotics, there was sometbil wild and stupid in my manner and appearance tbaj justified the charge of madness. And when I foll,° t that I was a ,prisoner, in a lunatic asylum, far, 13i away from the neighbourhood where, at least. had once been known, I gave way to Cle grief that further confirmed the story of 1:13J madness. I have been here two .years, oar clonally giving way to outbursts of wild cleY pair, that the doctor calls frenzy. I vileiso sinking into an apathy when one day I opo the little Bible that lay upon the table ° my cell. I fixed upon the last chapters in 08 gospel of John. That narrative of meek patiesel and divine love! It did for me what no powe, under that of God could have done. It saved 0' it saved me from madness! it saved me froll despair There is a time for the seetnad birth every soul; that time had come for me. From tb; hour, this book had been ray constant compani°' and comfort. I have learned from its pages II little it matters how or where this fleeting, snort life is passed, so that it answers its purpose of paring the soul for another. I have learned pada'', with sinners, forgiveness of enemies, and coefidev; In God. In a word, I trust I have learned the of salvation, and in that have learned everytlahlre Your coming, and your words, young friend. 1010 stirred within my heart the desire to be free, s. mingle again on equal terms with my fellow-beia, and, above all, to find and to embrace my chuti But not wildly anxious am I even for these eartltdd blessings. These, as well as all things else, I din; to leave to the Lord, praying that His will mg mine! Young friend, my story is told." ge " Madam' " said Traverse, after a thoughtful 10,11 A- "our fates have been more nearly connected t°;3 you could have imagined. Those Le Noire bsa been my enemies as they are yours. That Ye° a orphan heiress, who appealed from their crneltY the Orphans' Court, was my own betrothed. Heights was her patrimony, and is now her home, where she lives with my mother, and 1015 In their name I invite you to come. And take t comfort also; your enemy no longer lives; moot ago I left him ill with a mortal wound. This ing the papers announce his death. There renis':i therefore, but little for me to do, but to take 169 measures to free you from this place, and rest°oo you to your borne. Within an hour I shall set for New Orleans, for the purpose of taking Initiatory steps. Until my return thence, lady," said Traverse, respectfully taking lies ii "Farewell, and be of good cheer 1" (To be continued. )</p>
        <p>72," said Ine tl man, au s visitor, "I'm proud of my girls, see them all comfortably marrieii ; made a little money, they won't go bands penniless. There's Mary, i' - OYr old, and a real good girl. I shall • %Is, sand pounds when she marries. Tu who won't see thirty-five again, .11 'Lilo two thousand ; an' the man who 1., •; is forty, will have three thousand young man reflected a moment or e.nd L'oc# nervously Inquired, "You haN,,en't PI 1' have you? "On I And is it a real diamond Elurrourit the young woman, in the tivr4 maorbe imaginable. "it is not, Miss Fethcrii.oe." said ' h 0 young. man, firmly ; " but wi't: it toe reP, always been my custom to give •t•ci,eriop_ it with a real one itnmediatelY ance of the ceremony.' "MY son George is learnin ,tP;ighti new abinuey game they call gu:t, • .7', I- 418 pleased about it," said old 4 " tlegi goes out into the ten-acre lot to ;. • • aso iron tools, and, by Ji n h,t oli, much with 'em I lont, blieve I ; ploughin to do arter "AND so you refuse me?' " J , becuuse I am poor, I " not the reason. "Because El aristocratic than yours, pet 11K • 1,04 I see. You want to marry a title. • • ":01 tnato no such ambition." " Huiti! stranb Then why is it you refuse me " " eoalise can't bear the sight of you."</p>
