      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>" &amp;MAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>" &amp;MAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales ngards his winning of the Derby as en event of sufficient importance to be commemorated on canva-. The picture is ultim- ately to be enshrined in Marlborough House,where, since it lips been thought worth while to paint it. at all, it will long be an object of admiration and interest. The artist is Mr. G. D. Giles. If the scene and the subject have been suggested by his Royal Highness himself, it should be said that the Prince has been less inter,sted in the great excitement of the crowd at the moment of his victory than in the rush of the horses as they pass the winning post.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Johnston did not put the question in the House of Commons on Monday night of which he had given notice with regard to the national thanksgiving on the 23rd of September, by which date the Queen will have reigned longer than any of her predecessors. He originally postponed the question at the request of the leader of the House, and presurnAbly he has nqvv been asked tc with- draw it. The fact seems to be that in Court circles the idea is not popular in marking the mere fact that the Queen has occupied the throne longer than George 11E. by any special demonstration. Next vegr if Her Majesty lives, she will celebrate her 78th birthday and the sixtieth year of her reign,and as Lord Salisbury indicated, the latter event will afford s more suitable opportunity for some further recognition of the long and beneficent sway of the present sovereign.</p>
        <p>A small but highly interesting and Valuable ex- hibition illustrative of Sir Thomas More and his works has been arranged in the Chelsea Free r,ihrary in connection with the memorial statuette of the illustrative lawyer which has just been un- veiled in that institution. Most of the books, con- sisting of early editions of More's "Utopia," a first edition of his life attributed to Cresacre More printed in Paris in 1631 a rare second PH iti o n of Rohynson's translation of the "Utopia," 1656. in the original stamped binding. &amp;c., are loans from the collection of Mr. Alfred Cock,Q.C., who was mainly instrumental in promoting the scheme of the pub- lic memorial. There is also a scarce etching after Flolbein of More and his family, presented to the library by Sir Charles Dilke. The subscription for the purchase of the statuette has been influentially hacked, amongst the subscribers being many of the judges.</p>
        <p>Churchmen are losing all faith in the Glivern- went, and they are especially angry with Mr. Chamberlain. But what will they say when they hear that the Colonial Secretary proposes to dis- establish and disendow the Church in Sierra Leone? Not that there is very much of Establishment left, for the disintegration process, as Churchmen love to call it, has ben going on for sometime. Yet, strary-e to say, the Bishop reports that the native Church was never stronger and its finances were never more satisfactory than at present.</p>
        <p>The County Council at its meeting on Tuesday decided to invite applications for the post of chief officer of the fire brigade in succession to Captain Simonds, at a salary of £9OO a year, with ?,ensinii, free quarters, coal, gas, medical attendance, &amp;c. Captain Simonds retires at the end of the month with a gratuity of .£l,lOO and six months' salary and" emoluments.'</p>
        <p>Next session will be what may be called a Cham- berlain session—that is, a session devoted to carry- ing through some of the social reforms which Mr. Chamberlain has advocated. Hence, the resolution which has already been fls good as announced by Mr. Balfour, that the Education Bill will he shorn down te the most meagre dimensions, consistent with Riving aid to the voluntary schools. But much alarm has bagun to grow up among the Ministeria- lists,lest another meagre measure may prove a stumbling block in the way of next session's pro- gramme; for, however meagre, it will he attacked from two sides by widely e.ifferent parties, with widely different objects. The Church party will agitate for rate aid, and the Liberals will insist on local control, or a guaranteed amount of voluntary contributions by Churchmen, and on the aid being either confined to needy schools or given to all voluntary and all board schools alike. This implies that Nil.. Chamberlain's legislatson would be thrust well into the summer before it could be proceeded with, and this prospect the Colonial Secretary does not quite relish. As he is not likely, however, to give up his claim, the Government must either medify present intentions with regard to the fian- cis] clauses, or the House will be kept late into autumn. If anything is certain at all,it is that Mr. Chamberlain next year will not allow Mr. Balfour to mortgage the autumn to his grouse-shooting followers.</p>
        <p>The recent troubles in Johannegbur.g do not seem to have affected the price of property in that " golden " city. A few weeks ago eight stands, situated in Commissioner-st., Von Brandis-street, Kruis-st., and Fox-st., were sold for .£40.000. Less than two years ego this block of property changed hands for £8 000 and that was an enormous ad- vance on the original price.</p>
        <p>At the Royal Horticultural Society's show on Tues- day many aztrantive things were presented. First and foremost to claim attention was a spondid and varied collection of begonias from the nursery of Mr. H. J. Jones, of Lewisham. ConnoiAseurs. in- deed, pronounced it better than the best yet seen. Some of the single flowers, always more effective and individual than the doubles, measure some- thing like five inches in diameter. The Society, never lavish with gold medals, was constrained to award one to the grower of these magnificent blos- soms. The speciality of the day was,however, found in the stands devoted to cacti. A good deal of ad- mirable ugliness had resulted from the assiduous care of cultivators. Tilocereus senilis will always be an amusing caetns, if only for its wonderful imita- tion of the back view of the head of an aged man with shoulders mantled with long white hair. One collection numbered 230 species in all degrees of eccontricity, including a plant which elects to open its flawers at. the hour of 11 p.m.—surely the high society member of the cactus tribe. There was like- wise a strong display in the fruit department.</p>
        <p>An anonymous writer in the " Fortnightly Re- view" for August,dealing with Ministerial fortunes —or, rather,misfortunes—under the title of " Luck or Leadership," reminds 119 that the unlucky nu- meral thirteen has dogged the Government steps all the year.. On January 13 an inexperienced soldier was appointed as new Minister to China; early in February the Ministers committed themselves to thirteen Bills ; on February 13 Mr. Chamberlain made the speech in which he communicated the now historic invitation to President Kruger to visit England ; on March 13 the "Times" announced the Dongola expedition ; on April 13 the Irish Land Bill was introduced; on May 13 the Education Bill was read a second time ;on June 13 the Cabinet de- cided the course of business communicated at the famous Fogeign Office meeting on the 15th, and afterwards abandoned ; on July 13 'Afr. Russell's resignation was averted by a Government sur- render. And so on. What will the superstitious say?</p>
        <p>By the courtesy of the recipient. I have been al- lowed to see a private letter written by one of the best known men in Matabeleland, who is at present acting as the representative of one of the great Rhodesian companies at. Bulewave, and the picture that he gives of the position of affairs in the Char- tered Company's territory more than confirms the gloomy reports which recent cable messages have conveyed to us. The Kaffirs have now, he reports, been driven out of the Matabele portions of the country, but they have taken refuge, as we know, in the dense hill forests, from which he predicted, writing before Sir Frederick Carrington's abortive attack on the Matoppos, it would be found impos- sible to expel them by force. Starvation is the only weapon that will Se successful against the Matabele now, and it must be some months before they are reduced to subjec- tion, and in the meantime all businsss is necessarfly at a standstill, the writer proposes to join the ever- increasing stream of colonists who are making their way southwards to a country where the necessities of existence are to be obtained on more rsasonable terms. Every pound of provisions brought into Bulawayo costs is. 2d. for carriage from Mafeking, and from this tone fact alone it may be gathered that the cost of living in Rhodesia is quite beyond the means of the average private individual ; while it also throws a flood of light on the difficulties in connection with the transport with which Sir F. Carrington has to contend. From another source I learn that just a month ago in Bulawayo a bottle of milk cost, lie. 6d., and a tin of beef 4s. 6i. Many stores were being closed, and the traders were leav- ing by every coach for the Transvaal and the Cape, so that when winter rains come on it is probable that the country will be practically deserted,except by the men engaged in military operations.</p>
        <p>PARR'S BANK, LIMEPED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
