      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MEETING OF SHAREHOLDERS.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF SHAREHOLDERS.</p>
        <p>A general meeting of the shareholders of the above - mentioned bank was held at the Bank premises, Win- wick-street,Warrin:Jon,on Thursday week at noon. Mr. A. Turner, of Liverpool, in the absence of the chairstan and vice-chairman of the company, presided, and the directors present were—Sir Thomas G. Frost (Chester), Sir John James Harwood (Manchester), Colonel Gamble, C.B. (St. Helens), Messrs. James A. Bannerman (Man- chester), A. Crewdson (Manchester), T. H. Dixon (Gres- ford, Denbighshire), N. frarington Eckersley (Wigan), A. Hewlett, (Warwick), H. Warren Meade-King (Liverpool), R. Neill, jun. (Manchester), Peter Stubs (Newnham, Gloucestershire) and John Dun (Chislehurst). The shareholders present included : Drs. T. Starkey Smith, E. H. Beaman, U. E. Bower, Captain Withingten, Alderman Sinclair (St. Helens), Messrs. J. C. Parr, J.P.D.L , Alfred Jolley, J. C. Stead, C. C. Dunkerley, James Trickets, John Hartley, James Elandley (Garston),William Marlow,jun., Charles Baker, F. R. Hartley, Henry Booth,Henry Milling, Henry Dean, John Whitelegg, James Tabley, John Highfield, John Bodon,James Lees, John Roberts, John Matheson, Wm. Barlow,Georlze Blackliurst,William E. Lane,John White, W. Broster,Henry Nickson,Jameg Duckworth,W.Silcock, J. Murray, and Arch. Mackillop (manager Manchester branch).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>REPORT AND DIVIDEND.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT AND DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved the adoption of the report and balance sheet, the salient features of which v e printed in wur last issee.the dividend declared being one of 19 per cent. At the outset the Chairman said he was sure they would all be very sorry to hear that the Chair- man of the Company (Lord Playfair) was unable to bc present owing to Parliamentary duties and a command to attend the festivities at Marlborough House. Mr. Cecil F. Parr, the vice-chairman, was detained at home owing to deep domestic anxiety, and was unable to be present. In consequence it had devolved upon him to take the chair. He thought he had left the chair for ever when he ceased to be the Chairman of the Com- pany, but it happened that he must appear before them once more in that capacity, and perhaps they would allow him the privilege of a little retrospection. When he had the honour of joining that bank as a director at the time of the purchase of the National Bank of Liverpool, in December, 1883. the capital was £492,300, to-day it was £1,320,000 ; their reserve fund was then £269,650 whilst now it was £1,320,000; the deposits at the earlier period mentioned am ~luted to £3,454,000, whilst to-day they were £20,132 000. The chvi ;end in 1883 was one of 15 per cent. with a bonus of 4 per cent., making a total of 17i per cent ; te-day the shareholders received 19 per cent. In 1883 there was carried forward £12,000, to-day it was £37000. In 1883 there were 22 branches, new there were 87. There were then about 600 shareholders but to-day there were upwards of 5,000. Be thought that was, t quote a well-known phrase, progress by leaps and bounds. (Applause.) But if tie went still further back to the time when Mr. Dun came to Warrington to mannge what was then Parr's Banking Company the advance made was still more apparent. Then the capital was £lOO,OOO and the deposits was £BOO,OOO. The direc- tors and general manager had reason to be proud of the progress made. He taught they could only hope that they might go on in the same way. (Applause.) The report was in every way a most gratifying one. All the comparisons were equally satisfactory. The only point which called for special notice was the amalgamation of the Consolidated Bank Limited. For several years they had set their hearts upon obtaining a footing in Man- chester. and he thought they had now done so on mast satisfactory terms. Without in any way divulging secrets he might say that the Consolidated Bank had not only earned a dividend of 19 per cent, but had contributed to the reserve and general furl and nothing could be more satisfactory that the amalgamation so far. The neces- sary consequence of the amalgamation of the Consolid- ated Bank was an increase in the number of directors and they would have a resolution put before them that morning that six directors ef the Consolidated Bank be- come directors of ['area Bank, Limited. In adding to the number of directors they would be increasing the amount of remuneration, but he thought the labourer was worthy of his hire. (Hear, bear.) By the amalgamation, they had not only got an excellent foot- ing in Manchester with 12 or 13 branches but they had also freehold premises in Threadneedle-st., London, and a branch at Charing Cross which was doing very well. In the present generation almost everything went into limited liability companies, whether it was the greatest railway or the smallest shop. Everyone seemed afraid of private enterprise. Their Company had tried to keep pace with the times and had been able to do so. He hoped the American cloud would soon pass over, and that established as they were firmly in London, Liver- pool, and Manchester, he did not think they had any reason to fear the future. (Applause.) He concluded by proposing the adoption of the report and a dividend at the rate of 19 per cent. Mr. BANNERMAN seconded the proposition, and stated that as a director of the now extinct Consolidated Bank, he thotight toe amalgamation would benefit them all. If Parr's Banking Company had endeavoured to get a foot- ing in Manchester on their own " hook " they would have offered them a fair fight. But by the arrangement come to, Parr'a Bank had now an established business in Manchester and had obtained it on fair terms. He thought the bank would go on progressing. It was now one of the largest, best and safest in the kingdom. (Hear, hear.) The report was unanimously adopted. THE DIRECTORATE.</p>
        <p>Dr. BEAMAN moved the election as directors of Mr. James Alex Bannerman, Mr. James Tullock, Mr. Alfred Crowdson, Sir John James Harwood, Mr. Emil Heine- mann.and Mr. Robert Neill, jun., formerly directors of the Consolidated flank, Limited.--Mr. Broston seconded and it was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Aid. SINCLAIR moved that a vote of thanks be given to the directors of Parr's Bank, Limited, and the Consoli- dated Bank,Limited,for their services and that remuner- ation at £ll,OOO per annum be passed.—Mr. James Handley seconded the vote which was passed.- -Colonel Gamble moved a vote of thanks to the general manager and officials for their services, and referring to the permanent officers of Parr's Bank, he said they had done their very best for the interests of the bank. Re- ferring specially to Mr. Dan, the general manager, :he read extracts from the first annual report of the Bank, and referred to the satisfactory position of the Bank to- day. He congratulated himself on the fact that he had been able to spend 30 years of his life in association with Mr. Dun, the general manager. He was, however, sorry to say that all the first directors of the Bank except himself (Colonel Gamble) had passed away.—Cantain Withington seconded the vote of thanks.—ln a f.know- !edging the compliment, Mr. Dun said it certainly was a ma, ter of great pride and pleasure to him to have been instrumental in building up for the shareholders of that institution so strong and powerful a bank as it now was. He thought they had every reason to be satisfied with the ama.kamation which had been effected, and which had placed the bank well ia the front rank and made it one of the largest and ruost important joint stock banks in the United Kingdom. Mr. MACKILLOP said he had great pleasure in respond- ing on behalf of the officials of the Consolidated Bank. and assured the shareholders of Parr's Bank, Limited, that they had in the business of the Consolidated Bank which they had purchased, a thoroughly sound dividend paving business. Mr. J. CHARLTON PARR proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairmaii.—Sir John J. HARWOOD briefly seconded the resolution which was unanimously carried.—The Chairman haviug briefly responded, the meeting ter- minated.</p>
        <p>EPPS' COOO.I.—GRATEFUL AND COMFORTING By a thorough knowledge of the natural laws which govern the operttions of dli!estion and nutrition. and by a cargul appli- cation of the fine pro2erties of well selected COCOA, Mr. Epps has provide,i for our breakfast and supper a delicately fitvouret bevezage which may save us many heavy doctore 13n ls. It is by the judicious use (3! such artiele3 of diet that a constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to restst every tendency to disease. We may escape many a fatal shaft by keeping ourselves well fortifiNi with pure blood and a properly nourished frame."—C vil Service Gazette—Made simply with biAling water or milk Sold only in packets and pound tins, by Grocers, labelled— "1A1113 EPPs &amp; Co., Ltd., Homceopathic Chemis s, ondon." Also misers of Ppos' Cocoaine or Cocoa-Nib Extract : Tea- like : A thin beverage of full flavour, now with many bene- ficially takin, the place of tea. Its active principle being a gentle nerve stimulant, supplies the needed energy without unduly exciting the system.—Advt.</p>
