      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>WARRINGTO:q MUNICIPAL FINANCE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>MORE CRITICISMS AND SUGGESTIONS.</head>
        <p>MORE CRITICISMS AND SUGGESTIONS.</p>
        <p>The gentleman whose views we published last week but one on the subject of municipal finance, , one day this week expressed his opinions to an " Examiner " reporter upon the criticisms by Coun- cillor Shaw-Green which we published last week. "At the outset," said the gentleman interviewed, "let me call your attention to the water annuities and interest account in the Borough Treasurer's Report of 1893, and contrast it with the same ac- count presented in the report of 1896. What do we find? In the report of the earlier date I am shown a statement of the liabilities, and on the op- posite page the assets are set forth. Turning to the 1896 report the liabilities are shown but the assets are—blank! I have chosen the water account be- cause it is the largest the Corporation have to deal with,invoiving over a quarter of a million of money. Now, I want to know what has become of the lISSetB since 1893? le that arrant nonsense' or is it a reasonable question o ask? If it was necessary in 1893 to show the assets of the Water Works why is it not, the case in 189 d? To say the least of it, it is a little confusing. Then again in 1893 the same neeounts are made up to the 25th March, whilst in 1896, they are to the 31st March. Does that strike you as baing • clear, intellizible, and complete'"? "Councillor Shaw Green's point seems to be that the Corporation are obliged to comply with the regulations of the Local Government Board in com- piling the accounts, said the reporter. "That may be so, and tha accounts, do as Mr. Green says, 'follow each other' buz when I-ventured to criticise the Borough Treasurer's report in 1894, the rates of interest together with the names of the investois were given, but in the last report these are left out and only the initials are given. Surely this doesn't show much for the lynx-eye of the Local Government Board' which seems only to exist in the imagination of the Chairman of the Finance Committee."</p>
        <p>" I suppose you read that gentleman's statement that the Corporation were not like a private firm or trading concern that had to be balanced up to see if it was solvent.; "Yes, I did with same amusement. It does not matter whether you are a Corporation, a limited company or a private trading concern. you must keep the accounts in Ruch a way that they are per- fectly clear and intelligible to anybody. With goo,' book-keeping a clncern prospers, but when the contrary is the case loss creeps in and it is very difficult to detect it." "Well now,what of the criticisms respecting your suggestion for the consolidation of the Borough dent ? "</p>
        <p>"In the first place I have no desire to hamper the Chairman of the Finance Com- mittee with details. But if he succeedea in consolidating the Warrington debt, his name would go down to posterity as a public benefactor. I ap- preciate the difficulties. They require determina- tion and skill and close attention to business in order to overcome them. But I cannot agree with the Chairman of the Finance Committee that the benefit to be derived is not worth the Cost and trouble. Sir Artnur Forwood, at any rate, considered it was worth the trouble to consolidate the Liver- pool debt Chairmen of Finance Committees Borough Treasurers,and Town Clerks in Blackburn, Bolton, Derby, Halifax, Huddersfield, Macclesfield, Newcastle-on-Tyne, Rotherham, Tynemouth, Wol- verhampton, and other places have spared no ex- pense and no trouble to achieve what has proved to be a great benefit. Why not confer with these gentlemen and ask them how they did it? It is a molt important matter to save this £3,000 per annum. Itcan be done and the rates can be knocked down sd. in the .E. The bank rate is now 2 per cent. and it has been so for a long period. Consequently now is the time to improve the financial position of the Borough. Should consols decline And the bank rate increase it will not be so easy a matter to con- solidate the debt and obtain a reduction of the rate of interest which at present it is within the power of the Corporation to obtain. The suggested av- ing comes within the range of practical financesand is the goal for which we must strive." "The Chairman of the Finance Committee con- tended that any attempt to consolidate the debt would be opposed by the Local Government Board and not allowed." "1 cannot agree with him. The Local Govern- ment Board act as defendants, the Corporation be- ing plaintiffs. I know the Local Government Board will not furnish information without knowing for what purpose it is wanted, but the members of the Finance Committee must put. some backbone into their struggle with the Local Government Board and this can be done by employing good Parliamentary counsel who thoroughly understand just exactly what the powers of the Local Govern- ment Board are, what they can give, and what can be withheld."</p>
        <p>Have you anything further to state with regard to the assessments of the borough?</p>
        <p>"No, I am satisfied that that matter has been looked into. If they don't wish to give me any credit for urging them on in this matter through the medium of your columns in 1894, I am not going to quarrel with them. It is also to. fact that the Corporation has obtained a large sum of money to replace the stlrds borrowed at the rate of £3 5:3. per cent and the benefit arising from this the rate- payers will doubtless receive shortly." "As a last question, what do you think of Coun- cillor Shaw Green's suggestion that you should place yourself at the service of the ratepayers? "1 may say that I have no intention of doing so. I am hard pressed with business, and another objec- ion is that I live out of town."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>A RECORD WEEK.</p>
        <p>The seagoing tonnage entered and cleared at the port of Manchester during the week ended Friday last week was the largest recorded since the opening of the Ship Canal. Eighty-two vessels of 29,376 tons register passed inwards through the Easthana and Weston Mersey locks, whilst 95 vessels of 30,633 tons sailed from the canal—a total of 177 vessels and 60.009 tons net registered tonnage, against 191 vessels of 50,733 tons in the preceding week. The arrivals from foreign ports included seven steamers and three sailing vessels with timber from Canada and tbie Baltic, two sailing vessels with dyewoods from the West Indies, five steamers with over 2,000 tons of boulder flints from the North of France, a barque with resin from Savannah, a steamer with iron ore from Lulea, and a steamer with lead and esparto from Spain, besides the regular Continental boats. There were sail - lags to Blmbay, Australia (via Bristol), Para, Bahia(via Cardiff), Laguna, Rio Grande, Alexandria, Genoa, Bar- celona. and St. Petersburg, and to various French, Bel- gian, Dutch, German, and Scandinavian ports. The Irish steamers discharged 139 head of live stock at Trafford Wharf, including 75 cattle, 47 sheep, 14 horses, two pats, and a donkey.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holland, preident of the Manchester Chamber of Commerce, declared on Monday that if coal strikes were to occur at short intervals we should soon find ourselves face to face with the question whether the State should not assume ownership of coal mines throughout the country on fair terms.</p>
        <p>SURGEON-GENERAL C. R. FRketiClS, M.D.—" I was much impressed with the extreme care taken Lo secure purity of material and cleanliness in making it. Hops Ai c refreshes, stimulate, an.l acts as a general nerve tonic." DR. J. A. WEBSTER, M.D., M.R.C.S —" I like it, and it me. It is a sharp, light hitter ale, pleasant and appetising." Fifteen Gold Medals awarded. Two million bottles sold in ; one week. Bittered from choicest Hops only. Agent :—W. .cURNESS, Kops' Stores, Bewsey-street, Warrington. (4)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>GUST 1, 1896.</head>
        <p>GUST 1, 1896.</p>
        <p>"SPEECIEE DAY" AT WARRINGTON GRAIIMA.R SCIIOOL.</p>
        <p>The large schoolroom at the Warrington Grammar School on the occasion of the annual speech day pro- ceedings on Tuesday afternoon was innnveniently crowded with scholars, parents and friends. The pro- gramme submitted—although a long one—was of a most interesting character and the headmaster's report was evidence of the successful work of the scholars during the year. The ladies and gentlemen present were :—The Rev. Canon and Mrs. Willis, the Mayor and Mayoress, (Councillor and Mrs. Fairclnugh),Miss Mabel Fairclough, Mrs. Beatnont, Miss Smith, Miss Pierpoint (St. Austin's), Mrs. Willcocks,Miss Willcocks, Mr. R. D. Willcocks. Mr. and Mrs. R. H. Willcocks (London), Mr. and Miss Ful- lagar; Miss Dawson, Dr. White, Miss White, Mr. Robert Davies, Mrs. and Miss Broadbent, the Rev. E. C. E, and Mrs. Carleton, Miss Massey, Mr. Roberts, Mr. Bates, the !Rev. L. B. Radford (second master of the school), Mr. Young and Mr. Herzig (other masters), Dr. and Mrs. Braide, the Rev. G. A. Currier, Mr. F. and Mrs. Monks, Captain and Mrs. Waring, Mr. and Mrs. Jeans, Mr. and Mrs. Owen, Mr. and Mrs. R. Fair- dough, Mr. J. and Mrs. Hesketh, Major and Mrs. Burke, Alderman and Mrs. Harrison. Dr. and Mrs. Geary, Mr. and Mrs. W. S. Woads, Mr. and Mrs. McGregor, Mr. Robert Dobson, Miss Dlbson, Mr. and Mrs. Richardsnn,Mr.and Mrs. Gibson,Miss White (Legh-' street), Mrs. Owen, Mr. and Mrs. Milling, Councillor Bennett, Dr. and Mrs. Spinks, Rev. S. .1. Fleming, Rev. E. P. and Mrs. ;Wetherel, Rev. H. R. Wild, Mr. L. W. and Mrs. Reynolds, Mr. B. Mel- ville, Councillor and Mrs. W. Pierpoint, Miss Pierpoint, the Rev. J. and Mrs. Jones, Dr. and Miss Bowden, the Rev. W. R. and Mrs. Hartwright, the Rev. F. and Mrs. Sfater and Miss Slater, Mr. and Mrs. L. Talbot, Counciller Ellison, Mrs. and Miss Pierpoint, Councillor and Mrs. Davies, Mr. J. and Mrs. Reynolds, Mrs. Crossley, Mr. F. Crossley, Miss Curjel. Mr. F. Reynolds, Dr. and Mrs. Gornall, Mr. and Mrs. Beevers, Mr. and Mrs. Chompson, Miss Thompoon. Mr. and Mrs. Dielsten, Mr. Glre, Mr. and Mrs. Bolton, Dr. Cannel!, Miss Cannel!, the Rev. B. and Mrs. Percival,Mr, I and Mrs. Milner, Mrs. Lamb, Miss Lamb, Mr. and Mrs. Jones, Miss Jones, Mr. and Mrs. Miles, Mr. and Mrs. Forshaw, the Misses Rimmer, Miss Trotter, Mr. and Mrs. Thompson. Mr. and Mrs. Green, Mrs. and Miss Rose, Mr. and Mrs. Kidd, Mr. G. and Miss Efolford, Councillor VV, Rogers, Councillor and Mrs. Woods, Councillor Baitd, Mr. and Mrs. Gorse, Mr. and Mrs. Holford, Miss Moores, Miss Leigh, Mr. and Mrs. Randall, Mr. and Mrs. Saunders, Mrs. Bradshaw, Mrs. Scott, Mr. and Mrs. Evans, Mr. and Miss Hatton, Mrs. Loy, Miss Atkins, Mr. and Mrs. Chantry, Miss Blanchet, Sergeant-Major and Mrs. Sullivan, Mr. and Mrs. Knowles, Mr. and Mrs. Picton, Mr. and Mrs. Hayward, Mr. and Mrs. Parting. ton, Miss Craik, Miss Mason, Mr. and Mrs. Dewhurst, Mr. E. Wilkes, Miss Brough, Mr. and Mrs. Bailey, Mr. and Mrs. Cooke, Miss Poole, Mr. and Mrs. B. Browne, Mr. and Mrs. Poole, Mr. and Mrs. Roberts, Mrs. R. Byerley, Miss Byerley, Miss Carrall, Mrs. Westcott, Miss Freeman, Miss Whitlow, Miss Hilton, Mr. and Mrs. Dal- t3n, Mrs. Lowe, Mr. and Mrs. Ferguson, Mr. J. A. and Mrs. Sutton, Mr. and Mrs. Wallington, Mr. and Mrs. McGarty, Miss Pemberton, Mrs. Porter, Mrs. Englands, Mrs. Cowdell, Mr. Ruddeck, Mrs. Ruddock, and Miss Ruddock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>HEADMASTER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE HEADMASTER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Tho HEADMASTER, in his annual speech, said his first duty was to give a hearty welcome to all. If their his- tory during the past twelve months had been unevent- ful, yet they had passed through a course of quiet in- dustry,the fruits of which would be apparent in years to corn. The list of honours in the Oxford local examina- tion last year was very satisfactory. They were suc- cessful in gaining twelve certificates,including seven honourable di-tinntions in various subjects. (Applause.) One of the:r senior candidates, E. S. Kidd, who was seventh ic the first class.was placed first in Letin,fourth in Greek,and fifteenth in French in the honours lists, be- sides passing in seven other subjects. (Applause.) This result was most gratifying, and would prove to those in- terested in them that their syllabus was neither narrow nor restricted. Their other successes and appoint- ments since last speech day included : W. 'l'. Garnett, who bad been selected after open competition for an engineer studentship in the Royal Navy; F. H. Gornall, of Owen's College, who had been graduated B.Sc. with honour% Vic. Uni- versity; El. K. Gornall, scholar of St. Catherine's College, Cambridge, who had graduated B.A.with honours in the tripls (Applause). Dr. Gornall was to be congratulated upon the record which his three youngest sons ha established for the school. (Applause.) E W. Coates, scholar of St. Catherine's College, Cambridge, had graduated B.A. in hanour in the second class (senior optime) of the mathematical tripos. T. Porter had passed the preliminary examination of the Institute of Chartered Accountants ; F. A. Yeomans had passed the preliminary examination of the Pharmaceutical Society; C. H. Ferguson. of University Cllege, Liverpool, had won the gold medal of anatomy; R. D. Willcecks, of Ueiversity College, Liverpeol, had been awarded a prize for practical anatomy ; El. H. Ferguson ha, I passed his second professional examination at the New Veterinary College, Edinburgh; A. Down and Seger Owen had passed the fi .al examination qiialifyine for admission to the Royal Institution of British Architects and had been elected associates (A..R.1.8.a.); J. W. Isherwood, M.A., of Victoria University, had, after a year's resi- dence at Durham University been awarded honours in the examination for the degree of licentiate in theology; A. S. Kidd, 8.A., late scholar of St. John's College, Cam- bridge, had been appointed classical maeter in the Car- narvon County Intermediate School; R E.Kenyon,B.Sc., Vie. University, had been appointed chemistry master at the Liverpool Institution. The Rev. J. A. Hannah, 1 M.A., late scholar of Queen's College, Cambridge, and classical master at St. John's College, Battersea had been elected to the responsible position of principal of the Norwich and Ely Training College for school mistresses. (Applause.) He was pleased to have that further opportunity of again thanking the teaching staff for the loyal and zealous manner in which they had sup- ported him. They were losing the services of one of their masters—the Rev. L. B. Radford, fellow of St. John's College. Cambridge, who had done such credit to himself and honour to the school during the five years that he had been with them. (Loud applause.) Mr. Radford had been presented to a college living, the Rectory of Forncett, St. Peter, Norfolk. He would take with him the sincerest wishes for his future happiness and prosperity in his new sphere of work. (Applause.) Their warmest thanks were due to the donors of the special prizes and to the subscritvrs to the University Scholarship fund. It was with very great regret that he had to record the death of the late Right Hon. Lord Lil- ford—who was one of their most generous supporters, and one whose family had been so long and so intitnately connected with the school. His lordship was a true noble- man and Christian gentleman. His good works would follow him ani his memory would always be held in res- pect by those who knew him and had cognizance of his noble and kindly deede. The present moment was an anxious time for Grammar schools in view of the many changes that were likely to arise, in the immediate future. It could not be denied that secondary or intermediate education had long been in a more or less chaotic state in regard to organisation; and in its want of corraletion with existing agencies much waste of power was being lost by overlapping and undue competition. But a cast iron rigidity in the curricula of such schools would lead to deplorable results. The course of instruction in the school was maintained on a wide basis No one side of study was pushed to the prejudice of another, but all subjects necessary for a comprehensive and liberal edu- cation had their due amount of attention. Nor could it be said even of their greatest detractors with any justice that they were neglectful of the interests of those who were destined for a purely commercial life, for all boys of a certain standing in the school could be specialised or could enter the modern side. We would once again draw the attention of parents to the fact that they had lately successfully established a preparatory form with a lower capitation or tuitien fee far little boys under twelve year; of age. The exeminer in his report referring to the epecial prizes said that endeavour wee beneficial to the boys, it was a wholesome variation from the humdrum routine of school life, and tended to the development of special taste and talent that would otherwise remain latent. The examiner concluded be saying that the reel ts of that examination had been further to confirm his distinctly good impression of the general character of Warrington Grammar School. The curriculum was admirably arranged to bring out blend- ing best powers of the boys. There was a judicious of the older and more modern method. of study, and the result was seen not only in the successea of the boys but in the useful lives which so many of the former pupils were loading in the outside world. He could sin- cerely cengratulate the headmaster and his excellent staff upon the immediate as well as the more abi ling results of their work and the governors upon Vie use- fulness to the commuuLy at large of the lestitution which was under their management." (Applause.) DISTRIBUTION OF _PRIZES.</p>
        <p>The Itscron th—eTaistributed the priz-s.s, the Hat being as follows : ciassies.—Vl. Form. Kidd; V. Farm, Price; Upper IV. Form, weeks; Liw.ir IV. Form. Burke, ma. ; Form, Poole; II Form, Pierp)int, mi. ; I. Form. Burke, ter. Mathematics.—VI Set, Gibson, ma. ;V. Set, Miles; Upper and LowAr IV. Sets, Hatton. Arithmetic.—lll. and 11. Sets, Randall; Upper and Lower I. Seta, Percival, mi.</p>
        <p>Ancient Iligtory.—Vl. Form Greek 510-404 B.CI and Rothan 146-31 8.C., Kidd; V:Form, Greek 510404 B C., Scott.</p>
        <p>A CARD</p>
        <p>Trv" NOWN as Rev. Joseph Holmes' Mexican Prescription -113‘ is the only original guaranteed Remedy for all those who Buffet; from nervous weakness, exhausted vitality- kidney or bladder diseases, gravel, prostate, spinal weak• ness, brain fag and kindred complaints. It has stood the ,test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed stamped nvelope to Jos. HOLMES' Remedy Co: Bloomsbury Man eions, Bloomsbury Square, London. and get full particulars of this gr.at specific free of charge. Mention whether for male or female. Beware;of frand,iltnt imitators.</p>
        <p>English History 1485-1688.—Upper and Lower IV. Forms, Woods, ma. ; 111. and 11. Forms, Bryant, ml.; Upper and Lower I. Forms, Jones. Scripture Knowledge.—Lamplugh Memorial, St. Mark and Acts i.-xvi. with the Greek,Gibson, ma. • V. Form, St. Mark with the Greek and IT. Kings (Rev. :M. Steven- son's prize), Price; IV. Form, H. Kings (Rev. E. C. E. Carleton's prize), Burke,ma. ; Massinghans Memorial,St. Mark, Thompson; Upper and Lower I. Forms, Burke, mi.</p>
        <p>Science.—Vl. Set, physics,Wallace ; V. Set, chemistry, Reynolds, ma ; IV. Set, physical geography, Cook-imi. Modern Linguages (Dr. Gornall's prizes).—Vl Set, German,Wallace ; V. Set, German, Joseph, ma ; VI. Set, French, Holford, ma ' • V. Set, French, Joseph, ma; IV. Set. French, Weeks; 111. Set, French, Monks. Dictation.—V. and IV. Forms, Joseph, ma ; 111., 11., I. Forms, Brougli. Writing.—V. and IV. Forms, Bailey ; 111., 11., I. Forms,Brough. Drawing.—Shadinz, Reynolds, mi. ; model, Evans; advanced freehand, Randall ; elementary freehand, Bol- ton, ma. _</p>
        <p>Michaelmas Term—Form Prizes.—Vl. Form, Kidd ; V. Form, Price; Upper IV.Form, Glover; Lower IV.Form, Weeks; 111. Form, Burke, ma. ; 11. Form, Bryant, ml.; Upper I. Form, Burke, ter. English E4say.—The Early English Town, Miles. English Verse.—Virg. Georg. iv., 504-529 (Dr. White's priz-). Kidd. Natural History Collection (Miss Massey's prize), Rey- nolds, mi. ; Ashton Memorial, Hoovers, ma. Recommended for election to Senior Bolder Scholar- ships.—A. B. Miles and J. W. Scott. Oxford Local Examinations, Senior Candidates, Hon- ours Division.—First class, E. S. Kidd, distinguished in Latin, Greek and French ; Pass Division, H. Gibson. Junior Candidates, Honours Division. Second class, W. T. Gornall, distinguished in Greek • D. C. Wallace, distinguished in Greek. Third class, G. B. Helford,dis- tinguished in Greek. Pass Division, J. D. Fria*, distin- guished in Greek ; W. B. Forshaw,T. Porter J. W. Scott, R. Pierpoint, R. M. Roberts, T. F. WallingtOn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO THU REV. L.B. RiDFORD.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO THU REV. L.B. RiDFORD.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. B. Kidd, after having read some English. verse of his own for which he had received a prizefmade a pre- sentation consisting of a silver salver supplied by Messrs. W. Woods and Sin of Sankey-street, to the Rev. L. B. Radford. He said Mr. Radford on leaving them left be- hind him a reputation for the sincerest devotion to his work and to the boys under his care, and it was natural that they should wish to give him some token of their esteem and regard for his services. He found it his most pleasant duty to make that presentation oa behalf of the school to dr. Radford, their master and friend. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>7 The Rev. L. B. RLDIFORD in replying, said in the first place, he had to ask their forgiveness for many things. Boys were complicated creatures to understand. (Hear, hear and laughter.) There were no two boys alike, and no master was infallible in judging boy-nature. The re- sult was that every man, however earnestly he might try to do his d uty, was bound to make a certain number of mistakes,and possibly those mistakes hurt at the time probably no one more than the master himself. And so every master when he said,'Good-bye"to his boys had to ask for their forgiveness for characters misunderstoon.for faults exaggerated, and for many similar mistakes that he would not have made had he been able to read boy- nature better. But, on ttfe other hand, he txgged to offer the boys something, and it was his warmest thanks, not merely for that gift, much as he should treasure it, but for the gift they had bestowed upon him all theqe five years. There was an old Latin proverb, diseendo docemus, docendo diseimus—by learning we teach, and by teaching we learn—and if it was true that a boy grew up into the master by process, the process of his own education, it was equally true that masters went on learning every day from the boys from whom they were brought iato contact—things that were not usually found in the pages ot a book, but which were far truer, and of deeper import than many things found in black and white in a school text-book. (Applause.) Re had te thank them for the gift, and for a still greater gift that he had tried, however, feebly, to describe—the gift of the experience they had bestowed open him. If he might make one further remark it was this, that he hoped that what they had done for him they would do for his successor, that they would give him that loyalty and consideration which they had shown to him, so that when his time came to leave he might feel as deep , debt of gratitude as be did now, and always should do, to the boys of the Warrington Grammar School. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>RECTOR AND THE EDUCATION QUESTION</head>
        <p>THE RECTOR AND THE EDUCATION QUESTION</p>
        <p>The RECTOR said he was present on account of the death of Lord Lilford. His son, the present Lord Lil- ford, would have been present to distribute the prizes that day but for the death of his father. The late Lard Lilford wae a stranger to most in Warrington. owing to his ill-health. He was a great invalid, but his life showed what even an invalid could do. It should certainly be an encouragement to all of them who might in time be laid aside and live a life of retirement. His Lordship was a great student of natural history, and took the greatest interest in the publication of the most magni- ficent work which was now coming Out. He had a splendid collection of birds in his grounds, and though he was a strong Conservative he had the greatest belief in the people, as evidenced by his invitations to the shoemakers of Northampton, Kettering, and Welling borough, who visit-ed his grounds. His lordship had told him that he had never had the slightest cause to regret that invitation, because the visitors were so particular and did not leave the slig itest trace of their presence in the grounds. Kindly reference was also made by the Rector to the present Lord Lilford. Referring to science, the Rector remarked that it had sometimes been sup- posed that there had been a want of sympathy on the part of the clergy towards science. Formerly there might have been grounds for the supposition. As time went on, however, a different feeling prevailed, and there was no opposition now on the part of intelligent students amongst the clergy to the discoveries of science. If there was any place in which science should' be important it was in a manufacturing centre like Warrington, and he trusted that if their townsmen took an interest in this question that they would be able to see their way to giva what he was sure the Headmaster would greatly value, a laboratory for the use of the school, so that science might be studied as well as other things. (Applause.) On the other hand, the claims of literature were equally im- pertant ; it was for the spread of literature as well as of science that that great school was founded. There was another point of great importance, and it was with re- ference to the late Education Bill. Mr. Balfour, in speak- 'log the other day in °raise of the Bill, said that the ob- ject of it " was to associate with relief to voluntary I schools a scheme of education which would bring into facus primary and secondary education, and should effect a great reform in all departments of education lower than ,the universities." Surely that was a noble aim, but it was too large an aim, it was too comprehen- sive, and that was one of the causes why the Bill was withdrawn—because there was an attempt to combine elementary and secondary education. They were lonk- ing forward to a new Bill. He was 'inc who believed in the separation of elementary and secondary education. He believed in a limit of age and subject, and he wanted particularly to paint out that in those who believed in the importance of this line of demarcation between the two there was net for a moment any intention of making an attack on the higher grads:elementary school. They had a higher grade elementary :girls' school and if they had the same for the boys he should look upon both of them as bridges to step on from elementary schools into the hither secondary tillools,stich as this one. (Applause.) He beiteved that this was a matter of the greatest im- portance, and that before very long a Bill would be in- troduced which would deal wisely not only with the question of elementary education, but secondary also. His other poiet was this—the combination of religions with eecular knowledge, both in elementary and in secular schools. He had the greeeet opinion of their large English public schools. He was connected with one himself for FieVen years. of 800 hays, and he believed the public schoo's of Eekland had been the making of the English character. They made hors manly, brave, pure, and formel that English character which was the admiration of other countries. But, at the same time. he did not think that in these large Hagfish public schools there bad been that care for definite religious instruction there should have been. They were all of them anxious now about defi- nite religious instr.uction of children in their elementsry schools, but he did not think there was that same care for that instruction in the large public schools as when he was boy. He could honestly say of the home and school life of that school that the Headmaster and his staff and family took the deepest interest in realeprac- tical religion, that they tried to carry it out themselves in their lives and to teach it to the best of their power. In cnn..?lu4on, he wished all hsppinees, success and prosperey to the Headmaster and staff of the school. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>During tin afternoon, three plays were performed by a number of the scholars who acquitted thewiselves ad- mirably. The first was a French play entitled "Tin Monsieur qui prend Ia moucbe " (an irascible gentleman. The dramatis persnnre were :—Alphonse de Beaneednit, D. 0. Wallace; Mame!. G. B. Holford ; Jarancon (Becamers daughter), J. D. Price • Cynrien (Becan2ePs servant), T. Beevers; Dominique (fieauce luit's servant), J. E. Bryant. For the Greek play, scenes frem " The Wasps" of Aristophanea were selected, in which the characters were as foilows :—Philocleon E. S. Kilt! ; Bdelycleen, H. Gibson ; IClnthias, J. W, Scott; thenaeus, W. B. Forshaw ; Labes, L. Davies; Cynarinn, T. A. B. McGarty. The characters in the German play. " Ehrgeiz in dor Kadin " (a:nbition in the kitchen) were as follows :—Vatel (chief cook), A. D. McGregor; Cesar (hls son, a cook), R. Pierpoint ; Hasenbein (steward), G. W. N. Joseph; Regine (a cook), A. B. Miles; Laridon (a cook). D. J. Dunn; cooks, &amp;c., F. H. Cooke, J. Jackson, M. J. Glover. Mr. Birch Browne, of Sankey-street, was responsible for the wigs and make-up of the boys, and the onp,o- priate costumes were from the well-known house ,)f Mr. J. Burkinshaw, of Liverpool. Mr. W. S. Woods, music teacher of the school, played .the musical accmnpani- ments during the afternoon, and the boys very credit- ably sang a number of songs. The • proceedings con- cluded with cheers for the headmaster and Mrs. Will- cocks, the masters, governors, and other friends of the institution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>LOCAL OFF-LICENSE HOLDER. SUMMONED.</head>
        <p>A LOCAL OFF-LICENSE HOLDER. SUMMONED.</p>
        <p>A PECULIAR CUSTOM.</p>
        <p>At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, before Mr. Henry Nield (chairman), Captain Rogers, and Mr. John White, Alfred Eaton, provision dealer, Seven Oakes,was charged with Belling drink upon his off-licensed premises in eontravention of the sth section of the Licensing Act. 1872, at 9 30 p.m. on the 11th July.—Mr. Giles,soliciter, Chester, prosecuted, and Mr. Browne, solicitor, War- rington, represented the defendant.—At the outset, Mr. Browne raised an objection to the wording of the sum- Moe, and contended that it was bad as it was not in lconformity with Oakes' magisterial list.—Mr. Giles eepressed his opinion that the summons was in proper form.—The Clerk suggested that if Mr. Browne wished to press his objectien the present summons might be withdrawn, and a fresh one taken out.—Mr. Browne ultimately agreed to proceed with the case.—Mr. Giles stated that on the date named there were eight or nine persons with liquor before them found upon the defend- ant's off-licensed premises. It was a grocers' shop and it was frequently the customito give something in the way of disceunt,in some cases he had given bear If there wan a gift and the defendant received no valuable -. consideration at all, making a gift out and out, then, no doubt, he was not liable. But he would leave the magistrates to draw their:own conclusions from the fact that the police caught eight persons on the premises, some in one room and some in another, and these per- sons all had intoxicating liquors before them. Some had pints others had half-pints, and was it to be thought that on this day the defendant entertained the whole of these men because some were paying him for groceries P He would ask their Worships to say that he came with- in the sth section of the Licensing Act and liable to a penalty.—P.S. Allman, stationed at Great Budwortb, stated that he visited the off-licensed premises of the defendant at 9-30 on the 11th inst., in company with P.C. Ruxton. He went to the back of the premises and in the kitchen he found three men named James Webs- ter Walter Frith, and James J. Ridgeway. Each had a pia-pot part filled with beer. Constable Ruxton called his attention to three more men in the back kitchen named Peter Murphy, John Holmes, and John Smith. The place was in total darkness. He-struck a light and asked for the landlord. He asked the defend- ant what he was doing with those men in the place drinking. He hesitated for a short time and Webster who was near said "He generally gives us a drink when we fetch geode." The landlord then said "I give them a glass when they pay up." Witness re- plied that some of the men had pints and it did not lock like giving beer when one of the men concealed his beer under the seat. Witness told the landlord that he should report him for selling drink consumed on the premises, to which be replied " I am sorry but you must look over it this time, and it ehall not occur again." Ridgeway, one of the men in the house said "You must not be too hard with us master.'—F.C. Buxton gave corroborative evidence.—For the defence, Mr. Browne said the Act under which the proceedings were taken, was framed in order to prevent the illicit sale of drink on premises which only had an outdoor license. But his client had done nothing of the kind. He did not dispute the material facts of the case. He was going to admit that the whole *f the men were on the premises, but it was the custom Mr. Eaton's shop to give a drink of beer when the customers paid up the account. The estab- lishment formerly was simply a grocer's shop and occu- pied previously by the defendant's brother. He occupied it for seventeen years, and even when there was no off- license he was in the habit of Living a drink of beer to every customer when he paid up his account. Mr. Eaton's shop was the only provision shop in the district for a great distance and practically supplied the whole neighbourhood with their provisions. He sold very little beer and it was his invariable custom to give a drink of beer to every person when he paid up his account. He (Mr. Browne) was not going to defend the principle, and had told the defendant that the practice was open to the objection of the police who might visit his premises at any time. The defendant had told him that he should discontinue the practice in future. It should be borne in mind tnat it had been continually occurring for over twenty years, and no objection had been made until the present visit of the police to the back premises. All the people that were found there were customers of the de- fendant who got provisions from him during the week and paid him when they got their wages. With regard to the alleged concealment by one man of his beer, he might say that the man whose name was Ridgeway was unable to lift the glass from a table as he had sustained injuries to his shoulder, and that was the reason the beer was seen in a different place by the police. It had been argued that the drink was in the shape of discount, and that as such the defendant got sritn- advantage. But it was nothing of the kind. That custom of giving beer did not bring the c I,tomers to the shop as it was the in- variable custom of every shopkeeper in the district. He submitted that the beer was a pure gift.lt was a perfectly proper case to bring before the Court and the defendant was called upon to make a proper explanation. There was no doubt that the police had received information from some publican in the neighbourhood who felt the effect of the practise, but as it was a gift the defendant was not liable under the Licensing Act.—Mr. Joseph Eaton, of Comberbach, stated that the defendant was his brother. He had occupied the shop previou.ly for seventeen years and during that time he had given his customers a drink of beer.—Evidence for the defence was also given by W. Frith, Seven Oakes. Thomas Rideeway, Miss Lyon, William Hulse,George Wilkinson, Adolphue Wilkinson. James Webster, John Owens, and the defendant. The last named witness said he never knew there was anything wrong in giving the beer. The magistrates retired, and on their return the Chair- man said they had decided to dismiss the case. The police had done quite right in bringing it before them. They hoped the practice would be discontinued in future.</p>
        <p>The great event of the Sisley rifle meeting W3EI de- cided on Slturday, Lieutenant Thomson, of the Queen's Edinburgh, being proclaimed the winner of the Queen's Prize of £250, with a gold medal and badge. His total score was 273, as against 270 ma,de by the second man, Corporal Coe-ern, of the South Stafford. The Qoenn's Priz,nnan is in his 29:h year, and practises with loin father in Edinburgh as a civil engineer.</p>
