      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, AUGUST Ist, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>THE verdict ar.d sentences in the Dr. Jameson's trial of Dr. JAMESON and his col- _ _</p>
        <p>leagues should act as a necessary reminder that filibustering on behalf of char- tered speculators will not be tolerated in these days in any part of the Queen's donainions. After a trial before the Lord CHIEF JUsTICE, Mr. JUS- TICE HAWKINS, and BARON POLLOCK, Dr. JANIE- som has been sentenced to fifteen months impri- sonment, Sir JOHN WILLOUGHBY to ten months, Major ROBERT WHITE to seven months, and Colonel GREY, Colonel HENRY WHITS and Major COVENTRY to five months. These sentences will not be regarded as erring on the side of excessive severity by any one who takes into account the serious nature of the offence and the disastrous consequences that have followed the ill-judged raid into the Transvaal. At the present time Matabeleland is a scene of blo Ashed, and this is due almost entirely to the fact that the men who should.have kept order in that recently conquered province went with JAMESON in the "rush for Johannesburg" and were along with him sent out of the country by the victorious Boors. Naturally enough the black people of Rhodesia thought that thisopportunity for striking a blow for independ- ence had come when they saw the two dominant white races—the British and the Dutch—in arms against each other. The result is deplorable. Many white settlers, in same cases accompanied by their families, have been murdered by the natives, and the British forces are now engaged in a war of repression which would in all proba - bility never have been necessary if the unfortu- nate raid had not taken place. Under the cir- cumstanced Dr. JAMESON was not far from the mark when he said, after the trial, that his sen- tence "might have been worse." It is now said that a careful Home Secretary has taken these delinquents under his specie: charge. The verdict of the Court was simply to the effect that they should be imprisoned without hard labour, hut even before they had reached Holloway Gaol Sir MATTHEW WHITE RIDLEY had forwarded instruc- tions that they were to be treated as " first class misdemeanants." This means that they will be allowed to expiate their offence in a tolerably endurable fashion. They will be allowed to wear their own clothes and to have visits from their friends. They will not be expected to sub- sist on prison fare, but may have their meals sent to them from a first-class hotel. They will be permitted to write and receive letters, and, no doubt, to read the morning and evening news- papers, and they will not have to perform any of the menial and humiliating duties which fall to the lot of ordinary prisoners. In fact, nobody could expect to spend a few months in gaol under more agreeable conditions. It is clearly much safer to raid a friendly State at the instigation of a few wealthy and highly-placed money-grubbers than to engage, for instance, in an agitation against Irish landlordism. But, after all, we do not expect that there will be any outcry against these official favours. There is a widely-spread feeling that Dr. JAMESON and his officers are not the most guilty parties in connection with this scandalous invasion of the Tranvaal, and no one wishes to see them bear the penalties that should</p>
        <p>ACCIDENTS TO LIFE AND LIMB ,ttILIVAY ACCIDENTS. ENIPLOYBRS' LIABILITY. MITRED AGIINST Bit THE</p>
        <p>RAILW AY PASSENGERS' ASSURANCE, CO.</p>
        <p>ESTABLNITED 1849. CIAPIrfkL E 1.000.000.- COMPHNSASION PAID £3.600,003. 64, CORNIIILL, LONDON. A. VIAN, Secretary. - -</p>
        <p>Ara...a_ AGRNI`I• _ '‘utigton Mr. W. It. Rlti ‘ EY, ...... Nem iffe. Victoria Road Int-Word</p>
        <p>fall in some part at all events upon other and ; broader shoulders; The .further actions of the Government in connection with the South Afri- can inci.,lent will now be watched with interest. ! After the result of the JAMICSON trial they can be in no doubt as to the 'illegality of the raid, and</p>
        <p>Rek</p>
        <p>...Vir..T. B. LEACH. itardshaw-areet .Mr. ALFRED B. CUTTER ..... Jr. R.PINLOW.Rookery Bringe</p>
        <p>Fu i they can surely not, after punishing subordi- iT3 oft 11 i PILEPSY, Inates, allow principals to escape. It has been , GIDDINE.S. SI4.ASATIONS, FAINTS. i clearly shown that Dr. JAAIIiSON only car- ,... t} • . (11._ -ill demonstrate to the whole world how that most ried out designs which had been prepared ~'Nflfol of 411 c.tmplaint3, " Epilemy." which has been ! mithe" ~ (1,, con by others. It has also been shown thatsidered Incurable, can be permanently cured 1 114,7" t the chance of failure). W,rite to the "SECRETARY," these designs were not contrived for the pur- lyiii-"qa flouie. Durwood Place, Hyde Park, London. He Om of accomplishing any high or philanthropic le" You, " Gratis," full inatructlons for cure. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>MR. ANDREW HUTCHINSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>objects. It used to be said that the "rush for Johannesburg" was made solely for the purpose of protecting the women and children from name- less horrors. That, it is now known, was an in- genious fiction. The women and children were in no danger until a disturbance was caused by Jame- son's own movements. Then it was said that the expedition was organised in order to aid the Uit- landers of Johannesburg to secure the franchise and other rights of citizenship. This, too,it is now known, was not an honest explanation. One of the leading "reformers" of Johannesburg had himself written to his fellow conspirators that the Uitlanders did not care a fig for the franchise. Even stronger testimony to the same effect was afterwards given by British workmen who declared that they had no fault to find with the Boer Government, and that they preferred it to the capitalist domination which it was evidently the intention of the raiders to set up. Other excuses have been made for the invasion but none of them will, so to speak, "hold water." It was, in fact, a thoroughly unjustifiable piece of filibustering, and as it has now been declared illegal the Government should see that justice is impartially meted out to all concerned.</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE gathers on all sides British Prestige. to show that Lord SALISBURY is</p>
        <p>about as unsuccessful, in his dealings with the foreign affairs of the country as Mr. BALFOUR is in his leadership of the House of Commons. At one time the Tories used to speak of Lord SALISBURY as a " vigorous" foreign secre- tidy, but we hear very little of that kind of talk now. The vigour has been so little in evidence lately that in some quarters British prestige was never lower than it is to-day. For some time past a series of events—which chiefly take the form of massacres of helpless Christians—have occurred in the Turkish Empire and which may at any time bring about very serious results. At one time British influence was paramount at Constanti- nople; now, there is no other of the great powers of Europe treated with aO little respect. The Turks' disregard for this coun- try is shown in different ways. A few months back Lord SALISBURY found that his interposition on behalf of the Armenians was entirely futile; now, British residents in Tur- key are complaining bitterly that they are treated with less respect than the residents belonging to any other nationality. In a letter to the " Times " a correspcndent, who says he is the exponent of British public opinion in Turkey,strongly protests against the manner in which the grievances of British residents are dealt with by our Govern- ment. This correspondent cannot understand why he as an Englishman in Constantinople should not have his London newspaper delivered to him in the same way as French residents receive the newspapers from Paris. The" Times" was in- terdicted by the Turkish authorities because it contained a speech by Lord SALISBURY, yet the "Temps" containing the same speech in full wits delivered through the French post. The reason is not far to seek. The English Government is weak and yielding while the French authorities stand upon their rights and refuse to comply with all the whims of the Sultan. The result is that Turkish officials tamper in all sorts of ways with the letters of British subjects while French and Austrian correspondence is never molested. "The feeling of contempt for British interests thus engendered in the Turkish mind has," writes the correspon- dent referred to, "reached such a culminating point that it constitutes I serious danger and must end in a catastrophe some day." It is really time that Lord SALISBURY displayed some of his vigonr in his transactions with the "unspeakable Turk."</p>
        <p>The financial result of the Cycle Parade at Run- corn, has exceeded the most sanguine expectations of the committee, for not only was there amassed a total of £12,4, but, after deducting expenses, none of which can be cballeaged, the fund will have a clear £lll. The exertions of the Cycle Club and its helpers are responsible in the main for this cred- itable result, and the public theragel yes may feel that in the establishment of any ho.pitql which may take place they have had a good hand in help- ing the institution, fill ncially. It now remains for the fund to be enlarged to en extent that will per- mit the taking of suitable pnmises and the furnishing of them for bo4pital purposes. Added to this there will be the expense of maintaining the institution, so that the good work is only now commenced and remains to be carried on. It has beeika matter of talk for years; now the time has arrived for action. The fund is ovned and has got a good financial start and on the action of the public locally—because it must be a local institution —depends now the wished-for end. •</p>
        <p>The Sunday sacred concert held last week at Run- corn brings out, the cpestion of the poq=ibility of arran2ing a Aeries covering every Sunday for the summer seagon. It has been done in other towns and there is nothing to prevent, it in Runcorn. There are a nurob?r of hands in the town which would no doubt be willing to give services and take a fair share of the work.</p>
        <p>The happy result of tbe parade brings to mind the alleged dullness or trade in the town. One would be inclined to think from the collections made, from the attendances at football matches an from the number of people who travel weekly out of the town, that trade is not so bad in Runcorn after all May it not be possible that Runcorn is only suffer- ing AR other towns bbive suffered all over the coun- try through the recent wave of depression which has passed over it, and may it not be further sup- posed that like other towns it is slowly but steadily righting itself?</p>
        <p>This week .the Runcorn Co-operative Society announce that they will pay a dividend at the rate of 24. HI in the .C, which is ld. mare than that of the last half-year and twopence increase on the corre4ponding period last Year. Here is another in- stance of the prosperity of a purely democratic in- stitution at a time when had trade is the cry of the fathers of the town and everyone knows the effect of bad trade first sbowi itself through the purchas- ing public.</p>
        <p>•In a town like Runcorn cat through with waterways like a second and miniature Venice drowning accidents are sure to happen frequently. This week two fatalities have occurred in which the lives of a captain and a mate have been lost under similar circumstances. In addition to this, another bathing fatality was just prevented by the timely and extremely plucky action of a youth named Riley. Ile, at inconvenience to himself, and with risk to his life, plunged into the canal on Sunday, and brought to land a youngester who had got out of his depth. Riley not only deserves, praise but a tangible reward, and it is to be hoped that the matter will be put before the proper authorities.</p>
        <p>The Fedeiation of Master Cotton Spinners held their annual meeting on Tuesday. In the report it was stated that "Lancashire employers are tired of the trade, and moit firrns would get out of it if they could realise at anything like a reasonable price."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE.</p>
        <p>The district nurse repr,rts that from June 25th to July 25th she has made 250 visits to cases and 911 occasional visits; a total in all of 334, SUCCESS OF A RUNCORN ORGANIST. _</p>
        <p>Mr. William Cooke who is well-known in- Runcorn, Widnes and district has successfully qualified and has been elected an assoc;ate of the Royal College of organists and received his diploma.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN</head>
        <p>ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN</p>
        <p>The adult members of the St: Michael's choir tra- velled to Chester last Saturday per waggonette. Tea was served at the Pied Bull Hotel, after which the party vi.ited the many points of interest in the city, and spent an enjoyable afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT SUTTON.</head>
        <p>THE ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT SUTTON.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning before Mr. F. J. Norman at the Runcorn Police Court, Matthew Healey was again brought 9p on a charge of attempting to commit suicide by cutting his throat with a razor at Sutton on July 14th. The defendant was remanded until the same afternoon. He was then examined by Dr. Selby, who certified him to be insane and Healey was handed over to Mr. Cooke the relieving officer and afterwards taken to the Lunatic Asylum at Upton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WALKING MATCH AT RUNCORN</head>
        <p>WALKING MATCH AT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon a walking match from Run- corn to Hale across Widnes Marsh took place between Mr. T. Banks,hairdresser,Runcorn, and Mr. R. Richard- son,dra per's assistant. After passing Mathieson's Works Richardson gave out through a pain at the heart and the contest was stopped. The pedestrians and their friends proceeded on to Hale but it is understood that a fresh match will be arranged. CHURCH DAY SCHOOLS.--ANNUAL SERMONS.</p>
        <p>These sermons were preached in St. Paul's Church last Sunday, the morning address being given by the Rev. Stanton Jones and in the evening by the Rev. W. H. Staples, M.A. In the afternoon there was a children's service with an address by the Vicar. The collections were for the Parish Church Day Schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>THEFT FROM A RUNCORN BOATMAN.</head>
        <p>THEFT FROM A RUNCORN BOATMAN.</p>
        <p>Frederick Jackson. aged 12, and Het% ert Whitehead, 9, were charged at olanchester on Saturday with steal- ing a pony belonging to Job Jones, a Runcorn boatman. The pony was taken from a stable in Back Piccadilly, Manchester, the door of which was unfastened. The lads were seen with the animal, one of them being upon its back. In answer to the charge the lads said they had taken the pony for a drink. The Bench discharged the youn_ sters, and told the parents to take better care of them in future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN CO-OPERATP7E SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATP7E SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The committee of the above society have just issued their 138th half-yearly report in which it is shown that th. glees amounted to £42,426 183., as compared with £44,120 104. 61 of the previous six months. After allowing five p4-r cent, for depreciation, £950 interest on share capital, £l6 143. 11d, interest on, bank claims, and £4 is. 11d, interest on loan-holders' claims there is a di.-ponal balance of £6,116 18i. lid., wl.ich gives the committee power to recommend a-dividend of 23. 11d.in the £. This is one penny more than the last half-year, and twopence increase on the corresponding period last year.</p>
        <p>GREENWAY-ROAD PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday at this chapel two sermons were preached by Councillor Peters of Buckley, and in the afternoon a service of song, entitled "4 Rough Diamond," was given by the choir. The connective readings were read by Councillor Wilson. At the evening service special music was given by the choir. Mr. A. Shingler rendered the solo "Calvary," and Mr. Seewball ,gave the sob" A dream of paradise. The soloists for the anthems were Miss Hillidge and Miss J. Stubbs, Messrs. H. Woodwtrd and F. Shingler. Mr. W. Grounds presided at toe organ. There was a good attendance at all the services and the collections taken were in excess of last year.</p>
        <p>LOCAL SHOOTING</p>
        <p>The fourth (gold medal) competition of the year took place on Saturday under very unpleasant conditions. Under the circumstances tall scoring was not to be ex- pected and out of the eight who faced the batts Ser- geant Harvey came out at the top with 90 points. Scores :</p>
        <p>509 690 To-</p>
        <p>Rank and Names. yards. yards. yards. &amp;--1, Sergeant W H Harvey 33 ... 32 ... 25 ... 9) Private J Elsa Ards - ... go Lance-, 'orp3ral Bradbury Pi ivate J Hou4nton 23 ... 27 ... 19 ... 74 Lance-Corporal SI) Matthews 3O ... 21 ... 22 ... 73 Private James Sadler 26 ... 24 ... 22 ... 72 Private J Warburton Sergeant W Cooper HOW SEAMEN DRINK.—INTERESTING EVIDENCE AT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The following will be read with interest, following as it does so close on the Coroner's remarks on Wednesday. On Thursday morning before Mr. S. Taylor. J.P., C.C., James Kearns, sailor of the schooner " Donnybrook Fair," was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Percival-lane,Runcorn,about 10.30 on Wednesday night. P.C. Kendrick proved the case, and stated that he saw prisoner previous to ten o'clock and he was then in a condition to take care of himself. Later he found him very drunk with a bottle containing spirits in his pos- session.—Superintendent Okell explained to the Bench that it was a commo practice for sailors to go round the town and when they had got as much as they could, to purchase a quantPy of spirits in a bottle, for the pur- pose f drinkiog either on the way to the ship or on it.— A fine of ss. and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BRAVE RRSCUR FROM DROWNING AT RIINCDRN.</head>
        <p>BRAVE RRSCUR FROM DROWNING AT RIINCDRN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon an exciting incident took place under the Runcr.tri Bridge. A bo% named Waktfi..fld re.siding with his par,nts in Lock-at., was boning with several other lads in the Ship Canal, WakA,ld took a dive off the ferry slip and got into great danger of being carried away with the current. Hie friends seeing he danger toromoqy raised an alarm, and a youth aged 16, son of Mr. Thomas Riley. of Cannnn-it., quickly took off his clothing and plunged into the canal. Being an expert swimmer he soon miched the sinking lad, and seizng him by the hand safely brought him to shore. Riley's action is most commendable, for his exertions undoubtedly prevented another bathing fatality. The youth has been instrumental in saving four lives,nne be- ing his brother, and the crowd which witnessed his plucky rescue of Wakefiald expressed a general desire to see the matter placed before the Humane Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Screceqs or RuNcoarr PUPILS.</head>
        <p>Screceqs or RuNcoarr PUPILS.</p>
        <p>At the (()liege of Preceptors' oeitificate Examination, held last month, all the pupils presented by Mr. Jukes, Churchit.ld !louse School, were successful is gaining certificates. The following are the names of thn pupils with the class of certificate ga'ned:—Lionel A. Meredith, Class HI.. Ist Division, passed in scripture, English lan- guage, English history, geography, algebra, arithmetic, Latin and drawing; T. Arthur Chadwick, Class If 1., Ist Division, passed in scripture, English language, English history, geography, algebra. arithmetic (class TI.), and drawing (etas; H.) ; Harold Gamon, Class 111., 2nd Division, pasQed in scripture, English language, English history, geography, algebra, arithmetic and Latin; Randal Wright, Class lii., 2 ii Division, passed in scrip- ture, English language, Euglish history, geography, algebra, arithmetic and drawing. FUNERAL OF THE LATH MR, EDWARD HARRISON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon tne remains of the l;t-WMr. E Harrison, of 113. Green war-road, Runcorn, were in- terred in the Cemetery. The -first portion of the funeral ceremony was conducted in St. Michael's Church,where the deceased acted as sidesman, by Revs. Canon Wood. and H. B. Firth. Suitable music including the Dead March in. " Saul" was rendered by the organist, Mr. Timmins, jun., while the funeral party enteted the church. At the church the sad possession was joined by Messrs. J. H. Weston and Mellor (wardens), A. Light. burn, T. Clarke, W. Cropper,James Sherlock (sidemen), W. E. Dudley and A. Moulton (of the office staff of the Bridgewater Undertaking, Sprinch Works), Charlea Pea- cock, P. Deakin, I. Hayes, and S. Boyers (also servants of the Canal Company), and Dr. McKerizie. The coffin was covered by many beautiful wreaths sent by the wife and family of deceased, absent friends, children of Mr. and Mrs. Branch, deceased's mother and sister, Mr. Shot e. Mr. Knox and family, and the wardens, and sidesmen of St. Michael's Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. A. BUCK, RUNCORN</head>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. A. BUCK, RUNCORN</p>
        <p>We have this week to record the death otMr. Alfred Buck, aged 68 years, well-known in Runcorn as a chem- ist and druggist, carrying on business at 77, Bridge-st. Deceased came to the town 42 years ago, taking up the business of the late Mr. Lewis, which he successfully conducted up to the time of his death, which took place on Friday morning. Some years ago, Mr. Buck took an active interest in townswork and hld office oil the Board of Commissioners and also acted as manager of the ' Savings Bank. Be was churchwarden at Trinity Church at which place of worship he was a constant attendant and having passed thr 'ugh the various offices of the Ellesmere lodge of Freemasons was the oldest P.M.associ- ated with the brethren. The (funeral took place on Monday and many of the local tradesmen en route to the cemetery closed their establishments as a mark of respect. 'I he precession was headed by Messrs. Joseph Pritchard, A. Lightburn, J. Horsfield, J. H. Weston, F. Beeck, and M. Barker. After these came the following members of the Ellesmere Lodge of Freemasons : —Bros. G. Hail- wood, C. H. Hicks, W. E. Lancaster, Fox, T. Wright. E. Newton, T. H. Annett, W. Hankinson, G. Moss, W. Mellor, W. R. Wass, J. Coxhill, R. Wright and G. Hayes. At the Cemetery Mr. W. Whittaker joined the cortege. In the first coach rode Mr. Harry Buck (son), Dr. Ormsby and Dr. McDougall. The second carriage con- tained the Rev. W. &amp;holes and Mr. F. A. Lake. The c,-,ffin bore wreaths Placed there by the widow and chil- dren (Harry, Clara and Eva), and Mr. Hayes and family. The officiating clergy were the Rev. Canon Wood and the Rev. W. Scholes. SUNDAY SCHOOL rtiRATS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Several Sunday school treats were arranged and took place during the week. Monday last saw the scholars attending the S. John's Presbyterian and Primitive Methodist Schools travelling to Frodsham where, the day being fine, a capital day was spent. , On Tuesday the schnlare attending Mill Brow School visited the same place, and on Wednesday the scholars of Bethesda Con- gregational and Camden Wesleyan Schools spent several</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>hours in the neighbourhood of Overton Hills. On Mon- day. the junior members of the choir of the Runcorn Parish Church went eo Southport, while on Wednesday the members of the senior male class of the Parish Church Sunday Schools journeyed to Llandudno and there spent an enjoyable day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DROWNING CASES AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DROWNING CASES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning Daniel Banks, aged 46, of Pres- ton, captain of the "K, zia Harrison "was found float- ing in the old basin of the docks, Runcorn, about six o'clock in the morning, fie was last s.-en by another captain about midnight, and he is supposed to have fallen into the canal while getting on his boat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>INQUEST.</head>
        <p>THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>1 On Tuesday morning, Mr. Worsley held an inquest at the Police Station, Runcorn, tcuching the death of Daniel Banks, who was found drowned in the Bridge- water Canal on Saturday. Thomas Woodward, master of the " Sarah Jane," of Barrow-hi-Furness, said he knew deceased who was 46 years old, and was master of the szhooner, " Kezia Harrison," ot Preston. He last saw deceased alive at 11.10 on Friday night. He was then crossing the bottom locks 150 yds from his vessel. The ves- sel was lying in the old docks, and he (witness) thought there was some of the crew on board. Deceased said he was going on board. Deceased thrugh he had had drink was capable of taking care of himself. Mc) night was fine. Deceased would get on board from the quay, and the vessel was close to it. There would be no diffi- culty in getting on board if there was a ladder don .He (witness) went back to his vessel and did not hear any- thing more.—James Payne, 10, Peel-st., Runcorn, mas- ter of a vessel saw deceased alive at 8 o'clock on Friday night. Deceased had engaged him as mate. At that time he was sober and was going with his brother-in-law. Witness bad j iined the ship and went there on Saturday morning, a few minutes after six. The vessel was lying on one side of the quay and he found on board an ordinary seaman and the cook. He did not see anything of the Captain who had not been to his berth that night. S ien alter he and a fireman noticed something in the water, :and they found it was the captain. The deceased was not fully dressed. They sent for the police and deceased's brother- in-law. The men on board said they had never heard the captail come on board, or see him. His (witness's) idea was that deceased had got on b .ard and fallen over from the side.—P.C. Butler, re •eived information about the finding of the body,and found no m irks of violence. Deceased's watch had stopped at sighteen minutes to five, there was 3id. in money on deceased.—The Coroner briefly reviewed the evidence and said that it was a very sad occurrence. The deceased was a man in the prime faf life. He was sorry tor the wife of the deceased in the severe loss she had sustained:by the loss of her husband. —The j'ury returned a verdict to the effect that deceased was found drowned in the Bridaewarer Canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MATE DROWNED.</head>
        <p>A MATE DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Another similar fatality was -Verified on Tuesday morning' by the finding of the body of John Hughes of Amlwich, North Wales,in the Runcorn D )ck. The de- cemed was paid off from the British Queen on Saturday and, missing his train for North Wales, went to some friends of his on the steam.r " Baton," lying in the dock. A bunk was provided for him and he was seen to undress himself preparatory to retiring. The next morning the hunk was f lund to be vacant though de- ceased's clothes were there .nd nothing more was seen of him until the br,dy was found on Tuesday by Mr. Shaw (seamen's missionary) who dragged the water adjacent in company with Mr. Tonks and two sailor friends. The body was taken to the mortuaty on the police ambul- ance.</p>
        <p>Mr. Worsley held the inquest on the body of John Jones on Wednesday morning, at the Runcorn Police Station.--Robert P. Hughes, mate on board the " Baltic," of Liverpool. said he had known deceased for some time and he was ahout 35 years old. Dlceased belonged to the British Queen and was paid off that vessel on Satur lay. The -ame night he met deceased about eight o'clock and they spent Vie evening together. They then went on board the" Baltic " at 11.30 which was lying in the new docks in the Bridgewater Canal. Deceased went into the forecastle and took his jacket off and made it into a pillow. The cook was in the fore- castle with them. He did not hear deceased move in the night but when he got up on Sunday morning the deceased was missing. They did not make a search as they thought deceased would turn up, hut subsequently they gave information to the police. Deceased was very drunk on Saturday morning. Thomas French,cook on the "Baltic," said he saw de- ceased in the forecastle sitting up talking with Hughes. It would be about twelve o'clock. He did not hear de- ceased in the night and he was missing when the cap- tain called him at 8 30. Deceased's cap and jacket were hanging in the bunk.</p>
        <p>William Shaw, seamen's missionary,stated that he re- covered the body on fues.lay from the new dock of the Bridgewater Canal. The bo.iy had no jacket on, and no cip,and the trousers were down about the legs. He in- formed the police immediately. Mr. Shaw, on conclud- ing his evidence asked if,in cases were sailors having no friends and having only been in the water a short time, the bodies could not be taken to the Institute. He had had a letter from the wife of deceased stating that she was unable to come as she had four children and asking him to make the best arrangements for the burial of her husband.—The Coroner, in reply, said the matter was entirely at the discretion of the police but the mor- tuary was established for the purpose of receiving the bodes and it was the proper place for that purpose par- tinelarly when the body was in the water on the Satur- day night or Sunday morning. It was, however, a mat- ter entirely at the discretion of the police.—Mr. Shaw Is that in cases where there are no friends.— The Coroner : No, I take it that it is pro- vided as a matter of health for sanitary pur- poses. I may say I was instrutnental in getting the mortuary established because of chie tiorritile siehts and things I saw where bodies had to be removed ma state of decomposition to private places.—P.C. Kendrick said that he examined the body and found no marks of vio- lence except a slight discolouration on the right, 'I he Coroner : Anthing serious.—Witness : No sir.— Coroner: Thee I may say there were no marks of vio- lence. Can you express an opinion as to whether it was caused before or after death.—Witness : No sir.—The Coroner : It was not a serious bruise.—Witness : No air.—The Coroner: Was there any money on the de- ceased.—Witness : Os. 11.1 d. and a pipe and a knife. The Coroner in putting the casa to the jury said that the fazts were similar to the inquest held in that room the day before (Tuesday). D?ath was evidently occa- sioned in both cases through the SAME' Cause. It was a singular thing that they should have on two consecutive day, inquests of preckely the same character. In his opinion he thought the police should make inquiries from these class of men as to where th se persons got the drink with a view to teeing if something could not be done to prevent the supply of intoxicating liquor to sea- men who had recently been paid off and e.rtninly could not have been in a condition to be sopplied with at least the latter portion of the drink as those men had. The jury returned a verdict of" F-oind drowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BOW LI N G.</head>
        <p>BOW LI N G.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH V. HALTON.—A main of bowls be- tween the St. Nekton Heath and Halton clubs was com- pleted on the'bowling green Saracen's Head, Stockton Heath, On Saturday. The Ii et portion of the game was played at Halton on July 11th, when the Halton team won. The return match was played on Saturday, when the Stockton Heath team were victorious, but on the two games the Halton team had the majority. Raiscoart V. ST. ANNE'S GLUE.—On Wednesday at Runcorn the home team, secured a decisive victory over the St. Anne's team a pleasant afternoon's play resulting as follows :</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>T. ANNE'S.</p>
        <p>Dean 2l Guy 9 Wrlkht 2l Houghton T Richardson 2l Cryer T Woods 2l Walton S Dag:eish S Johnson 2l Clarke R Richardson 7 Ackers 2l Madgett ..... 13 R Brim le II Treacly/ell .. 210</p>
        <p>Tot al</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>Majority for Runcorn, 110</p>
        <p>LANcasntim V. YORICSHIRI3.-4 ilk important engage- ment was played at Clecktioatoti on Saturday uader most unfavourable conditions. th 3 rain having made the green difficult to play upon. It will be seen that the Warrington contingent were very successful. Scores : LkNCASHIRE. ""I"King• J J ?if eAkin, Kersal 2l J Davis, Hudderafleld ...... 12 J. W G Coomb, Witliton 2l E Hargreaves, Skiptou ... 10 S Eckerson, Otftham W J. Arnold jun.. Timperley l6 A Brooks, 01,ckheaton ... 21 H Lonsdale. Withington J. Crowther,Harpurhey...... 20 J Middleton. Span W Wells, Heaton Mersey... 21 W Westwooi, Ossett W Shaw. Warrington 3 2l JB. ooks, Cleeklunton ... 17 F Westwell, Withington W Fielding, Lr Broughton 21 H Singleton, Mirfield l6 A Thompson, Urmstem 2l If Roberts, Cleckheaton... 13 J Codling Irghton C 2l H Pearson, Siren IL Williamion. Witiewtou... 21 T Sykes, Low Moor C Worsencroft, Didsbury ... 21 P Bentley, Even jHopewell, 7.r. Irghton... 21 Xlfirst. Spen .. F Berry. Warrington 3 2l S Royston, Ihrddersfield 14 p Hackett, Warrington S2l W W sharp. Misaad. l4 J Allis,on, Warrington N 2l T Benton, Chapeltovin... 19 J Isherwood. Rade.iffe 6 S Rarniden. Clifton 2l J Williamson, Newton l7 C Booth, Mirfield ' 2l F Knowles. Clieethoin 2l A Verity, Dew.burv...... 11 T Bolton, Warrington N l2 C Hint, Huddersfield... 21 A J Jones, Warrington N 2l H Squires. Clifton l4 F Shaw, Tyldesley J Is.lez wood, Leigh 3 Brimelow, Waegton N 2l 3 Mcorhouse, Skipton ... 15</p>
        <p>506 Intor-eouuty Resulte.</p>
        <p>1893—Played at Brighouse and Warrington. Majority for Lancashire 120 1894—Played at Choetham Hill and Huddersfield. Majority for Lancashire 138. 1895—Played at Mirfieid and Higher Broughton. Majority • for Yorkshire 76. 1898—Played at Didsbury and Cleckheaton. Majority for Lancashire 214.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB COTTAGE HOSPITAL FUND.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon a large gathering took place on Runcorn Hill where the band of the 2nd (E C) V. B. Cheshire Regiment gave a selection of sacred music. The band was conducted by Sergeant Outhwaite, the pro- gramme being :—March, " Le Lion de St. Marc" G. Fabiani ; chorus, "The heavens are telling" Haydn : cone," The Holy City" Adams ; fantasia, " Eureka H. R iund ; selection " War M-irch of the Priests (Atnalie) Mendelssohn ; chorus, " Hallelujah " Handel.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the coll-ctors and the amounts tak,n :-Miss Crowley, Ll 9. ' • Mr. W.ll. Mundy, £l7s 611. ;Mr. W. H. Mack, £l-65.51-1; Mr. H. Writ, £i 4, 81.; Mr. Elarry Price, 184. 21.; Miss Abel, 17s. 9 1.; Mr. F. W. Hancock, 14s. 811.; Mr. Frank Streeton, 13; 71- I.; Mrs. Whinyates, 13s. 31; Miss Broadshaw, 13; 211.; Mr. A. Grounds,lls. 10,41.; Miss F. Ab,4l, 84. 1t; tvlr. L. Whinyates, 64. 811. Miss May Aitkin, 7. 811.; Miss Dutton, 6s. 11d1. Miss M. Barnes, 54. 911. Mr. J. J. Neild, 4s. 911 ; total, £l2 9s. Bd. The many subscribers to this fund will probably be pleased to hear that the receipts have been more than was anticipated at the outset. The Treasurer (Mr. H. Wright) has worked out the receipts to be as follows, from all sources. Subscriptions: Sir J. T. tirunner,£lo ; Mrs. Soull v, £33;. ; other eubscriptions, £1 14s. ; adve.tisements on programmes, £5-14s. ; c.eitributions to the prize fund, £l3;. 6,1. ; hr sale of matches, 64. 21.; sale of linneorn rock toffee, £2B;. 611. ; proceeds of concert, £6 Os. 51.; sale of programmes, £9 12;. 11d. ; street collection, £63 7s. 51. ; fixed collecT.inl, boxes, £6 16m. 21-1 ; receipts from printing in streets, £l4;. 6d.; open air Sunday concert, £l2 14s. 1011. ; total, £124 6s. id. MEETING OF WORKERS.</p>
        <p>A well attended meeting of workers was held at Riley's Cafe, Runcorn on Tuesday night, Mr. Munday occupy- ing the chair, being supported by Mr. W. Mack, secre- tary, and Mr. B. Wright, treasurer to the fund. The business was to hear Mr. Wright's statement, the list of receipts bring read by that gentleman as printed atove. In their exnenses they had to par for advertising, 104.; printing, £7 lls. U.; przes, £1 2i. 61.; materials for collecting p )les, etc., 103. 8,1 ; reireshments, 9s. 4d.; Mr. B. 'Previa, for Runcorn rock toffee, £1 44.; postages, wages, judges' expenses, etc., 164. ; Primitive school- room (hire), 10s.; making a total of £l2 13. 101., and leaving in hand a balance of £lll 174. 31., which was the actual result of the parade. Mr. Wright pointed out that none of the sub: cribers'inoney had been touched but that their expenses had been paid out of too advertise- ments, printing, sale of toffee,matches, and programmes and there was a balance even out of that to go to the general fund.—Mr. Crawfcrd moved the adoption of the treasurer's report, and the passing of the accounts and Mr. Rushton seconded. The motion was carried.— A vote of thanks was proposed by Mr. Mack to Mr. Wright, and Mr. Clare seconded.—Mr. Wright in reply, said he had tried to do his best and if they were satisfied he was. The result had been gratifying to everyone who had assisted and he could say for himself that he did not do the work for thanks but b. cause he liked it. (Ap- plause.) It there was anyone who deserved their thanks it was their secretary whi had displayed untiring efforts at every turn, and he would have great pleasure in pro- posing that a vote of thanks be accorded Mr. Mack —Mr. F. Hancock, briefly seconded the resolution, and Mr. W. Mack, in acknowledging,thank2d the many workers who had assisted the committee and officials to bring the parade to a successful issue.— the proceedings shortly afterwards terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>COLLEGE OF PRECEPTOR? EXAMINATION.—In the list of successful candidates for the Junior Form. College of Preceptors' Examination held in June, appears the name of E. M. Tdey, a pupil at Holly Bank School, Frodsham.</p>
        <p>TEcatsicar. COMMITTEE. This committee met on Thursday evening, when there were present :—Messrs. C. E. Linaker (chairman), J.M. Williams (treasurer), E. Rhodes, and J. Jones. The Treasurer stated that during the year ending June 30th he had received £79 185.10 d., and of this sum £54 16s 101. had been paid out, and £l4 19s. 81. was due from the committee. The accounts were passed on the motion of Mr. Williams,seconded by Mr. Jones.— Mr. Williams referred to the good work that had been done by the committee since its formationiand the Secretary was authorised to convene a public meet- ing of the rateplyers to appoint a new committee and to receive a report of the work done prior to the commence- ment of the coming season. Messrs. T. Rigby, C.C.,and T. B ixter, C.C., were invited to be present. INTERESTING WEDDING.—OR Wednesday afternoon a marriage, in which unusual interest was taken, was solemnised at the Frodsham Parish Church. The con- tracting parties were Mr. Arthur Henry Falkner, son of Mr. Robert Falkner, of Castle Hill, Kingsley, and Miss Adelende B. Steele, only daughter of Dr. Steele, J.P., late of Runcorn, now residing at Onger Hall, Frodsham. The officiating clergy were the Rev. C. Steele, brother of the bride, of Sharrow, Sheffield, and the Rev. H. B. Blogg, vicar of Frodsham. The service was fully choral. The bride wore a white satin dress, trimmed with tulle with duchesse train. She also were a wreatta of real orange blossoms and a diansond bracelet the gift of the bridegroom, and carried a beautital bouquet. She was attended by Mr. Fl. Wilson, of Seaforth, cousin, Miss Margery Wilson, Broughton Park, Manchester, niece of the bridegroom, and Miss Grace Weatherhead, of Ere- wont, cousin, each of whom were prettily attired. After the service a reception was held at the residence of the I.N ide's parents, which was attended by 200 guests. The presents were varied and numerous. The honeymoon is being spent in North Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>FRODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. P. Sp:-!akman and T.</p>
        <p>DAMAGE TO PROPERTY.—WaIter Booth, Arnett Booth, Edward Worrell, and J. Yarwood, four boys, were brought up at the instance of Mr. J. W. Lighthurn on a charge of doing wilful damage to property at Newtown, Frodsham, to the extent of £2 10i., on or about June 25th.—Mr. Lightburn said the property belonged to the United Lancashire and Cheshire Building Society, and they had suffered considerable loss from damage done to it. They had an estimate in at the nresent time for structural repairs to one house for £5 ss. The °hint of the proceedings was to prevent the wilful &amp;image to the property. The boys were not content with breaking windows but entered the house and committed damage —P.C. Price said that, in consequence of complaints, he had made inquiries and the boy Yarwood made a state- ment to him.—Yarwood was discharged on making a statement incriminating the other defendants, and the cases against the other three were dismissed the magis- trates remarking that if they came arain on a similar charge they would he severely dealt with. AN ASSAIILT.—John Leach and William Cobb were summoned for assaulting a boy named Peter Sutton, of Alvanlay, on July 17th.—Complainant's evidence was that he went to Mr. Snelson's farm and the two defend- ants caught him and struck him several times with the open hand. Complainant when questioned as to the cause of the assault admitted that it was because he had been taking gooseberries. Under the circumstances Mr. Barton who appeared to defend was not called upon and the case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>ColwAll, Joseph Dtvies, and Robert Garner, of Frodsham, and Joseph Jones,of Ffelsby, were fined for being drunk and disorderly at different dates. A CYCLIST FlNED.—James McGeener, of Chester, was ordered to pay the costs of a summ me for riding with- out a lighted lamp at Etelsby on June 29th at 10.15 p.m.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL 'lO LLANDUDNO BRAUMARIS, BANGOR. AND MENAI BRIDGL. DAILY SAILLN GS from Prince's Landing Stage (Sunday's excepted and circumstances permitting).</p>
        <p>4 6 Q OT. r UDNO "</p>
        <p>Leaves Liverpool 10-45 a.m., arrives back 7-30 p.m. POPULAR AFTERNOON EXCURSIONS TO AND FROM LLANDUDNO. Passengrrs by the " St. Elvies." leaving Liverpool on Mimdays, Tuesd aye. Fridays and Saturday., at 2-15 p.m.. can connect la itta the "St. TudnJ " leavang LLinducluo at 5-15 p.m., and arrive back in Liverpeol at 7-3.. p.m. SPECIAL REDUCED PARES FOR PARTIES. For Fores and further particulars apply to LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES SUCAMSHIP CO., LTD.. T. G. BR ,W, becretary, 20. WA fHit-ST., LIVERPOOL. Tel. 6366; or to PEERS BRIMELOW, Horse and Jockey, War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ASTON.</head>
        <p>ASTON.</p>
        <p>Tax BAZAAtt. —This event proved very successful from a financial point of view inasmuch as upwards of £3OO was realized. The provision of an organ and organ i Ichamber for the Aston Parish Church is the end n view ! of the Bazaar C-nnmittee, and they now have four-fifths ' of the required amount which is estimated at £5OO MEETING OF WORICERS.—A meeting of stallholders at the bazaar held last week in Aston Hall took place on Tuesday the vicar occupying the chair. The amount of money at the disposal of the churchwardens for the new organ and renovation of the church was set down at £4OO, made up .by £4O balance from the restoration fund, £lOO jumble sale and subscriptions, and £2BO nett proceeds of the bazaar. After considerable discussion it was decided to build a new organ and a chamber to con- tain it at a cost not to exceed £3OO and the balance of £lOO is to:be spent in erecting choir stalls in the church. A committee was appointc.d to consider and report as to probable cost of carrying out the work. A vote of thanks having been passed to the chairman, the meeting ter- minated.</p>
        <p>WANTRII, GENERAL, for private family ; plain cooking, washing and ironing; good character indispensable; wages £lB.—Mrs. haulm, Wyndhurat, Fairhaven, Lytham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH BRASS BAND.—The annual meeting of this successful institution took place last week end, the Bev. W. H. Stables occupying the chair. There was also a good attendance of members. The Chairman, in cbn- gratulating the members on their success, said the band had got ea well indeed, thanks to the energy of Mr. Walter Tudor, and was one of the best in the district as well as one of the most flourishing of his Church So,:ie- ties. He felt very proud of the Church Band, and hoped it would always maintain its present position. After- wards the following gentlemen were elected to form (with the curate and sidesmen ex•officio) the committee of management: Rev. W. H. Stables (president).Messrs. E. Imison, C.C., T. H. Williams, T. T. Sill, J. Tudor, T. Owen, W. Hope, G. Tudor, and W. Tudor.</p>
        <p>WHY SUFFER ONE .N.O3iENT from torturing, dis- fig,uring akin diseases ,when a warm bath with CIITICURA SoM. and a single application of CUTICURA (ointinenr) the great skin cure, will afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy, permanent, and eco- nomical cure, when the best physicians and all other remedied fail. No preparations ever compounded in tho history of medicine have performed the wonderful cures dailf made by CIITICIMA remedies. They are in truth the greatest skin cures, blood purifiers, and humour remedies of modern times, and appeal to these who have suffered long and hopelessly, with irresistible force.</p>
