      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MORtle INQUESTS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MORtle INQUESTS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>FATALITIES TO WORKMEN. At the Parade Room, Irlam-st., on Wednesday last week, Mr. F. A. Jones conducted an. enquiry relativo to the death of James Graham, 10, Haydock-st. who died as the result of a fall while at his work at the Wt.ite- ercss Company's Works. Mr. Fansbaw appeared for the Whitecross Company.—Margaret Graham, wife of the deceased, said the latter was a wire-roller, aged 41 last birthday. The right leg was a wooden one. Be left home to go to his work at Whitecross Works on Wednes- day at twenty minutes pas' 5 p.m. He was in good health. Lie returned at 7-40 the next morning, and made no complaint until Friday when he was called up. Be said he felt very ill with pains at the back of the head and chest. It w..s the "last turn" and he went out to his work at a quarter to four. On Saturday morning when he returned he said he had a tall on Wednesday night. His wooden leg bad caught some iron and he had fallen and struck his head against a box. Deceased went to Dr. Fox at night and lost consciousness at 12.30 on Sun- day nig t. Be died at 8.20 on Monday night. It was nineteen years since be lost his leg and he had worked 24 years for the Whit ecross Cornpany.—Richard Graham, nephew of deceased, said he also worked at Whitmeross and was there with deceased last Wednesday. Deceased commenced about 5.30. Witness saw the accident happen when deceased was turning the last" hole." He had got the wire too long and it stuck in the top of his tongs; ho was trying to get it back:and it was dropp- ing at the back of his neckwhen be ducked acd his wooden leg tripped him. Hir head '.truck an iroh box but he went on with his work and made no complaint. —John Birchal, fellow-workman of deceased gave simi- lar evieence.—By Mr. Fanshaw : The box w.is abut two yards from deceased and out of the wai . There was no grease on the iron plate deceased stood upol, and nothing dangerous anout.—Mr. Fanshaw said the box was there for the deceased and the man with him. They needed it handy for their use. He saw no necessity for it to he removed.—Tte jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death" and suggested that such boxes be removed 20 yards away.</p>
        <p>The deputy coroner then held rib inquest on the body ef James Leigh, who was drowned in the Meney by falling from a ps-ntoon.—Mr. Browne appeared for Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited. Deceased was an employe of theirs. Mr. Newell ac- cninpanied Mr. Browne.—Maria Leigh, wife of deceased, 10 Powys-street. Warrington, said he was a ship's car- penter and aged 51. He left, home on Tuesday at 6 a.m. fip serlked f r Crosfield's and had been subject to epilep- tic fita for years. He had had them oftener lately. When he entered the firm's employ he was well and she thought they did not know be was subject to them.— Frederick Bennett; another employe ut Crosfield's was working with deceased on Tuesday. They were bring- ing a pdntonn down the river from Welton Arches to Bank Quay. At 8.30 they. had reached a quarter ot a mile on the lower aide of the Arches when witneßs said " here's the fl,iod coming." D-ceased said " we shall cet flood when we get back,for being so long." Witness said it could not be helped. Witnet.s saw deceased then fold his arms, lift his foot up and fall into the river. He came to tile 6urfuce and witness put a boat hook to him and shouted to Lim hut he took no notice. He then threw a rope twicu and deceased maee one movement with his hands and sank. The.works were halt-a-mile off, and when be got there witness gave information. Witness had seen dee^.-“e.d in three or four fits before, but never like the or,, ~n Tuesday. He was on land on the other occasions.—Br Mr. Browne: The manager did not know they had cone ou the pontoon. Witness was ordered to boulk a boat, hut it had gone and they went on the pontoon. The Manager of the department did not know that deceased had fits.—Wi ham Hunter said that he saw the body of deceased recovc red at 10 30, and there was about eight feet of water. He was quite &amp;ad. Dr. Cannel' was. there and lonsii;ered &amp;ceased had died before he ~r,c,t into the water Is none crime from his rnouth.—The jury found that deceased died on the river Merfiey in an epileptic fit.</p>
        <p>A fire broke out at wieicigi.t., ou shop at Ilfracomh, Tuesday in a turni ture - and it spread so rapidly that in a few hours thirty-four houses and shops were destrty,-ed. 1h?. oss of the property is stated at nearly £lOO,OOO.</p>
      </div>
