        <head>MOST READERS</head>
        <p>MOST READERS</p>
        <p>Have had some experience of sick rooms, either as patients or attendants. How many such are there who have not felt the weakness or debility which follows the sickness, more discouraging than even the weakness it- self? Th e.igh the disease may have given way before the timely application of proper remedies, and though.in ems( quence, no immediate danger is to be apprehended, the slow progress towards convalescence is very dis- heartening co patient and attendant alike. Hew often do we hear people say :7" Yes. The fever has left him. He is free from any immediate danger. But he is so weak. If only he could regain strength ! " This debilitating effect of fever and other diseases is indeed a great source of anxiety to medical men as well as to their patients ; and anything which would tend directly to shorten the period between the departure of the fever and the complete recovery of strength would be naturally welcomed by both doctors and patients. This is one ',f the reasoe.s why Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is so popular, why doctors prescribe it so largely, why chemists sell it so gemerally, why patients have re- course to it so readily, and why everybody has a good word to say for it. Not only does it attack the disease in its stronghold, not only does it drive the evil fr ,m the system, but it gives back directly the strength of which the system has been deprived by illness; it infuses new life into the blood, and the recovery of health and strength, instead of being, as it otherwise would be, dis- couragingly slow, intermittent, and uncertain, becomes, 1 when this excellent medicine is resorted to.encouragingly rapid, regular, and certain. It is therefore confidently recommended as an unrivalled tonic medicine, and may be safely, used by all who suffer from any form of weak- ness or debility from whatever cause it may arise. Mr. J. YOUNG. of 107, Laxton-street, New Brompton, Chatham, writing to a friend, says "I thank you very much for sending me the ' GWILYBI EVANS' BITTERS 'handbill. I have taken two bottles, a 2a. 91. and a 44. 61. It has done me more good than anything I had ever tried before, and I am so pleased with it that I shall lecommend it to every one who comes to see me."</p>
        <p>Gulilynt Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, sold by all Cnomists and Pat Put biledicine Vendors in the Kingdom. !Jed everywhere; praised by evervbody. Agents in all parts of world. Prices—Pottles 2. 91, 4s. 64., cases of three large bottles 12. 61. Sent at above prices free to any address, by parcel post, direct from the Proprietors—</p>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING Co., LTD., LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A SAAULT ON &amp; POLICEMAN.--..A. B fIIITAL CROWD.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court before the Mayor (chairman), Alderman Harrison, and Mr. F. Monks, John Metier, 7, Carlisle-st., was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Dial Curt at a quarter to twelve on Saturday night. He was furth .r charged with assaulting P.C. Clough at the same time and place.—P C. Clough stated that he was on duty in Dial Court on Saturday, and there saw defendant drunk and using bad language. Witness warned him.] He was taken home but came out again and commenced a disturbance in Carlisle-st. He said he did not care for 40 b-- bobbies. Witness seized defendant, who kicked him twice on the left leg, butted him and pulled his whistle off. The latter was put down a grid so that witness could not get assistance. Defendant and himsslf were a considerable time wrestling on the fib et., and people kept coming from the crowd and kicking witness in the body. He had had considerable pain ever since. Eventually Sergeant Cheney came up and rendered assis- tance.—Sergearit Cheney said that when he arrived there was a rough crowd of about 150 people for the constable to contend with. He was very much ex- hausted.—Defendant : I did not kick him intentionally. —The Mayor: They think it fair game to assault a constable.—The Chief Constable said if a constable was by himself there was a certain class of crowd repre- sented typically by defendant, who would assault him in a most brutal manner. He would ask then to deal severely with defendant in this case, as the police were assaulted too frquentiv.—Defendant was sent to gaol for one month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ANOTHER BEGGING LETTER IMPOSTOR SENT TO GAOL</head>
        <p>ANOTHER BEGGING LETTER IMPOSTOR SENT TO GAOL</p>
        <p>_ B fore the magistrates at the Warrington Borough Court on Tuesday, Thomas Russell was charged with begging in Church-st., on Monday evening.—Detective Barlow proved the case, and said he saw prisoner stop several persons and beg from them. Witness went to him and was just in time to prevent him destroying three begging lettets.—The Chief Constable read the letters, which were all similar, as follows :—" Please open this letter. and I will wait the answer. rELOMAS RUSSELL."--" Please pity a poor man with a wife and four children. lam 'out of work. I'am a good trades- man if I could get work to do. lam ;ashamed to beg,so what can Ido for my little ones',sake ? Knock and it shall be opened unto you.' 'Those that give to the poor shall receive a blessing from God,'" The Chief con- stable added that these letters were handed to people from door to door. Inquiries had been made, and it had been found that the nrisener had neither wife nor chil- dren. He had told the police that he lived in Charter- at., Manchester—a street of a very unsavoury character —hut when they offered to telephone to her he said that his wife would not be there now. The prisoner was a sturdy beggar, nothing more nor less.—Prisoner said he had never been locked up before.—The Chief Constable: The letters were written for him by a young lady.—Pri- goner : Let me go to the Workhouse, and I will never de this thing again.—The Chairman: You want to get work P—Prisoner . I do, sir.—The Chairman: Have you ever tried the treadmill P—Prisoner : No sir, I never did ; I have never been in gaol in my life —The Chairman: There is good experience to be got t}aere.— The Chief Constable : It is almost blasphemous to quote Scripture in this way.—The Chairman (to the prisoner): —I think we will send you to the treadmill for a month with hard labour.—Prisoner seemed much upset at the decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. J. H. Fleming (chairman), and Mr. S.</p>
        <p>Wright. HASSALL Y. HassaLL.—Mr. Browne said that the parties in this case had come together and he now asked for the case to be withdrawn.—The magistrates agreed. Row BVTIVERN Ngraanoußs. Daniel Higham was chat ged with assaulting John Crofts, on the 18th inst.--Mr. Browne 'represented complainant, and said both parties lived in Powya 4street and that on Saturday night defendant. called complainant's wife feul names and complainant came out in consequence. Higham then assaulted him.—John Crofts, complainant, who is very much less than defendant, corm- , borated and said Higharn gave him a black eve. His wife came to his as-istance and was also assaulted.—Mrs Crof's also supportedithe evidence.—Witnesses for the defence said complainant butted defendant in the stcrnach with his head. —The Bench s ,id they thought the case was a drunken squabble and dismissed it. A BRUTAL AS4AULT. —Alice Hayes, Barry-st., Latchford. was charged with assaulting Sarah Swift, We h-lane, on the 20th July.—Mr. F. R. Roberts appeared for the complainant, and said that his client was on Monday even.ng in the house of William Lee, Barry-st., where her furniture ,aas stored. She was arranging to take it away when defendant came in and said to complainant " I'll take it out of you for the 'ot." She struck her in the mouth and loosene4 three teeth. Defendant then dragged complainant to the ground and struck her and kicked her. She then went away, but when complainant went out again defendant smut her in Bat ry-et ,and again assaulted her and gave her a bad black ere. Complainant could not understand why she was assaulted as she had not seen defendant before. Mr. Roberts thought that see (def .ndant)believed complainant had madgned her,which was untrue. She had evidently made a huge mistake —M-s. Swift, who was bsdl it bruised, and Mr. Lee,corroborated as to the assault in Lee's house, and Annie Tcdd as to that in Barry-street. The latter witness said defendant was under the influence of drink.—The defendant made no appeara ,ce and was sentenced to 14 days hard labour. Common Assams.—For assaulting Mary Fleming, Dial- it., on the 15th July. Elizabeth Edmond, Diel-caurt (14th appearance), was fined 10s. including • costs.—Eliza McGuin- ness, Worsley-st., was charged with assaulting Eliza Smith, Beweey-read, on the 20th July. Mrs. Smith said defendant struck her on the shoulder. Defendant had to pay the costs. —Thomas Barker. ,5, Providence-place, was charged with assaulting John Delooze, 62, Lythgoe's-lane. Comclainant said that defendant passed on the day in question and spoke to him. He replied and defendant then butted him with his head and struck him. Defendant alleged that complainant had insulted him. Friends of complainant corroborated his statement about the assault.—Defendant was fined 10s. In- c uding costs.</p>
        <p>SITUADAY.—Before Mr„John.Bnrgesa (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Wright:</p>
        <p>A STATION LOAFER IN TROUBLE.—John Heaton, Friare- green, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Parker-st. at 7-43 on Friday night.—P.O. Edwards proved the offence, and prisoner, who had a bad record, was fined ss. and casts, or in defawt seven days. Morms.Y.—Before the Mayor (chairman). Mr. F. Monks, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Alder-rnan Harrison</head>
        <p>Alder-rnan Harrison</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY LIMISE —Mr. Massey, Spread Eagle Hotel, Macclesfield, was granted temporary authorit*, to sell on the Warrington Football ground on Monday next, the occasion of the Rugby Football Club Sports. JULIA ANN SHIELDS' SANITY QUESTIONED. —Julia Ann Shields, Pillings'-court, was brought up on remand charged with being drunk and disorderly in Fennell-street on July 18th.—The Chief Constable said that during the remand the prisoner had ben medically examined as to her sanity Her comduat in the cells and violence, and her frequent appearances before the Court, had convineed him that she was not re.ponsible for her actions. The medical man said that she was quite sane.—The bench commented upon the apparent uselessness of sending prisoner to gaol and re- gretted there was no place where she and similar characters could be permanently kept. They had, however, no alterna- tive but to committ her fora month's hard labour. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. —For being guilty of drunken- ness coupled with disorderly conduct the following persons were dealt with :---Peter Higgins, 10, Ship-yard, fined 10s. and costs; Mary Grimes, no address, sent to gaol for one</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA is both, a stimulating and refresh- ing drink, and a nutritious food, probably more nourishing than any other beverage. It is absolutely puredcontaining no foreign admixture.—Advt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURD AN, AUGUST I 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURD AN, AUGUST I 1896.</p>
        <p>month S,.rah Appleyard 21. Albert-terrace, fined the costs ; Emily Lowe, 3, Stevenson-street, fined the costs. A. BILL POSTER FlNED.—James Smith. bill poster, Coffee Howe, Florsemarke...-st.. was fcharged wit!, causing an ob- struction in Wharf-st., on the 21st July, by allowing a hand- cart to remain there an unusuat time. Defendant made no appearance —P.O Prank Cooper stated that at 6 o'clock on the day in question in consequence of information, that an accident h d OCC u-reA to aOh -shire Lines' van in Wharf- st., he proceeded there ani found a hand,3art in the street with two large ladders Apon it and some buckets and bills. The horse of the van had shiel at these ladders and had backed down the bulk of the river. Witne•s had difficulty in getting it up again Th • handcart was there two hours and 45 minutes.—Sergeant Chantler said that when he served de- fendant with the eummons he said he was unwell and had left the cart —A fine o 103. and costs was imposed. TUESDAY.—Befor, Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair), and Dr. T Starkey Smith. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>INDECFNCY.—Joha e, a iou At looking 3oung man, was charged with indecently exposing his person in Museum- street on Monday with intent to insult certain females.—He pl enilty—P.C. Clough stated that he was at home, near the Old Barracks, Rank Qoay, on Moaday. He saw prisoner about 40 yards off, opp mite the Trinity school. Prisoner wasbehaving in an indecent manner and there were several little girls and b iys about. The childr-n wer ,just tis.n com- ing from school. Witness went up to prisoner and spoke to him, but he gave no explanation of his conduct. He was taken into cust idy —The Ch ef Constable pressed for the full penal y of three month' as such cases were far too common and it was getting hardly safe for young children to be outside unprotected.—Prisoner expressed sorrow for the offence but was sent to gaol for six weeks' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>RUNCORN PE I'TY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PE I'TY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. -Before Messrs. P. Speakman and F. J. Norman. LICENSING 13:-SI5ESS.—J. Jones was granted permission to sell at the Volunteer Arms, Granville-street, lately occupied by Sadler. HEATSICAL Caddick was granted a license to perform stage plays in the Theatre Royal for the en uing year 013.CENE LANGUAGE —John Cavanagh was charged with using obscene language in Nelson-street on July 16th. Defendant pleaded guilty and was fined 53. and 85. 60. costs, or 14 days. AN ASSAULT CASE. George Cornelius Duckett was sum- moned by Chirles Samuel Rose for assaulting him on July 23rd in Union-st. There was a cross summons against Rose for using threatening 1 ngal,ge to Duckett. —Charles Rose said that he was hawking in Union-st. and Dnckett came out of the public-house and threatened if he did not leave the street that he would cut his (R. se's) throat. He also threatened to kick witness out of the street, and then Catiglv hold of him by the throat and nearly throttled him.— Two witnesses were called in support of the assault.—On the other hand Duckett denied toe assault and complained of being an ,oyed by Rose and a boy working with him. Rose threatened him several times. Witnes,es were called by Duckett to prove that no assault took place, but that he was threatened by Rose.—The bench considered that the case was lan unfortunate squ tbble. and that the two parties were just las had as each other. They would he bound Over on sureties I of £lO for six months and pay their own costs.—Defendant was sent to gaol for 11 days. A SUSPICIOUS CHARACTER.—WiIIiam Woodier was charged with being on the pr mises of the Runcorn Cs-operative Stores. Rowlands-road. on Sunder morning, with intent to commit a felony. —Sergeant Lee said he was on duty in Shaw- st. and heard a noise coming from the Co-operative Stores. Having silent boots on his approach was not heard and he succeeded in ge tin g to the back of the Co-operative Stores. He heard someone moving in the yard, and trying the door. He could not get in the yard. and was walking towards a shed to look over when he kicked something whi h made a noise. Someone began to run, and he heard the yard door rattle and saw Prisoner get on the top of it and jump down. Witness ran to him and asked him what he was doing there and prisoner, who at first could not say anything,said ha was lo king for something to eat Ha searched prisoner and found in his possession a piece of toacon and two onions Walter Mercer, manager of the branch stores in Lowlands- road, stated that pris,ner had no right on the premises. Woodier pleaded guilty to being on the premises. and was I sent to gaol for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>DARES9URY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARES9URY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. —Before Mr. Henry Nield (chaittnan), Captain Rogers. and Mr. John White. DRUNK AND DISORDr;RLY.—JOIIII Rooney Was charged with being drunk and disorderly at Grappenhall.—P.C. Brew proved the case and a fine of ss. was imposed. DOG OWNERS IN TROITBLE.—SaMtIeI Sutton. Of Appleton, was summoned for ha,ing on the 18th inst , allowed his dog to be at large Defendant who was unab:e to appear, was fined ss. including costs.—Rdward Short. of Little Legh, was similarly dealt with for allowing a dog to be at large on the 9th July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>FIR3T COURT.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. T. J, Gillespie (chairman), and Mr. W. P. Gonch. DRUNICEZ,MESQL—David Griffiths for being drunk and dis- orderly on the highway at Newton was fined ss. and costs. Bee'rei. CoNDUOT OF k HAXDOcK MAN.— Thomas Cun- liffe, hairdresser. Church-road, Haydeck, was charged with an aggravated aseault on Agnes Hindley on the 13th July.— Mr. Jenkins, soLcitor. proaeciated, and Mr. Stuart, solicitor. defended. Mr. Jenkins said the prosecution was brought'bv the female prosecutrix for an aggravated assault, and her condition was sueh at the time that it was thought wise to take out a warrant. The facts were that on the 11th July there had been a trumpery dispute between the defendant's child and his ( r. Jenkins') client. The child had thrown, as tke complainant called it, some nasty stuff into her door way, and she chastised him tor it. Cunliffo seemed to have taken the slight incident very much to heart. and must have forgotten himself.On the 13th inst he went to the complainant and used language of a very offensive nature, and then with out any reason struck the woman two violent blows on the side of the head, and afterwards with the gremeet brutality, he kicked her in such a position as mnde it very dangerous, considering the condition the woman was in. The fasts would be borne out by evidence, but they had been obliged to summon witnesses. The de- fendant was wh it he would call an "agitator," and he had many friends who did their best to &amp;Reis, him and back him up. That had beer their difficulty in obtaining sufficient I evidence. Sue was a poor woman, but fortunately for her, frien is had furnished her with sufficient funds that she should not be crushed. - Mr. Jenkins' statement was corro- borated by the proseeutrix, Agaes !Hadley, living at 374, Church-st.. Ilaydock ; Mrs. Daebyhire, 373, Church-road ; Alfred Smith ;' and a boy named Rob rt Hill.—Dr. John Roberts stated that he was called to the house of Mrs' Hindley on the morning of the 13th inst.. and she complained of in- juries. On examination he found a wound on the r.glet thigh and it moat have been a rather severe bl' w to cause it.—ln cross-examination he admitted that the wound might have been inflicted at an earlier date.--P.O. Blackbetrn stited that he was called to the house of the complainant between twelve and one o'clock on the date named, and foend her cryino end upset. She complained that the defendant had assaulted her.—Mr. Stuart said his d-fence was a complete denial of the material fscts. His client went to see the com- plainant about the assault on his boy, and she, commenced caring him names. She afterwards struek him on the side of the head and he then pushed her away. That was all he did to her, and the wound of which she complained was coin- mltted by her husband the week before. The doctor was unable to say whether the wound was inflicted the week previously, and a witness would prove that the complainant told her that she was black and blue " through the treat- ment. of her husband. He (11/Ir. Stuart) was surprised at the etatement of Mr. :Jenkins thatthe defendant was an agitator. It was only a short time ago that Messrs. Richard Evans and Company took up it case for him and they were n3t likely to do that for agitators.—John Boardman and Richard Bat tersby stated that the defendant only pushed the complainant' There was no kicking. Dominic Haley and Mrs. Griffitha also gave evidence in temp irt of Mr. Stuart's statement.— The Chairman said, the magistrates had not the slightest doubt that. an assault was committee, and imposed a fine of 20s. and cots. The advocate's and doctor's fees were allowed. —A charge ageinst Agnes Hindley of committine an assault upon the four-year old son of Themes Cunliffe was dismissed. A COLLIER'S, MEAN AET.—Thomas Mullay. or Haydock was charged with stealing a quantity of coal the oropeity of Thomas eirnms, between the llth and 12th inst.—Mr. Stuart, solicitor, prosecnted, and stated that. both the complainant arsd defendant woiked in the Queen pit of Messrs. R. Evans and Co. The complainant worked along with his son in what was cal ed a shunt, getting coal, and the defendant did pre- cisely the same work in another part of the mine The coal was placed into tuts and the men were paid in proportion to the amount of cell they sent to the surface. In order to as- certain fach man's wor the miners were provided with tallys which they affixed to the tubs of coal they filled. On the 11th inst.. Simms ran short of toilets, and fixed a coloured piece of string on three of the tubs of coal in order that he might be accounted with them. He afterwards took a sam- ple of the string to the weigh-man. and told him tl at he had f stened similar string on three of the tubs. It was after- wards found that the string had teen removed from one of the comolaina.nt's boxes, and a tally numbered 42 had be n affixed instead. No one had any right to meddle with the box, and the only one who would benefit by changing it was the defendent whose numiaer was 42, and who would receive sevenpence for coal gained by the complainant.— 'Themes Si mm said the number on his taliy was 57. He ran short, and fixed a coloured piece of string on three tubs of coal which he had filled. The de- fendant went down the mine after witness had left it. The removal of the stringon one bex and the substitution of the de 'endent's tally me nt a loss of sevenpence to him and a gain of that amount to the defendant. itvidence was also given for the prweention by the compleinant's son. who cor- roborated Mclru.eWenoresliety._, aTnhd, defendant denied that he put his tally on one of the corn- rmo rbo rßa,t,,ecd, tho in- f tatit he e ru'ns plainant's boxes.—The magistrates considered the case fully de'rtmemaneangte. raonfd proved, but as it was the defendant's first appearance before them, he would only be ned 5s and costs, A MUTUAL SETTLEMENT.—Br!dget Rabbit was summoned for having on the 21st inst at Heydock used threetennag language to Ellen Jennings, and there was also a cross-sum- mons against Ellen Jennings chargingher wlth committing a similar offence on Brideet Rabbit. On being questioned by the Clerk, both expressed their willingness for the cases to be settled on their being bound over to keep the peace.</p>
        <p>SECOND COURT. Before Colonel Wood (In the chair), and Mr. C. B. F.</p>
        <p>DISORDERLY INRBRIATE3.—Fines were Borron, C.C. 'Michael Smith, J .hn Fearick, Patrick Melee. of the following for having been drunk and disorderly : imposed upon each Martin Haul- Rao, and John Pimblett, Golborne ; Andrew Kinealey, and John Havey, of Haydock ; Thomas O'Rourke, and :Vartin Cross of Rarlestown.—Blizabeth Travis, of Golborns. made he,. 36th am:ea.-once, charged with being drunk and disorderly on the 21st inst. She was committed to prison for a month. PUGILISM.—Jamts • dmond and Joseph Wood were proved guilty of a breach of peace by fighting at Colborne on the ni ht of the 9th July, and were both bound over in sureties of £5 each to keep the peace for three months. COWARDLY ASSAULT AT HAYDOCK.—MIcIueI Donnell, col- lier, was summoned by Mien Ashton, both of Haydock, for assault.—Complainant alleged that as she was passing along Kenyon's-lane, Ilaydock, on the night of the 14th July defnirlant ortme out of a neighbouring fim!d and shook TRIED AND PROVED!</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Gr.ivel, Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back Dropsy. Wind and Water Complaint, Diseasss of Kidneys Bladder Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a positive Cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices is. Of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps. from HoLnnovn's Medical Hall Cbeckheaten, Yorks. Sold at 35, Bridge-st., Warringtau. Don't be put off, If you cannot get them write to the kropristomnd a box will be sent next post— Axlvt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>her violently. He then cbarzed her with speaking against him to other people, anti ultimately he struck her in the mouth with his tit. knocking her to the ground, anti dislolging two or three of her teeth. Ghe got up and ran away. He followed her, end on reaching her abods he challenged her husband out to fi ComPlainant's st dement was corrobortt.d by two witnesses. —Defendant admitted slightly shaking the woman. an push- ing her, but denied that he struck her, or knocked he- to the ground.—A witness supported him, but the ma.Tistr,tt-s wee satisfied that the defendant was gui'ty, and fined him ICls. and costs, the Chairman characterizing the assault its dis- graceful and brutal.</p>
        <p>THE GREENGROCER AND THE 13NR AGF.T. MATRON —Joseph Holden. greengrocer. of Go!horns. summoned Mary Ann and Joseph Pigott (mother and son) for assaulting him on the 22nd July.—He complained that while driving his cart down Mill-street on the night of the date named the female defend- ant came behind him, and knocked him town, the son then beginning to kick hirn.—These elle rations were partially sup- ported by two witnesses. but the mother and son both empatieally denied the truth of toem. The female complained that the man Holden had hoesewhinperl one of her little boys and that when she went to Ho'den for an explanation he, being drunk at the time, ran at her to treat her the same. Her son, coming up at the time intereosed. and having received the! b'nve maant for her pestle(' Holden back. The man was too intoxicated to stand, and fell to the ground. cutting the hack of his head.—The son, and three witnesses corroborated, all the witnesses deny- ing that Joseph" ' ibbert had kicked complainent.—The Bench finally bound both defendants over in £5 each for three months to keep the peace. THEFT OF WATCHFS Bekersley WAS charged with having at Golborne on the 14th June stolen two slyer lever watches and a silver alhert value £3 64., the property of William Dick nson.—The complainant, who is a collier. re- siding at Bros ;-st., Gelhorne, stated that on Sunday. the 14th of June, he was in comnany with the prisoner, and they went to a reservoir near Golborne Collieries for a bathe. Re gave the twa watches and guard prodnoed to the prisoner to hold. When he cams out of the water he found that the prisoner had decamped. He gave information to the police. George Godfrey. labourer, Wargrave, New'on, stated that at 5 p.m. on Sunday. the 14th of June, the prisoner came to his house and stated that he was out of work and was hard up. He asked witness to buy a watch for 12s. Witness gave him 11 .—Robert Kirkpatrick. pawnbroker. Skelmersdale. stated that the prisoner came to his shop and offered the watch and guard produced in pledge. He asked £2 for the articles. Witness gave him 355. He knew the prisoner, who had re- sided near his shoo.—P.S. Bagel spoke to apprehending the prisoner at Billinge, on the 10th inst. He admitted the I theft. —fhe magistrates committed him to prison for one I month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
