      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BRITISH WORKMAN'S AND GENERAL ASSURANCE COMPANY, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>BRITISH WORKMAN'S AND GENERAL ASSURANCE COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>An advertisement on our front page contains extracts frem the directors' report and balance sheet of the British Workman's and General Assurance Company, Limited for the year ending April 30,1896,and a marked increase If business is recorded. The Company, which has its chief offices in Birmingham was established in 1866, and since that time tbe business has steadily de- veloped, The !success whic'n has attended the company in recent years may be judged by the fact that in spite of all opposition, in addition to having Increased the in- come by £143,000 in four years, the company has paid- £2o,ooo in bonuses and added £lll.OOO to the funds. The total income last year was £438,250 15s. 11d., and the total expenditure £395,249 I.lB.ld.,leaving a balance on the year's acceunte of £430,001 4s. 10s. The increase in the receipts during the oast year amounted to about £50,000, and Mr. Henry Port, the managing director states that the business is at present increasing at the rate of over £l,OOO a week beyond last year. There is not only an increase of £lO,OOO in the premium income in the nine weeks this year since the completion of the balance sheet, but the com- pany is already £6,188 ahead of the previous year in the surplus. The claims paid last year amounted to the sum of £175,823 Us. 11d., making the entire sum paid during the thirty years' existence of the company £1,635,683 13s. ld. The funds of the society are £249,581 4s. 91, and notwithstanding the cheap- ness of rnoney,the company is making a good rate of in- terest on its investments. £81,684 Os. 101. are invested in mortgages on property within tbe United Kingdom ; £19,253 84. in house property,and large sums are also in- vested in railway and other shares, debentures, ground rents. and Leicester, Perth, and Rochdale Corporation stocks. The increased business has rendered it neces- sary for the company to largely extend their head offices in Birmingham, and branches are being established in various parts of the kingdom, there being at present over 150 in existence. During the past year a further effort was made to meet the requirements of the Old Age Pension scheme,under the company's endowment assur- ance policies, and special tables of benefits have been issued, together with special industrial tables. The ;su- perintendent for Warrington district is Mr. E. B. John- son, under whoee energetic management the company's lendne sis largely increasing in this neighbourhood. His office is at the Union Bank Chambers, Market Gate, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>01): AN D</p>
        <p>THE VALUE O? AN ALIAS. —Jimmy : "Say, I got out of&amp; bad serape to-day." Tommy:" Did yer? What was it ? " Jimmy;. "Somebody told me mother a feller called Mugsey ' broke a man's winder down de street. if sho only knowed dat wuz me !"</p>
        <p>HIS LIFEWORK." Oh, papa, what makes old Mr. Grabenheimer walk so stoop-shouldered? He looks like a horseshoe." "I have heard, my son, that many years ago, when he was a very little boy, no bigger than you are now, he found a cent,"</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE.—Bingo: "Bobby, did you eat that little pie your mother made for you yesterday?" Bobby: "No, sir. I gave it to my teacher." Bingo: "Did she eat ?" Bo!Jby guess so. There wasn't any school to-day." PROVED.—Wife: "Don't you think I am more</p>
        <p>economical than I was a year v..-- o?" Husband: " I haven't noticed it." Wife(teuroacthfully) "Why, dear, I get twice as many things with the</p>
        <p>same money." Still she hesitated.</p>
        <p>" You__ are. poor," she</p>
        <p>argued, "while I have a million." Springing forward impetuously, he gathered her to his bosom. I will marry you," he eried " fGr nil that 1"</p>
        <p>SHAMEPUL OVERSIGHT. Uptown; "Burglars got into my house last night and 'lifted' everything, of any 'importance—with one exception." Down- town: " What was that ? " Uptown: "The</p>
        <p>mortgage." WORLDLY WlSE.—First Mite: "I'll tell on you !" Second Mite: "If you do, I'll tell on you !" First Mite : "Nobody 'll believe you after yer reputashun's destroyed." SOME people spend enough time in grieving over spilt milk to earn another cow. THE RULE OF THREE.—Mrs. Fussy: "John, how did you happen to buy a new umbrella to-day?" Fussy: "You told me always to leave one at home and one in the office; so, in order to keep off the rain, I had to buy a third." NOT EXACTING THERE.—Biggun (pompously,. at Western hotel) : "I am Senator Biggun "---- Clerk: "That's all right. We care nothing about a man's bad character at this house, as long as he behaves himself here, and pays in advance. THE price of beef is rising so fast that after awhile the rich man will have cows on his lawn, instead of fawns.</p>
        <p>Pallet: "Oh, I belong to the realistic school! Paint things just as I find 'em, you see." Farmer Barnes: "Don't believe in that, young man; don't believe in it. Neighbour Brooks was jest that kind of a fellow—painted his house without puttyin' up a crack. An' now look at it !" A WARNING.—RuraI Ragges : "No, mum, I haven't come to beg. I've come ter warn yer agin men travellin' round callin' theirselves tramps.</p>
        <p>They ain't tramps at all, mum." Mrs. Hussaf : "How am I going to know them?" Rural Ragges : "Easily enough, mum. They look as if they was really hungry. They won't ask yer fer money. They'll want to do a whole lot of work fer a bite ter eat. They'll do the work willingly. An' after they've eaten the scraps you give 'ern they'll go away lookin' grateful. Yer can't mis- take 'em fer tramps, mum." ENDING A DISCUSSION.—Mr. Grumpps (hotly) : "You must be crazy." Mrs. Grumpps (icily): "Just what everybody said when I married you."</p>
        <p>- SMYTHE : "She wasn't sure which she liked better, Jones or me, but she ',rave me the benefit of the doubt." Bluffs: "And made you happy?" Smythe: " Yes ; they have been married more than a year now." Mrs. Casey: "Let's immigrate to Americay, Moike. Do yer moind ph what luck Pat Shanahan had? He was only there two year, whin he doied worth t'ree hundred pounds." MR. FINEMIND : "My dear, my scientific works are bringing me in a fortune, and we will soon be rich." Mrs. Finemind : "Too bad. Now we will have to associate with a lot of nobodies who inherited their wealth." Miss KENNETH: "Rosy is it they do not use the telephone in Russia?" Mr. Potter: "Well, you see, ' Hello ' in Russia is Tzizakenfitkrajanjanski— hence the telephone has not been introduced into that country.'</p>
        <p>ONE GOOD POINT. - A custom prevails at funerals among the Dutch settlers in Natal for some one of the deceased's friends to make an oration at his grave, recording his virtues and the good deeds performed by him. It happened once, however, that an individual died who was not considered to have been possessed of any virtues whatever. Consequently the obsequies were nearly being completed in silence, when one of his country said, in solemn tones—" Him was a berry good smoker." " \VII° is going to be the best man at your wedding ? " asked Hicks of Garraway, who is shortly to marry a "new woman." " I don't know," said Garraway, sadly. "Miss Strongmind and I have not settled yet which of us is to have that honour." REUBEN (telling of his visit to London) : " Wun day I sat right 'longside two burglars in a street kyar." Cynthia: "How did ye know they wuz burglars, Reub ? " Reuben: " Waal, wun sez t' the other 'l'm goin' to Rob Roy t' night; will ye cum?' and the other sez, Yeou 'bat I will. I</p>
        <p>hope it'll be as good a Robin Hood.'" A POSSIBILITY.—How true it is that a guilty ' conscience needs no accuser was well shown by the ' following little occurence. A gentleman went out of town for a day's fishing, taking a luncheon with him. When he reached the creek he discovered that he had dropped the luncheon somewhere on the way, and hastened back to look for it. By-and-by he met a burly nego, who looked very well pleased, and was brushing some crumbs from the breast of his coat. "Did you pick up anything in the road as you come along ?" asked the gentleman. "No, sah," answered the coloured man. "I didn't pick up anything. Couldn't a dog have found it and eat it up ?" A BRILLIANT IDEA.—An interesting incident occurred while a company of a certain battalion stationed in one of our garrison towns were going through their musketry training. Owing to a strong wind blowing from the right, the bullets kept falling to the left of the target. An old major, who was in charge at the ranges, came over to the colour-sergeant and inquired the cause of the bad shooting. On being told that there was too much wind blowinc, from the right, causing the shots to fall wide, he astonished the sergeant by asking—" Wouldn't it be a good idea if the targets were moved more to the left?" The colour- sergeant barely restrained a smile. QUITE OI7T OF THAT KIND. Lady (to dog dealer) : "I live in a lonely house in the country, and I want a good house-dog." "Yes, mum." "But I don't want one that will keep me awake all night barking at nothing." "No, mum." "He must be very strong and fierce, yet as gentle as a lamb with us, you know." " Yes, mum." "And he must drive away every tramp who may come near the house." " Yes, mum. "But he must not interfere with any honest person comirer. along." "No, mum." "if a burglar comes, the m dog must attack him imediately.' " Yes, mum." "But he must not molest anyone who makes a friendly call at any time.' " 0, mom. Is there anything else? "Oh, yes; he must not, of course, interfere with any persons who may have to call on my husband. He is a doctor, and a great many people call." "Yes, mum; just so. What you want is a thought-reading dog. Very sorry, but I'm quite out of that kihd," TAKEN.—" Don't want any soap nor pictures," said the woman to the strange young man at the door. "I'm not a pedlar, madam," said the stranger. "My business is of an entirely different nature. Do you rememher a weary, ragged, and hungry tramp calling at your kitchen door about six weeks ago?" "No, I do not," " Let me refresh your memory. You were dressed in an old morn- ing wrap, tied at the waist with a string; your front hair was in pins, and there was a dark smudge across your nose " She turned to slam the door, hut his foot happened to be in the way. "You had a rolling-pin in one hand and a kettle of hot water in the other, with which you threatened to scald the poor wayfarer." "Well, I didn't scald him, anyhow. You can't get any assault and battery against me." "Nothing of the sort was intended, ma'am. I merely wish to let you know that I was that supposed tramp. The bundle that I carried on that occasion was a lightning camera, and I have already been offered ten shillings by a neighbour for the picture of you I obtained, but if you want it for seven-and-six- pence, it is yours." She had it. A tragic accichlit, is reported from S-,mmaut' a rural commune in the district of Antun, Fiance. While a peasant was mowing in the fields his little son, three years of age, approached too close to the scythe., his head being severed trorn his body. The horrified father, beside himself with despair, left the field, awl went and hane-,d himself.</p>
      </div>
