      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PRODUCTION.</head>
        <p>PRODUCTION.</p>
        <p>AN EXPERIMENT IN CO-OPERATIVE</p>
        <p>A correspondent contributes the following interesting article to the "Manchester Guardian" :—Together with a great revival of interest in the theory of Social- ism, we have of late seen a revival of those practical experiments in Socialism which, with a vividness that mere theory cannot attain, embody in definite shape not only all the arguments that the Socialists urge in favour of their views, but also all the objections with which those views are confronted. The last decade has already witnessed three movements on an almost national scale —the American co-operators at Topolobampo, the Aus- tralian Communif ts in Paraguay, the European Free- lenders in East Africa. There are also numerous smaller experiments at work in all directions around us. If one classes Starnthwaite with these more ambitious schemes, it is not so much because the pro- moters of that experiment have definitely at- tempted to realise a socialist programme as be- cause they have, in spite of themselves, illus- trated all the main benefits and difficulties of practical Socialism. The ideal of Starnthwaite has been a co-operative village that shall employ all its own peo- ple in supplying each other's needs ; that shall, in fact, take these people out of the competitive system, which can give them no employment, and shall set them to work at producing for each other instead of the over- rocked world market outside. The man of the move- ment has been Mr. H. V. Mills, who, after setting forth this ideal in "Poverty and the State," slowly got toge- ther promises ammunting to £4,000 with a view to prac- tical work ; but then came General Booth's scheme for a nclony in Essex, and a circular was issued to subscri- bers recommending them to support that effort instead. When, however, it became clear that the Essex farm colony was to be only a feeder for a large colony abroad and not necessarily a self-sustaining group, a new ap- peal was made, and some £6,000 in all has now been spent on the Starnthwaite experiment. Land has cost £2,200 ; old buildings, stock, crops, and implements have absorbed over £1.500 ; and the rest of the money has been spent in maintenance, materials, new stock, and general improvements. Mr. Mills, has, of course, never received a penny ; in fact, he has freely devoted the greater part of his own means and time to the work. The est ,te consists of four acres at Starnthwaite and 126 at Browhead. The former property includes a timber mill and corn mill and valuable water power,and most of the buildings are concentrated there. The two places nearly adjoin each other, and are within seven miles of an excellent market at Kendal. The site is exceedingly picturesque, and only an hour's walk from Bowness-on-Windermere, and the beauty of the scenery is not without its effect on the lives of the residents. An old lady who came from a fried-fish shop in Salford once told me with tears in her eyes that when she got up on a summer morning she now at last felt it was good to have been horn. The main industries are market-gardening, pig-breeding, and corn-milling, but the colony aims also to supply all its own needs. Thus till quite recently they spun into cloth the wool from their own sheep, and themselves made the cloth into clothes, so that they knew that all they wore was of honest manufacture; but the shifting character of the population made this a very expensive plan, and the cloth-making has now reluc- ta.itly been abandoned. They have valuable peat beds on their estate, and use the peat not only for fuel but as litter for cattle; they have the right of taking a neigh- bouring landlord's fallen tim'eer hi return for keeping his fences in order; they have bred horses with a considerable amount of seeeess, but ficd that the Present enthusiasm for cycling is telling against them in this respect. The residents in return far their work receive beard and lodging, clothes and boots, and all the necessaries o • life, and half-a- crown a week besides; their children are kept and educ- ated free of cost. This does not seem much, but in the case of a man with a wife and three grown-up children it means 12s. 6d. a week for pocket-money, which is a good deal more than such a family would probably get outside. In fact there is little to be done with the extra money except to spend it on finery and tobacco, and re- cently, in spite of the walk entailed, the theatrical com- panies that visit Kendal have been extensively patronised by the more adventurous colonists. Starnthwaite has always been a compromise. The majority of the sub- scribers have aimed at giving a lesson to poor-law guar- dians rather than at initiating a co-operative community but more might have been done in the direction of prac- tical Socialism if the first settlers, who included some well-known Socialists, had not made a premature attempt to overrule the subscribers' committee and to take the management into their own hands. So the pen- dia= swung to the other side, and recently the Mile End Guardians offered to send down paupers and pay five shillings a week per head, but as they could not promise to send strong workers the plan fell through. The place is now practically self-supporting. Last year it showed a deficit of £2B, but against this must be set a large expenditure in manures and permanent im- provement of the land. The reason why progress has been so slow seems to be the disproportionate outlay of labour and capital on permanent work, such as new buildinv.s, fencing, and so forth, and the poor quality of the workers. Ninety-nine have now passed through the place in four years, the present number being 34, and it is impossible to select more than 1 per cent, of the applicants ; yet out of three selected men very few are able at once to do work that is worth their keep. Over and over again efforts have been made to conduct some part of the work without outside supervision,but hither- to all have failed. The relaxation of control has not re- sulted in voluntary co-operation of the workers. Yet in many ways the place has illustrated the advantazes of co-operatiee industry and the economies of the corn' tnunal life. The waste of Individualism has been stopped ; it has been found possible, for instance, to get fish wholesale from Grimsby and to sell so much at retail to the neighbouring farmers that the coin- munitv's supply costs nothing at all. The selfishness of Individualism has been counteracted; many a time some of the men and wemen have worked far harder and longer than they would for an employer, because they were producing not for the profit of others but for their own use. [he morose seclusion of Individualism has been undermined ; the people have been far more corn- foitable and happy in their communal life than they ever were under the old regime of isolated dwellings and</p>
        <p>separate interests,. The subseribers' committee has now dissolved, and the sole management is in Mr. Mill's hands, suti.ct to the trust-deed and the trustees. In little more than four years all the initial hardships have been passed and the initial expenses met, and a very small amount of capital per head will new enable a much larger number of people to be employed. If the faculty of self-government could only be developed among the members, Starnthwaite may et grow into a tor more perfect experiment in brotherhood that it has been in the past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ANOTHER NEW LOCAL COMPANY.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER NEW LOCAL COMPANY.</p>
        <p>The prospectus of the Mersey Bedstead Company Limited will be foun I in another column. The share capital is £25,000 divided into 500 5i per cent preference shares of £lO each (cumulative and with priority in respect of capital as well as divi- dend), and 2,000 ordinary shares of £lO each. The company is formed for the purpose of aciuiring and working as a going concern, and to extend and further develop the business, now carried on at the Fladfield Works, St. James'-street, Latchford. War- rington, by the Mersey Bedstead Company as metal- lic bedstead manufacturers. The manufacture of bedsteads at these works was commenced about two years ago with a plant capable of making 16 differ- ent patterns, and the plant now possessed by the company has been so enlarged and extended that they are able to make more than 200 different pat- terns of the newest and best design, of perfect fit and finish, including French bedsteads of every des- cription, Parisian, Italian, half tester, and fourpost bedsteads suitable for all markets,cots,folding chair I beds, institution beds, &amp;c., &amp;c. The demand and output have increased so largely that the present. foundry accommodation is very inadequate to the call upon it, and one of the first opera- tions of the new company will be to erect a new foundry of the dimensions required to meet an output of 1,000 bedsteads per week. , The busi- ness is a sound, flourishing, and increasing one. The chief reason for the conversion of the business into a limited liability company is to provide fur- ther capital in order to cope with the increased demands for their goods. The stoves erected by the company are constructed on an improved principle, and are probably the best in the trade, the cost of japanning being reduced to a minimum. When in possession of a wharf on the Mersey at the back of the works, the Company will have special facilities for water carriage, and an important factor in the increase of the Company'e trade has been the ad- vantageous position of the woiks which has enabled them to send bedsteads to Lancashire and York- shire at a much less rate than was possible from the chief centre of this industry. The Directore of the Company are :—Mr. Joseph Titchett, Mr. David Fanahaw, Mr. George Clay, and Mr. James Proctor Jones, all of Warrington.</p>
        <p>WESLEYANS AND 'DENOMINATIONAL</p>
        <p>EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Conference at Liverpool was occupied for some time on Wednesday in considering the educa- tion question. Mr. R. W. Perks, M.P., moved a resolu- tion to the effect that information be collected as to the number of Wesleyan children attending denominational schools, the nature of the religious instruction given in such salmis the character of the catechism or books of devotion used,the operation of the conscience clanse,and any religious tests upon Methodist scholars as a condi- tion of becoming pupil or assistant teachers. He urged the necessity of their arming themselves with exact in- formation, so that they would be ready to meet their op- ponents in this matter in the future. He further men- tioned some of the results of his own inquiries with re- ference to the disabilities under which Nonconformist children suffered ir. th? country districts. The resolu- tion was adopted.</p>
      </div>
