      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above chamber was held at Market Gate Chambers on Monday. Mr. John Fair- Clough J.P, „presided. Councillor ARTHUR BENNETT (the hon. secretary) reported that,with Mr.J.H.Fleming J.P.,and Mr.Williarn Long,J.P.he attended the recent third congress in London of the Chambers oi Commerce of the Empire on behalf of the Warrington Chamber. He said that the various resolutions and amendments which had been brought forward on the subj,ct of preferential trade between the mother country and the colonies, were withdrawn, and the following resolution was unanimously carried "That this Congress of Chambers of Commerce of the Empire is of opinion that the eats blishm•nt of closer commercial relations between the United Kingdom and the Colonies and D,pendencies is an object which deserves and demands prompt and careful consideration. The Congress, therefore, respectfully represents to Her Majesty's Government that if the suggestions should be made on behalf of the Colonies, or s•sme of them, it Would be right and expedient to promote such considera- tion, and the formulation of some practicable plan by summoning an imperial conference thoroughly repre- sentative of the interests involved, or by such other means as Her Majesty may be advised to adopt. That copies of this resolution be forwarded to the Presi- dent, the Prime Minister,the First Lord of the Treasury, the Secretary of Stare for the Colonies, the leaders of the ' Opposition in both Houses, the High Commissioner for Canada, and the Agents-General of the other Colonies." In addition to that, resolutions were also adopted in favourof the creation of an Imperial Consultative Coun- cil ; of the codification f the commercial law of the Empire ; of a uniform system of bills of exchange ;uni- form cspyright law ; the establishment of inter-imperial postaland telegraphic rates ; improved postal facilities ; arbitration in international disputes; establishment of boards of conciliation and arbitration in trade disputes; blll4 of ladiag reform; n' the reduction of rates of freight to South Africa, which seriously handicap British com- merce ;of the encouragement of emigration to the Colonies instead of to foreign countries ; of the adoption of the metric system, throughout the Empire, and of the various other resolutions of great importance to the trade of the Empire. A leler was read from the Mansion House Associa- tion on Railway and Canal fraffic in reference to special centracts made by railway companies, by which in cosi- sideration of accepting reduced rates they relieved them- selves from a portion of their liabilities. It was unanimously resolved :—" That this meeting, having read Lord Ci3arles 13..resford'is serious statement with regard to the manning ot the navy and the condition of the naval reserves, hereby desires to express its sense of the grave danger of our present position, and urges her Majesty's Government to at once institute a foil in- quiry into the whole question of the manning of the fleet,the royal navy res rve.and the mercantile marine, with a view, if necessary,to definite legislative proposals next year, including a suitable prevision in the estimates"</p>
        <p>It was resolved that the Chamber should be ire Pr- esented at the forthcoming autumnal meeting of the As- sociated Chambers at Southampton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LIBERAL PIC \ IC TO BELLE VIIE.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL PIC \ IC TO BELLE VIIE.</p>
        <p>A. picnic pro:noted by the Warrington Liberal Asso- ciation was held on Saturday. The committee, with some forethought, had fixed upon Belle Vue, as the destination where erijo merits of such a varied nature can be obtained even it the weather be unfavourable. Saturday opened exceedingly wet and it was feared that the trip would he of very minor proportions in conse- quence, but at 2 o'clock, which was the time for the special train to leave the Central Station, there were sings of a cessation of the downpour and about 150 people, who feared not the weather, assembled on the platform. Starting punctulaly,the party was conveyed to BAle Vue Station via, Cheadle II ulme, and Stockn3rt,the gardens being reached in the course of an hour from the start. Here, of course, the party ceased to be a party, for everybody " ganged their am n gait" and proceeded to enjoy the innumerable attractions which the Zoo- logical gardens incompasses. To particularize is need- less, and it suffices to ray that for seven hours gaiety was in full swing, unmarred too by a single drop of rain. The grand spectacular firework display, for which Bene Vue is famous, concluded the programme and at 10.30 the pic-nicers were cenveyed home, reaching Warring- ton in about threequarters of an hour. Allowing for the weather in the early part of the day, the trip was a com- plete success, and augured well for future occasiens when it is to be hoped climatic conditions will be more favourable.</p>
      </div>
