        <p>EA.RLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Earlestow-n-E-b-en-e-z,r Lodge held its weekly session on Tuesday evening in the Co-operative Hall, Earle-street, Bro. T. Dean, C.T. presided. There was only a moderate attendance, several members being away on their holidays. Bro. Woods gave an address, and solos and readings by the members being given, the meeting then closed. INEBRIETY.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown-NIIC-(;—S-tation before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, on Monday morning, Joseph Bristow for being drunk was fined 24. 61. and costs.---George Holden, of Derby-st., and Thomas McLoughlin, of St. Helens, were each lined 2. 61. and costs for be- ing drunk.-Thomss Holden for committing a breach of the peace was bound over for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>POLICE CHANGES.</head>
        <p>POLICE CHANGES.</p>
        <p>Police Constable Johnson, late of Bury division, has been promoted to the position of Sergeant, and appointed to the Newton section of the Leigh Division in the place of Sergeant Clarke, who has been appointed Inspector of Weights and Measures. CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, NBWTON. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>In aid of the above church choir funds,a musical service was given on Sunday afternoon by the choir under the leadership of Mr. W. H. Thompson, organist and choirmaster. Mr. Thompson submitted a couple of well executed organ solos, and vocal solos were well rendered by Miss F. Hopwood (late of Earleetown), and Mr. D. E. Williams of Manches- ter. The choir efficiently contribut6d several choruses. The collections amounted to a little over £4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLAR PICNIC FROM EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR PICNIC FROM EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual picnic in connection with the 'Hope of Barlestown Lodge of Good Templars took place on Saturday. The party numbering about 30 left Earlestown at 8.45 for Frodsham, which place they reached in due course. The weather was most in- clement, rain falling heavily the greater portion of the day,but as the party remained under cover they suffered very little inconvenience. Dinner and tea was partaken of at Mersey View, and in the after- noon the party gave an entertainment in the cave. Under the circumqtances they passed a very enjoy- able day, and returned home about 9 30. ACCIDENT ON THE RACE COURSE.</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoGn, a boot and shoe manufac- turer of 17 Trafford Road, Salford, met with an accident which will incapacitate him for several weeks. He went into a booth opposite the grand stand for a glass of beer, and as he was leaving he put his foot into a hole and overbalanced himself and fell, dislocating and fracturing the ankle joint of his right leg and also breaking the leg about four inches higher up. After lying on the ground some time, Police-Sergeant Barnes, a member of the St. John Ambulance, was called to him, and seeing the nature of his injuries, made splints of newspapers and bound up the injured limb, and after putting him into a conveyance drove him to Dr. Patterson. After the doctor had attended to the injured limb the man was sent to Manchester Infirmary where he was detained as an inpatient. Sergeant Barnes was highly commended by Dr. Patterson for the excellent manner in which he had attended to the</p>
        <p>ir-I;Ct-i-i-r-e-i, considering the limited means at his disposal. SHOCKING SWING BOAT FATALITY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening about half-past six, whilst the Earlestown,,Market ground was crowded with people, and the hobby horses, swings, &amp;c., were in full operation, a collier named George EF.l.gelton, of 14,Cross-4treet, St. Helens, 24 years of age, was killed by falling out of a swing boat. The deceased man left home about 12 noon on Saturday in com • pany with his wife, for the purpose of visiting the Newton races. After the races he, his wife, and brother visited the market ground at Barlestown, and deceased and his brother Thomas Eggelton got into a swing boat. They were both standing up swinging,and the man in charge of the boats called to them to sit down. tie turned away to attend to another boat,and looking round twain, saw that the deceased had fallen out of the boat backwards, alighting on the side of his head. lie was picked up in an unconscious condition and taken to Dr. Valentine's surgery where he died a few minutes after admission without recovering consciousness. His injuries were of a shocking description.—An inquest on the body was held at the Oddfellows Arms,Legh-street, on Monday morning by Mr. S. Brighouse, when evidence bearing out the above facts was given, and a verdict of "Accidental death " was returned, no blame being attached to anyone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly se—,:sroi of the Elope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. E. Newman, S.J.T., presided, and there was a large attendancce of members. Bro Newman conveyed fraternal greetings from the Good Templar Mission at War- wick. One candidate was initiated to membership. The digest of business for No. 1 Convention, to be held at Widnes, was read as also was a corntr.unics- don with respect to a 29 days' mission to be held in Liverpool to finish up with a large meeting in St. Georgs Hall. Bro. John Warburton gave a re- port of the picnic to Frodsham on Saturday,which was successful. The evening's programme was "Sing, recite, or pay a penny." Several songs and recitations were given, but a good number of members paid the forfeit. An enjoyable evening was spent._ _</p>
        <p>NEWTON FREEMASON'S PICNIC. Makerfield lodge 2,155 had their annual pic- nic on Wednesday. The party numbering 40, left Newton-le-Willows at 7.30 in a special carriage for Llangollen, which they reached about 10.15. Light refreshments were served on arrival, and the party afterwards drove to Beryn where an excellent luncheon was partaken of about 12.30. They re- turned to Pontyscillio, and after viewing the church and horse shoe fall they made the journey back to Llangollen by boat on the Shropshire Union Canal. This pert of the journey was thoroughly enjoyed. Llangollen being reached about five o'clock. A capital dinner was served at the Royal Hotel, and ample justice was done to the good things. Llan- gollen was left shortly after seven, and Earlestown was reached about 945. A very pleasant outing was spent, and the arrangements were excellently carried out by Mr. Singleton. VIADUCT I NSTITUTE TEC II NIC AL INSTRUCTION</p>
        <p>CLASSES. The following results of Bcience and Art exami- nations held in the subjects named in connection with the above classes have been received :—Build- ing : John Evans, H. Hopwood. ff. Starkie, W. Stephens, 2nd A., T. Dring, T. Morris, W. M. Stephens, J. Wilson; E.pass. J.S. Howarl, A. Hunt, T. Monks, E.fair. Magnetism and electricity : E.G. Hulme, Ist A.; J. E. Benison, 2nd A. Geometrical drawing: J A. Bullock, J. A. Chambers, T. Dring, A. R. Hill, W. Marsh, T. Morris. C. T. Partington, pass. Applied mechanics, J E. Price, E. pass. CONGREGATIONAL SUNDAY SCHOOL, NEWTON. The annual treat to the B,:holars attending the above school took place on Friday. Owing to the very wet day, they were unable to enjoy a romp in the field. Consequently they mustered at the schoolroom at 2 o'clock, and after amusing them- selves in various ways, tea was served. After tea it cleared up somewhat and the children were allowed to play:in the school grounds.Nuts,&amp;c.,were I distributed, and the scholars dispersed at dusk. Oa Saturday,the elder scholars had their annual picnic, the destination this year being Chester. They left Earlestown at 8.45; and reached Chester shortly be- fore 10 o'clock. The rain greatly interfered with the arrangements and after wandering about the city for some time,they adjourned for dinner. The 'afternoon was anent in a visit to Eaton Hall, after I which the party returned home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>NEWTON IttiCE-COURSE.</head>
        <p>THE NEWTON IttiCE-COURSE.</p>
        <p>With reference to the rumour that a syndicate had been formed to purchase Haydock Park and lay it out for toe purposes of racing, we learn (says the "Sporting Chronicle,")that there is some foundation for the report. It seems that the racecourse at Newton is the sole pro- perty ef Lord Newton,and for some years past buildings have been encroaching on this estate. His lordship re- ceived an offer from gentlemen who desired to purchase the site for the election of large works,and to build cot- tages on the land remaining for the accommodation of the emplos Is. Lord Newton gave the proposal his ear- , nest considerations and eventually decided that no more race meetings should be held on the old course, so that whatever happens we have seen the last of Newton races on the ground so well-known to racegoers. The Messrs. Frail, of London, were then approached by the agent of Lord Newton, and they were asked if they were dis- posed to form a syndicate. and endeavour to carry on Newton Races at Haydock Park. Messrs. Frail went oNer this ground on Friday, and were perfectly satisfied ' with the suggested course, and all its surroundings as a suitable place as a successful meeting. The only impedi- i ment is that the old Newton course has only been allowed two days' racing in the year by the Jockey nub. Ot course, with such limited scope it would be impos- sible to bring the project to a happy issue. If the pro- moters can induce the stewards' of the Jockey Club to give two additienal days for racing, a syndicate will be formed, and Newton races will be conducted in Haydock Park.</p>
        <p>VIA DUCT INSTITUTE, EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the members of this Institute was held in the large reading-room on Thurs- day evening. Mr. J. W. Emmett presided, and there was a fair attendance of members.</p>
        <p>The Secretary (Mr. T. Ormand) read the report for the year ended the 30a1 June last in the course of which he stated that in reply to a question put by Mr. Garnett at the last annual meeting,the President stated that they were doing all they could to secure a series of Gilchrist lectures. It was now a matter of common knowledge that such a course had been granted to Earlestown. It was a compliment to Earlestown to be selected by the Gilchrist trustees as one of the 15 towns, out of 67 applicitions, for a course of lectures ; and they felt sure the members of the Institute would by their attendance and unflagging interest make the lectures a magnificent success. (Hear. hear.) They ventured to hope, or rather predict, that such a coutse of science lectures would be the means of estab-</p>
        <p>fishing a seri4s of University Extension Lectures which would make secondary education a very powerful factor for g3od in that neighbourhood. There were very few items in the balance sheet which demanded special com- ment or explanation. It was gratifying to know that although they commenced the year with an adverse oalance of £ll 9s. sp. they could present to them a statement of accounts with a balance in hand of three shillings after having paid every bill. (Applause.) The total income for the year was £36 13s. 10J., more than that of the corresponding period ending June,lB9s,which was chiefly owing to augmented subscriptions, both ;.e.neral and public, apprentices' premiums, and a profit of £lO ha 71. ,on the soiree. The aggregate exrenditure was £l2 54. 60. in advance of 1895, but this was more than accounted for by the cost of the pavilion sun blind, tennis nets and racquets and a special grant to the band. The committee had again voted £2 to the Cricket Club for prizes to be awarded as the committee of the club deemed best ; and also £2 for priz.s to be bowled for by the members of the Institute. In addition to these annual prizes they had purchased four bats (value £3 153. 6.1.) which are.to be won by those amateur cricketers having the best batting and bowling averages at the close of the current season. To encourage the members of the Bowling Club who are taking part in the compe- tition for the Legh Challenge Cup it has been unani- mously agreed to place two medals at the disposal of the Club's Committc.e, to be awarded as follows :—One for the best average obtained in the home team ; and one for the best average secured in the team competing away. During the year just ended, 8,688 books were issued from the library,the largest number yetcirculated in twelve months. It was with considerable reluctance they reported that there was no material increase in the number of readers this year compared with the previous one. Out of a membership of over 1,300 they could only record 355 readers as against 333 in 1895. This was not a fair percentage and they sincerely hoped the members would be more alive to their opportunities and make a better use of the library than they had done hitherto. Since the catalogue was published in 1894, no less than 268 books had been added to the library; and any of them could be obtained on application to the librarian after the Ist prox. The readers were cordially invited to communicate with either the librarian or secretary when they desired any particular book which was not in the library, and if it was Rot on order and was suitable for general circulation, it should be ob- tained as speedily as possible. They should, in fact, be pleased to receive any suggestion which would tend to enlarge their sphere of usefulness or bring happiness and contentment to the homes of the people by the dissemi- nation cf pure and healthy literature. Wordsworth,had very beautifully said in one of his finest sonnets,— . .</p>
        <p>"Books we know. Are a substantial world, both pure and good, Round which. with tendrils strong as flesh and blood, Our pastime and our happiness will grow." (Applause.) It was not their intention to criticise any the examination results or make a comparison between this Year's entries and those of previous sessions, as they would be dealt with in their proper place. But as it was not politic at a prize distribution to refer specially to the number of young men attending the classes from the Company's Works there, they took that opportunity of expressing their surprise and regret that the advantages which must of necessity accrue to those wha were in regular attendance at any of the educational classes which had been established for their benefit, were not laid hold of and made to serve as stepping stones to better things. They thought, that at least 75 per cent, of those und,r 21 years of age should be receiving instruction during the winter months in some branch of knowledge applicable to their own trade if poasible,but if not, then for the purpose Of general culture. At the distrib ition of prizes to the students of the National Art Training Sehool, London, last week, Mr. Luke Fildes, ft A., said" it should be made compulsory for every child in the kingdom to be taught to draw for nothing tended more to cultivate the power of observa- tion." They had again and again referred to the import- ance of this subject and the necessity for an acquaint- ance with the elementary principles of drawing was daily becoming mo a essential to those who wished to be experienced craftsmen and make their power felt not only in the workshop, but in the world. (Ayplause.) At the conclusion of the reading of the report the Chairman said he thought they would all agree with him in saying that the report which they had just heard read was an excellent one. It must have taken the as cretary some time to prepare it, and he believed he had touched upon everything connected with the Insti- tute. There were one or two items at the end of the report which he should have been glad if they could have left out,but it was put in in order that it might be known throughout the works that there were not as many youne men taking an interest in the Science and Art Classes as could be wished. They were stand- ing in their own light. He (the Chairman) was getting on in years, and he knew what it was from ex- perience to he able to draw. He had not those advan- tages when he was a young man, which they had there, but he embraced them later on,and if he had not learned to draw he would not have been standing there that day and he would urge young men who did not attend the classes, to do so, and thus better their position. If he wanted a foreman, he should look round the shop and select a young man who could draw. He might be able to make a wagon but unless he could sketch out some- thing like what he wanted a man to do, he was not the man in the right place. Therefore he wanted men who ' could control, point out and show men what he really wanted. They might be able to tell a man what they wanted, but if they showed him with either pencil or chalk, so much the better. He thought he had said enoughon that subject to try and get young men to join the classes. The other day he happened to get hold of a newspaper, and saw in it a report of what Earl Spencer had said when distributing prizes. He said "he should consider it perfectly right to have Foreign language taught in primary schools,for England would find, in its keen competition with other countries, immense ad- vantages in members of the working classes being able to understand and speak a Foreign language." He (Mr. Emmett) had been in various countries, and feund it a great disadvantage in not being able to speak the language of the country he had been in. If he had been able to do so he could have learnt a great deal more than he kmew at the present time. Earl Spencer was of opinion that unless this great nation of ours cultivated education more than it had done, we should certainly come behind. He had said there were 45 millions of people in Germany,and out of that number 25,000 were in various educational institutions. We had a popula- tion of 30 millions and out of that number there were only 5,500 attending our colleges, showing that the Ger- mans were far ahead of us in percentage in that respect. We wanted to educate ourselves better than we were doing at the present time; unless we did that he thought we should have to play second fiddle some time to other nations. There was no increase in the number of readers at the library, or at any rate very little. When the nine hours' movement was started, a petition was sent to hirn,and in it it was stated that if the nine hours wore granted what an advantage it would be to the men by giving time and opportunity to educate themselves, but hp would point out that they had not fulfilled their pro wese of those days,and he hoped they would see to it for the future. (Applause.) The balance sheet and report was then taken as read, and adopteo. The following were elected on the committee of man- agement, Messrs. W. H. Bailey (753), Joseph Emmett (733), T. Ormand (768), John Barr (708), D. Barcroft (691), William Crawford (612), George Gaff (415), Messrs. W. Stephen and H. H. Anderton,were re-elected aa up dp oi tionrtse. d sNelreAs irns e. eJr as mf oers tGheee naenxdt We e. Ct loanr .e , jun., were Mr. J. Waddington said there was not a sufficient per centage of young men under 21 Years of age, and asked if it would not be an incentive, if, when the foremen ea- sgua bg ;ode tas I would do uatldthbee bweosrtkfso,rtnhienly twootualkdeinl.)rmvvohnimld iwt not be better for the authorities to look after the candidates and not for the candidates to go to them. The Chairman said the danger was if the foreman be- gan to suggest to an apprentice lad what he should do, or should not do, the parents might step in and say they did not thank the foreman for telling his lad whet to do. The father was the proper person to encourage his son to attend the classes. The suggestion was very well on the face of it, but the only fear was that the boy might think it was a litVe coercion. Votes of thanks to the Chairman, terminated the meeting.</p>
        <p>Buda-Pesth is at all times one of the most interesting of continental cities, but this year it is also one of the liveliest. The Hungarian Milennial Exhibition has already been visited by over 2,000,000 people, and the residents are, from all accounts. devoting all tlaeir time to entertaining the foreign visitors. Although the Ex- hibition must, of course, be adding considerably to the revenue of the town, yet the people seem to be working for glory rather thin money, and they are determined that no one shall leave the town without carrying away with him a pleasant impression of Hungarian hospita- , 'ity. Everybody is invited to make himself at home at all the public institutions, and p irties of tourists are •,specially welcomed. Learned societies and similar or- ganisations are journeying from all parts of Europe to this aocient and picturesque city, which abounds in his- torical associations. The Exhibition itself is a remark able show in many ways.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY AUGUST 1, 1996,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY AUGUST 1, 1996,</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY A.T EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDE NT.</p>
        <p>The shocking swing-boat accident, which hap- pened on the market ground on Saturday evening and by which a young fellow only recently married lost his life, should operate as a serious warning to many others who behave in a reckless manner when apparently enjoying themselves in the boats. This young man, according to the evidence given at the inquest, was standing up in the boat and he evi- dently overbaland himself and fell out. The practice of staniing up is becoming much too com- mon, and very severe measures should be taken to put a stop to it. I have no doubt the proprietors of the swings are only too anxious to prevent it,but of what use are their warnings when men will per- sist in risking their necks.</p>
        <p>This accident prompts me to refer once more to the provision of a public mortuary for the reception of the dead bodies of strangers. The publicans of Earlestown have to all appearance taken it into their heads to follow out the law on the point, as laid down by Coroner Brighouse, that they are not bound to receive a dead body, and the police ex- perience much difficulty in finding a resting place for such stramgers as are so unfortunate as to come to an untimely end in this neighbourhood. The question of a mortuary has been brought up on several occasions, and although the members of the District Council are fully aware of the difficulties with which the police are beset, they do not make any effort to remove them. if the police would opily go a little further with the law, and deposit the next stranger's body which comes under fheir care on the doorstep of the churchwarden of the parish in which it is found, I think the mortuary question would quickly find a place in the forefront of the business.</p>
        <p>As usual the races this year brought into the town a large number of .undesirable visitors, but, doubtless owing to the rain, the attendance was not so great as in former years. It is evident however that we are not tq be troubled in this way in tha future. For some time it has been persistently rumoured that this is the last occasion on which the races will be held at Newton Common, and it now appears as though the rumour is correct. There will, it is said, be no more racing at Newton, and there is a proposition on foot to transfer the venue to Haydock Park, and to hold two or three meetings yearly. Newton Common is to be let—so it is said —for the erection of a large works, and cottages, and if that be so we may in the near future see the growing of a small town in the vicinity of the Com- mon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>...-......_ AFTER THE RACES.</head>
        <p>...-......_ AFTER THE RACES.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. J. Gillespie sat at the Earlestown Police Station on Friday evening to decide upon any cases brought be- fore him as the result of the races at Newton. The fol- lowing were all fined 203. including costs :—Edward Jackson, of Leeds, for gaming with cards; Thomas Do wns,of Earlestown, for gaming with box and dice; Mary Fearns,of Bolton, for gaming with a turn table ; Joseph Cowle,for gaming with box and numbers ; Geo. Adams, Manchester, and George Gaysford, Sheffield, for gaming with box and numbers; and Thomas Deakin (Manchester) for gaming with box and dice.—Patrick Cuncannon tor being drunk in Juno last was fined 53. and costs.—Thomas Errineton, of Newcastle, for being drunk was fined 2s. 63. and costs. SATURDAY. The police had aeain a good haul of delinquents from the race course, who were arraigned before Mr. C. B. F. Borron at the Eariestown police station on Saturday evening. The first case called was that of Ralph Pen- nington, of Haydock, who was charged with being drunk and disorderly at Golborne on the 12th inst. He appeared at Newton Town Hall that morning, but was too drunk to plead and he was put back to the cells to sleep off the effects of his potations. He was fined 2s. 6d. and costs or 7 days.—John Mackey charged with play- ing a game of chance with certain instruments known as sticks on Newton race course, pleaded guilty to play- ing a game of skill. The constable described how the game was played and said the, the defendant gave his custome s threepence every time they knocked the button off the stick and it fell on the clay. The defend- ant said it was just the reverse, if the button fell on the clay, he won, but if it fell on the ground, he lost three- pence, and contended it was a game of skill. Mr. Borron decided it was a game of chance, and inflicted a fine of 10s. and costs, or 14 days.—Thomas Leigh for a similar offence was fined 203. including costs, although he protested it was a game of skill, and offered to show the magistrate how it was played.—Alfred Lomas,a bookmaker's clerk was charged with stealing 43., the money of Thomas Stokes, 103. from Charles Sarsfield, and 503. from James Herbert. The prosecutors alleged that they made bets with the prisoner, but were unable to obtain their winnings. It transpired in the evidence that the prisoner did not handle the money but simply booked the bets for his principal who had got clean" away. The inagistrates gave the prisoner the lac nefit of the doubt and discharged him with a caution.—Annie McKay, who was charged with playtnga game of chance with box and dice, glibly told the magistrate that she was not playing but was going to do if she got the chance, and she would only plead guilty to intention. A constable said he watched her play for about ten minutes and she was fined 20i. including costs.—Joseph Ashcroft, when charged with playing with pins and ball, said it was a game of skill and supported his contention by showing the magis- trates how both pins could be knocked down at once. He was, however, fined 203. iucluding costs.—Edward Murphy for playing pitch and toss with coins was fined 53. and costs.—John Nicholson and Mary Burns were charged with playing with a turn table and horses. Both defenJants declared they were absolute strangers to each other, and had never seen each other before that day, although a c.instable stated he saw them together the previous day. Nicholson for assisting the woman was fined 53. and costs, and the woman Burns also was fined a similar amount.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>WARRINGTON BANK RUPTCY COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BANK RUPTCY COURT.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. Registrar Ridgway. A SWINDLER'S BANKRUPTCY—With regard to the bankruptcy of John Garlick alias Grant, the Assistant- Official Receiver said according to instructions he visited Walton Gaol and saw the prisoner Grant there. The debtor raised many objections to the proofs, and it was impossible to get him to confess to anything. Mr. Lowe had also been to see the prisoner.but he had been unable to complete the statement. He would ask for an ad- journment until the 18th September, and Mr. Jokneon, his deputy, would make an application at the Cocart on August sth for an order to bring the bankrupt up. RE WILLIAM BOSTOCIC.—In this case the assistant official receiver (Mr. Potter) stated that the debtor scheduled surpluses from sezured creditors value £3,305, and unsecured,£2oo. Mr. Bostock had spoken to him of paying 203. in the £.—Mr. Browne, solicitor, submitted that his client(Mr.Bostock)would carry out what he had promised, and hoped to pay the costs. Mr. Bostock had seep the mortgagees and they were perfectly satisfied to go on.—The Registrar said if there was a chance of the thing being carried through he thought an adjournment Was the proper thing.--Mr. Browne: The creditors will be paie off.—An adjournment ot the examination until the 181 h September was made. A LYarat BANKRUPT.—Joseph Roberts, Sunny Bray- Rush Green, Lymm, an engineer's draughtsman, ap- peared to undergo his public examination in bankruptcy. The debtor's deficiency was £298 Bs, 61, the liabilities being £317 123. and the assets £l9 3s. 6d. The bank, rupt stated that he had never been in business on his own accaunt.Flis deficiency was caused by the excess of his expenditure over his income, owing to the ill-health of his wife and family during the last seven years. He became aware of his insolvency about four years ago ; and during that period, and with such knowledge, con- tracted debts, which were still owing, but in the hope that his expenditure would decrease as his wife's and family's health improved. The liabilities were almost wholly for money lent. Wit t 3 regard to his assets the bankrupt stated that his father, William Roberts, died intestate 17 years ago. His stepmother took out letters of administration, and retained all the property, though he was entitled to two-thirds of the estate, which am- ounted to about £l,OOO. his stepmother lived in Sydney, Australia. The security in the hands of the creditor fully secured was a policy for £250, effected on the bankrupt's life about nine years ago,with the Prodential Assuranee Company, at an annual premium of £72s. 6d. payable half-yearly. Hu was married twelve years ago, and he accounted for his failure by his wife's ill•health, and that of his son. He had been advised to live in the country on account of his wife. His salary had been nearly £4 a week. He did not know the rate of interest he had paid on „the loans. It would perhaps have been better if he had.—The ex ,mination was closed.</p>
        <p>Mns PoNSONBY'S HAIR RE9TOREic, a great discovery, a Most Refreshin4 Wash, Marvellous Results, Innumetable Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. In great demand by Professionals. makes the Hair Grow, prevents Bald- ness, Invigorating, restores hair to its natural colour, gives healthy and youthful appearance. Most astonish- ing results ; 2s 61 Bottle. pos: free.—Address Mts. Pox- soNnr, 25, Lime-st., Liverpool—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Northampton county constabulary have for the past six months been permitted in the rural districts to use their own bicyles when Gn duty in certain instances, the police authorities ing to the constable twopence per mile for the bicycle when thus used, The experi- ment has been so far successful that the Standing Joint Committee have decided to sanction the arrangement for another year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the Howe of Commons on Monday Mr. HAsenuir replying to Mr. H. Heaton, said the Postmaster General had been considering for some time whether it would be practicable to arrange that the sender of a parcel of dutiable goods frorr this cc untry to a place abroad sbould be allowed • o prepay the duty, so that the parcel might be delivered to the addresses free. Mr. MORLEY questioned Mr. Curzon and Mr. Cham- berlain in regard to the Venezuelan boundary dispute. The Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs replied that the statement of the Venezuelan c4se had not yet come into the possession of Her Majesty's Government. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said he had no information which would enable him at present to state the limits and conditions of settlements in British Guiana.</p>
        <p>Answering Sir E. Bartlett, Mr. CHAMBERNAIN said he had received no request up to that time from Sir F. Carrington for military reinforcements for Rhodesia. Sir C. DUKE asked a series of questions with regard to the Niger Company's administration, and also with respect to France and Madagascar. Mr. CURZON in- formed him that no final decision bad been taken yet by the French Government in regard to concessions in Madagascar. On the second reading of the Uganda Railway Bill,Mr. Labouchere moved an amendment declaring that no further funds should be voted for making the railway until more clear and definite estimates of its cost had been present-cd. On a division 88 voted for the amend- ment and 239 against, the Government majority thus being 153. The bill was then read a second time. On the report of the Conoiliation (Trade Disputes) Bill as amended by the Standing Committee, Mr. Ashcroft withdrew an important amendment relating to the sub- sidising'ef workpeople on strike. After further debate the bill was read a third timetand the House proceeded to the consideration of the Coal Mines Regulation Act Amendment Bill. Mr. Abraham moved an amendment to omit the provision that secuiity should be given for costs before the workmen could go into arbitration proceedings. The amendment vi as rejected by 150 votes to 87. In pursuance of a compromise suggested by the Government. the Attorney General then proposed that workmen should be liable only for the costs occasioned by their representation before the arbitration court. The amendment was agreed to, and, later, the Bill was passed. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>TUESDAY,</head>
        <p>TUESDAY,</p>
        <p>In addition to passing the second reading of the Uganda Railway Bill and the third reading of the Con- ciliation (Trade Disputes) Bill, at Monday night's sitting —which was protracted until 2.15 am.—the House of Commons passed the third reading of the Coal Mines Regulation Bill and made some progress with the Truck Bill. At Tuesday's sitting of the House, Mr. Chamber- lain, replying to Sir E. A. Bartlett, made a statement in reference to the necessity or otherwiAe of more troops in Rhodesia. From this it appeared that Lieutenant Goodenough telegraphed on Monday that Sir F. Carring- ton reported that he was no doubt hampered by the want of more men for the rapid and effectual crushing of the rebellion, but if more were sent he could not feed them under existing difficulties as to transport.—The Colonial Secretary also informed Sir E. A. B trtlett that Sir Jacobus de Wet was net entitled to a pension, but he had ben offered £3OO a year "as an exceptional act of grace and favour." WEDNESDAY.—TORY DISUNION.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons had another all-night sitting on Tuesday, the report stage of the Irish Land Bill not being disposed of until nearly half-past five on Wednes- day morning. At noon the House met again to take the third reading of the bill. Mr. Smith-Barry, as a repre- sentative of the Irish landlords, moved the rejection of the bill, stating that he and his friends considered a small and simple measure facilitating the working of the previous Acts would have snffice I. This bill, how- ever, went a great deal further than was necessary, and infringed the remaining rights of the landlords. Colonel Saunderson, who seconded the motion, taunted the Government upon the withdrawal of their amendments, not upon principle, but simply because of the opposition that was evoked. He was both surprised and sorry, he said, that a Government which he and his friends had done so much to help into office should have actad in a manner so hostile to them, and should have given a parting kick to "the English garrison." In the debate Which followed Mr A. J. BALFOUR, reply- ing to landlord criticisms, said it might have been sup- posed that the authors of the bill were a set of revolu- tionists, with little or no regard for property. When he listened to such criticism he aske4 himself whether it was he or his critics who were mad. The bill was read a third time, without a division. The Finance Bill was also read a third time, after some of its provisions had been criticised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>TEIURSDIY.</head>
        <p>TEIURSDIY.</p>
        <p>-- • In the House of Commons on Thursday Mr. Curzon nromised Mr. Kenyon that he would obtain information from France, Germany, Switzerland, Austria, Russia, and Japan as to the rates of wages paid and the heurs worked in the cotton, woollen, and iron trades in those countries. Mr. BATLEY asked if the Colonial Secretary or the Under Secretary for War would make a statement as to how the Government proposed to deal with the officers in the British army who took part in the raid into the Tranava,al. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN referred the hon.member to Mr. Brodrick. and Mr. BRODRICIC declined to give an answer at the present time. Mr. P. O'BRIEN asked that Dr. Jamesan and his fellow prisoners should he treated as first-class misdemean- ants, and Mr. Ungan that they should be regarded as political prisoners. The Home S^cretary answered that the Came, had not exercised its power to order that the prisoners should be treated as first-class misdemeanants and he had no power to alter its definite directions. The prisoners were being dealt with in the ordinary way re- quired by law, and he was not at present prepared to say whether the case was one in which the Royal prero- gative should be exercised. Mr. T. M. HETALy asking whether the prisoners were to be treated worse than they were under President Kruger, the Home Secretary said he did not know how President Kruger treated them. The ATTORNEY GENERAL declined to state, in answer to Sir M. Reed, whether or no the Government intended to bring to justice persons who had prepared or fur- thered the raid into the Transvaal,without risking their own lives.</p>
        <p>Mr. RITCHIE introduced a bill to legalise the use of the weights and measures of the metric system. Efe stated, however, that he only did this in oider to put the Gov- ernment views before the Chambers of Commerce so as to elicit the opinions of those bodies before the question was taken up seriously in another session. The bill was formally read a first time. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN then moved that a Select Committee be appointed to inquire into the administration of the British South Africa Company, and to report what alter- ations were desirable in the government of the territories under the control of the Company. The leader of the Opposition had given notice of an amendment to make it clear that an inquiry into the origin of the raid into the Transvaal was contemplated. There could be no possible objection to that, and the amendment would be accepted. After some debate the resolution,as amended was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND THE MEDITERRANEAN. A corresponden.t of the Mariche;ter "Guardian " writes :—lf there is one line of steamers running to Manchester which more than another deserves success, and all the assistance towards that end that Manchester can give, it is Knott's Prince line, whose directors from the opening of the Ship Canal have persistently worked up and now maintain a spend. id service of steamers with the Mediterranean. rhus service has been es- tablished in the face of the strongest and most insidious opposition from steamship owners it is possible to imagine,. One is therefore all the more glad to learn that the Prince line have succeeded in establishing the position they set themselves to make, and that they are about to extend their operations by a new development of an especially interosting character, by putting on the Manchester service an entirely different set of steamers. The new vevels all have high-class passenger acconsmo- dation. Commencing in September next, they will run regularly between Manchester and Egypt and the Holy Land. The vessels will be both larger and faster than those hitherto employed, and the importers of cotton will be interested to know that the time occupied in the passage from Alexandria will be considerably less than was the case last season. The latest addition to this fleet is the Trojan Prince, 365 feet long, 5,0)0 tons cargo capacity, now about to make her maiden voyage to the Argentine Republic. She is fitted with every modern ap- pliance both for the disposition of cargo and the com- fort of passengers.including hot-water circuits for warm- ing the ship in cold weather and a perfect system of fans for hot climates,which can be kept in motion at night and the positions altered so as to change the ditection of I the current of air.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>A LOCAL AIITROR.—Many of our readers will know Mrs. Blanche Wray as the wife of the Rev. G. D. Wray, but perhaps few will know her as the authot of "A Shadow of a Sin" now appearing in a recently-started ladies' periodical, issued from the capital. The opening chapters of the story are distinctly above the average, and tend to point to the fact that in literature Mrs. Wray has secured a congenial field of work. It may be said that our local author is the only daughter of the Rev. S. Wilkinson, one time vicar of St. Paul's,Warring- ton, and now rector of Timperley. GRAMMAR SCHOOL.—Tbe report of H.M. Inspector just received is very favourable, and shows that the highest grant has been obtained in all subjects. The school has i 4 e n excused examination for the year ending June, 1897.</p>
        <p>In the Queen's Bench Division on Wednesdav,an action was brought by a London pianoforte manufacturer and his foreman attainst a number of trade union officials to reeover damages for loss sustained by reason of a system of picketing set up at plaintiff's works on the occa,ion of a trade dispute. The plaintiffs were awarded £2OO and £2O respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The " Times " correspondent at Berlin says that in de- fault of a new anti-Socialist law, the German Govern- ment avails itself of every possible opportunity of com- bating Socialist propaganda. Particularly is this the case in the army and navy, which for some years past have been the special ebjects of Socialist solicitude. Shopkeepers guilty of furnishing soldiers with wares wrapped in Socialist newspapers have frequently been sentenced to long terms of imprisonment, while few military punishments are severer than those meted out to soldiers convicted of smuggling revolutionary prints into their barracks. Nevertheless, the anxiety of the military authoritiee would seem to be rather increasing than decreasing. The "Imperial Gazette" publishes a regulation drawn up by the Minister of War fc rbidding soldiers and non-commissioned officers to take part in any meeting or festival or to contribute to any subscrip- tion without special permi,sion from their superior officers. Soldiers are further commanded to report all instances of S-icialist newspapers or writings having been introduced into barracks, and are ordered to abstain from uttering revolutionary cries or singing Socialist songs. This ordinance, which applies to the reserve as well as to the regular army, excites the admiration of the Conservative press. The "Post," indeed, sighs for tha decree to be made applicable to Universities and other institutions at present distinguishable from bar- racks, and quite overlooks the consideration that previ- ous mileary regulations of the same kind have proved powerless to check the spread of Socialism. Needless to say, the equanimity of the " Vnrwarts " is undisturbed. Its only comment is the characteristic remark that by this time the Minister of War ought to know better than to think he can check by such inadequate means the progress of Socialism.</p>
        <p>The " Daily News " publishes an amusing story with reference to Mr. Gladstone and Civil List pensions. Some years ago Mr. Gladstone had met a possible claimant for a Civil List pensicn whom he believed to be in suffi- ciently poor circumstances, and had almost decided to grant it, when he received an invitation to dinner with the person in question. This raised some doubt in his mind. On the one band, should a Civil List pensioner be able to afford to entertain. On the other hand, it might be only a dinner of herbs, and it seemed hard to deprive a publie benefactor ot a pension because he was ready to share his crust and water. Knowing that in any case there would be a feast of reason and a flow of soul, Mr. Gladstone accepted the invitation, and on the way propounded to his companion the following test : "No champagne, pension ; champagne, no pension." There was champagne, and the host lost his pension. It was the dearest bottle of wine on record, for it cost the purchaser £lOO a year.</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN AND AN IRISH CENTENARTAN.—Mrs. Margaret. Hobbs (widow of Captain Thomas Hobbs, who was wounded at Quatre Bras on the day before the battle of Waterloo), who completed the 102nd year of her age on the MAI inst., has received from the Queen's private seceetary the following congratulatory message : —" Windsor Castle, July 16, 1896. Madam,-1 have had the honour to lay before the Queen a letter received from your grandson Major Herbert Hobbs, in which he informs Her I‘l4j-sty of the touching fact that on the 18th of the present month you attain the great age of 102 years, and also gives gratify,ing details (lithe service rendered by the late Captain Hobbs and ten other mem- bers of your family. lam commanded to convey to you the sincere congratulations of the Qleeu on the occasion of the 102nd anniversary of your birthday, and to assure you of Her Maj -sty's true appreciation of the valivd services rendered by your descendants to Queen and country, and to express Her Majesty's earnest hope that the military instinct which has Nen so marked in the past and present may be equally developed in the future history ef yoisr fainiiy.—Believe me, madam, yours very faithfully,—" ARTHUR BIGGE. "To Mrs. Hobbs, Barnaboy House, Franktord, Queen's County."</p>
        <p>The judgment given on Monday at the close of the inquiry into the loss of the Drummond Castle was pain- fully but,we think,inevitably unsparing. The Court did not mince its opinion that the vessel had not been navi- gated with proner and seamanlike care. The weather was thick and the coast dangerous, yet the vessel was kept going almost at full speed. The soundings, which might have been taken without the smallest loss of time were neglected, and a serious miscalculation of the easterly current—the strength of which is a matter of common observation to the most unseamanlike of voyagers in th.sse parts—was thus allowed to go uncor- rected. In the light of these facts—which, indeed,have been patent from the first—no further explanation is needed. Doubtless the nasty corner is often taken too sharply to be consistent with perfect safety ; but even so, with ordinary care no accident need have happened. The French coasting packet to Ushant always comes to a dead stop when the mist descends, yet the Drummond Castle neither slackened speed nor used the lead. The whole affair is a melancholy lapse from seamanship—a solitary lapse, it should be added, in the annals of the line, and punished with tragical promptitude by a ter- rible disaster.</p>
        <p>An interesting presentation has just been made to Mr. Robert Crockett. uncle of the author of "The Raiders," on his retirement, after twenty-seven years' servive as porter at the railway station at Castle Douglas. The old gentleman, who is a great devourer of books, is natur- ally very proud of the successful career of his nephew, for whose college cr.urse in Edinburgh he managed to save enough out of his modest wages. There were many subscribers to the presentation, which took the form of a gold watch and chain.</p>
        <p>How far English Consuls abroad can assist in develop- ing English trade is a question of which we are nearer the solution now than when it was first raised, ten years ago. Actively to interfere in the interests of Bri- tish trade in general or one trade in particular would be to endanger a consul's diplomatic position. What he can do is to supply early and sound information on the state of trade and labour in his district. Laudable at- tempts have lately been made in this direction, although with a few exceptions, the success has not been very conspicuous. Still reports like Mr. Rennell Rodd's from Egypt or Mr. O'Neill's from Rouen have shown what can be done. According to an important statement by Mr. Curzon on Monday night. Europe is to be mapped out into four districts, each under the control of a com- mercial attaché, whose duty it will be to forward system- atic reports from his district. Apparently it is intended to map out Asia in similar manner, and consuls are to he sent out to recently opened ports in China and Japan. The idea is excellent,and if the right men are appointed to the new pests they ought to prove of great service to merchants at home.</p>
        <p>A company of glass workers have recently discovered that ordinary plate glass will make a more durable monument than the hardest marble or granite known to stone masons. Glass is practically indestructible. Wind, rain, heat and cold have their effect on the hard- est rock; solid granite eventually crumbles away, and one can seldom read the inscription on a stone grave- stone fifty years old. A glass stone will look as fresh a couple of centuries after its manufacture as en the day it was erected, and the inscription can be made inefface- able. The thick plate glass used to glaze the portholes of steamers can resist the stormiest sea, and is practically unbreakable.</p>
        <p>Milk may be bought by the brick.in sunimer,just the same as some kinds of icecream. It will be frozen solid, though,and if intended for use as soon as received lathe household the lacteal fluid for the tea or coffee will have to be chipped off with the handle of the knife er fork, according to the quantity desired in the drink. From a fad, frozen milk has grown to he more or less of a neces- sity in the warmer countries of Europe. The Belgian Government designs to increase the trade at an annual outlay of £IO,QOO, and in Copenhagen, the capital of Denmark,a company has been formed and arrangements have been completed for the regular export of frozen milk. The neces-ary plant has been erected, and con- traets have been made already for the delivery of 110,0001hr:1. per week, which will be sent to all parts of the world in bricks or blocks like ice.</p>
        <p>An interesting discovery is reported by a party of land surveyors w!ao have been making surveys in the barren arid region of South Dakota known as Bad Lands. They have observed there numerous dunes, which ap- parently change their location and outline with every movement of the wind ; and they tell also of a hill there that is constantly vibrating, and within or beneath which are heard continuous subterrnean noises. The discovery is attracting the attention of geologists and other scientific men.</p>
        <p>American Democratic organs touch high keys of enthu-ia.m in their appreciations of Mr. W. J. Bryan, the Party candidate for the Presidency. This is a ypical one : " In his personal character Mr. Bryan has all that can be asked in a candidate before the American people. He is clean and reputable. His life is irreproach- able, his domestic relations charming. He won his way by his own efforts and his own talent..Ho has been honest and sincere in his public life. He is one of those champions of the people who have been true to the people in office as well as in campaigns, as he saw and taught the welfare of the people to be. Presumably there will be little personal politics in this campaign. It will be fought out on the issues presented by the two great I parties. In this respect at least the country is to be I congratulated."</p>
        <p>, can strike a Frank Ives, the great billiard player billiard ball with a cue harder than any other living man. Ives thinks that his peculiar ability is due to some unusual construction of his arm, and he has just made a will in which he directs that at his death his right arm should be severed from his body and sent to his physician for dissection. Ives, by the way, has saved £60,000 during his career.</p>
        <p>At the sitting of the Commission on the Licensing Laws on Tuesday the Clerk to the Justices of Newcastle- on Tyne admitted the" bad eminence" of that city in regard to e,onvictions for drunkenness, but disclaimed for it the discredit of being the " most drunken place in the kingdom."</p>
      </div>
